const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. Before the Capitol","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Tribunes and Senators aloft. And then enter Saturninus and his followers at one door, and Bassianus and his followers at the other, with drums and trumpets.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Noble patricians, patrons of my right,","Defend the justice of my cause with arms;","And, countrymen, my loving followers,","Plead my successive title with your swords.","I am his firstborn son that was the last","That wore the imperial diadem of Rome;","Then let my father's honours live in me,","Nor wrong mine age with this indignity."],"id":2,"m":"Noble lords, protectors of my rights, defend the justice of my claim with weapons. And you, my countrymen and loyal followers, argue my right of succession with your swords. I am the eldest son of the last man who wore the imperial crown of Rome. So let my father's honours live on in me, and do not insult my seniority with this outrage."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Romans, friends, followers, favourers of my right,","If ever Bassianus, Caesar's son,","Were gracious in the eyes of royal Rome,","Keep then this passage to the Capitol,","And suffer not dishonour to approach","The imperial seat, to virtue consecrate,","To justice, continence, and nobility;","But let desert in pure election shine,","And, Romans, fight for freedom in your choice."],"id":3,"m":"Romans, friends, followers and supporters of my cause: if Bassianus, Caesar's son, was ever well thought of by royal Rome, then guard this way to the Capitol. Do not let dishonour come near the emperor's throne, which is dedicated to virtue, justice, self-control and nobility. Let merit shine out in a free election, and, Romans, fight for the freedom to choose."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Marcus Andronicus aloft, holding the crown.","id":4},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Princes, that strive by factions and by friends","Ambitiously for rule and empery,","Know that the people of Rome, for whom we stand","A special party, have by common voice,","In election for the Roman empery,","Chosen Andronicus, surnamed Pius","For many good and great deserts to Rome.","A nobler man, a braver warrior,","Lives not this day within the city walls.","He by the senate is accited home","From weary wars against the barbarous Goths,","That with his sons, a terror to our foes,","Hath yoked a nation strong, trained up in arms.","Ten years are spent since first he undertook","This cause of Rome, and chastised with arms","Our enemies' pride. Five times he hath returned","Bleeding to Rome, bearing his valiant sons","In coffins from the field.","And now at last, laden with honour's spoils,","Returns the good Andronicus to Rome,","Renowned Titus, flourishing in arms.","Let us entreat, by honour of his name","Whom worthily you would have now succeed,","And in the Capitol and senate's right,","Whom you pretend to honour and adore,","That you withdraw you and abate your strength,","Dismiss your followers, and, as suitors should,","Plead your deserts in peace and humbleness."],"id":5,"m":"Princes, you who are competing for power through parties and supporters: know that the people of Rome, whom we tribunes represent, have chosen by a common vote, in this election for emperor, Andronicus, surnamed Pius (the dutiful), for his many great services to Rome. There is no nobler man or braver warrior alive within the city walls today. The senate has called him home from tiring wars against the barbarous Goths, a strong and warlike nation that he and his sons, a terror to our enemies, have conquered. Ten years have passed since he took up Rome's cause and began punishing our enemies' pride. Five times he has come back to Rome bleeding, carrying his brave sons home from the battlefield in coffins. And now at last good Andronicus, famous Titus, returns to Rome loaded with the spoils of honour, at the height of his success in war. In the name of the man you would have succeed your father, and out of respect for the Capitol and the senate, which you claim to honour, we ask you to withdraw, reduce your forces, dismiss your followers, and argue your claims peacefully and humbly, as candidates should."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["How fair the tribune speaks to calm my thoughts!"],"id":6,"m":"How kindly the tribune speaks to calm me down!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Marcus Andronicus, so I do affy","In thy uprightness and integrity,","And so I love and honour thee and thine,","Thy noble brother Titus and his sons,","And her to whom my thoughts are humbled all,","Gracious Lavinia, Rome's rich ornament,","That I will here dismiss my loving friends,","And to my fortunes and the people's favour","Commit my cause in balance to be weighed."],"id":7,"m":"Marcus Andronicus, I trust so much in your honesty and integrity, and I love and honour you and your family so much, your noble brother Titus and his sons, and the one to whom all my thoughts bow down, gracious Lavinia, Rome's richest jewel, that I will dismiss my loyal friends here and leave my cause to fortune and to the people's favour, to be weighed in the balance."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt the followers of Bassianus.","id":8},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Friends, that have been thus forward in my right,","I thank you all and here dismiss you all,","And to the love and favour of my country","Commit myself, my person, and the cause."],"id":9,"m":"Friends, who have been so eager to support my claim, I thank you all and dismiss you all, and I trust myself, my person and my cause to the love and goodwill of my country."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt the followers of Saturninus.","id":10},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","cont":1,"lines":["Rome, be as just and gracious unto me","As I am confident and kind to thee.","Open the gates and let me in."],"id":11,"m":"Rome, be as fair and kind to me as I am trusting and kind to you. Open the gates and let me in."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Tribunes, and me, a poor competitor."],"id":12,"m":"Let me in too, tribunes, a humble rival."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. They go up into the Senate House.","id":13},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Captain.","id":14},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Romans, make way! The good Andronicus,","Patron of virtue, Rome's best champion,","Successful in the battles that he fights,","With honour and with fortune is returned","From where he circumscribed with his sword","And brought to yoke the enemies of Rome."],"id":15,"m":"Romans, make way! The good Andronicus, champion of virtue and Rome's greatest defender, victorious in every battle he fights, has returned with honour and good fortune from the lands where his sword has cut down the enemies of Rome and forced them under the yoke."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound drums and trumpets, and then enter two of Titus' sons, and then two men bearing a coffin covered with black; then two other sons; then Titus Andronicus; and then Tamora, the Queen of Goths and her sons Alarbus, Chiron and Demetrius with Aaron the Moor, and others as many as can be, then set down the coffin, and Titus speaks.","id":16},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Hail, Rome, victorious in thy mourning weeds!","Lo, as the bark that hath discharged her fraught","Returns with precious lading to the bay","From whence at first she weighed her anchorage,","Cometh Andronicus, bound with laurel boughs,","To resalute his country with his tears,","Tears of true joy for his return to Rome.","Thou great defender of this Capitol,","Stand gracious to the rites that we intend.","Romans, of five-and-twenty valiant sons,","Half of the number that King Priam had,","Behold the poor remains, alive and dead.","These that survive let Rome reward with love;","These that I bring unto their latest home,","With burial amongst their ancestors.","Here Goths have given me leave to sheathe my sword.","Titus, unkind, and careless of thine own,","Why suffer'st thou thy sons, unburied yet,","To hover on the dreadful shore of Styx?","Make way to lay them by their brethren."],"id":17,"m":"Hail, Rome, victorious even in your mourning clothes! Like a ship that has unloaded her cargo and returns with a precious load to the harbour where she first raised her anchor, Andronicus comes home, crowned with laurel, to greet his country again with tears, tears of real joy at his return to Rome. Jupiter, great protector of this Capitol, look kindly on the rites we are about to perform. Romans, of my twenty-five brave sons, half as many as King Priam had, look at what is left, the living and the dead. Let Rome reward those who survive with love. These, whom I bring to their final home, let Rome reward with burial among their ancestors. Here the Goths have let me put away my sword. Titus, you unkind father, careless of your own, why do you leave your sons unburied, hovering on the dreadful bank of the river Styx? Make way to lay them beside their brothers."},{"t":"sd","text":"They open the tomb.","id":18},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["There greet in silence, as the dead are wont,","And sleep in peace, slain in your country's wars.","O sacred receptacle of my joys,","Sweet cell of virtue and nobility,","How many sons hast thou of mine in store,","That thou wilt never render to me more?"],"id":19,"m":"Greet each other there in silence, as the dead do, and sleep in peace, killed in your country's wars. O sacred store of my joys, sweet cell of virtue and nobility, how many of my sons do you keep that you will never give back to me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Give us the proudest prisoner of the Goths,","That we may hew his limbs, and on a pile","Ad manes fratrum sacrifice his flesh","Before this earthy prison of their bones,","That so the shadows be not unappeased,","Nor we disturbed with prodigies on earth."],"id":20,"m":"Give us the proudest of the Gothic prisoners, so that we can hack off his limbs and sacrifice his flesh on a pyre to the spirits of our brothers, in front of this earthly prison of their bones. Then their ghosts will not go unsatisfied, and we will not be troubled by evil omens on earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["I give him you, the noblest that survives,","The eldest son of this distressed queen."],"id":21,"m":"I give you him, the noblest one alive: the eldest son of this grieving queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Stay, Roman brethren! Gracious conqueror,","Victorious Titus, rue the tears I shed,","A mother's tears in passion for her son.","And if thy sons were ever dear to thee,","O, think my son to be as dear to me.","Sufficeth not that we are brought to Rome,","To beautify thy triumphs and return","Captive to thee and to thy Roman yoke;","But must my sons be slaughtered in the streets","For valiant doings in their country's cause?","O, if to fight for king and commonweal","Were piety in thine, it is in these.","Andronicus, stain not thy tomb with blood.","Wilt thou draw near the nature of the gods?","Draw near them then in being merciful.","Sweet mercy is nobility's true badge.","Thrice-noble Titus, spare my first-born son."],"id":22,"m":"Wait, Roman brothers! Gracious conqueror, victorious Titus, pity the tears I shed, a mother's tears of grief for her son. If your sons were ever dear to you, then think that my son is just as dear to me. Is it not enough that we are brought to Rome to decorate your triumph, captives under your Roman yoke? Must my sons be slaughtered in the streets as well, for fighting bravely for their country? If fighting for king and country was a sacred duty for your sons, it was the same for mine. Andronicus, do not stain your tomb with blood. Do you want to come close to the nature of the gods? Then come close to them by being merciful. Sweet mercy is the true badge of nobility. Thrice-noble Titus, spare my first-born son."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Patient yourself, madam, and pardon me.","These are their brethren whom your Goths beheld","Alive and dead, and for their brethren slain","Religiously they ask a sacrifice.","To this your son is marked, and die he must,","T' appease their groaning shadows that are gone."],"id":23,"m":"Be patient, madam, and forgive me. These are the brothers of the men your Goths saw alive and dead, and for their brothers who were killed they ask for a sacrifice, as religion demands. Your son is marked out for it, and he must die, to satisfy the groaning ghosts of the dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Away with him, and make a fire straight,","And with our swords, upon a pile of wood,","Let's hew his limbs till they be clean consumed."],"id":24,"m":"Take him away, and build a fire at once, and let us hack his limbs with our swords on a pile of wood until they are completely burned."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Titus' sons with Alarbus.","id":25},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["O cruel, irreligious piety!"],"id":26,"m":"O cruel, ungodly piety!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Was never Scythia half so barbarous!"],"id":27,"m":"Even Scythia was never half so barbaric!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Oppose not Scythia to ambitious Rome.","Alarbus goes to rest, and we survive","To tremble under Titus' threat'ning look.","Then, madam, stand resolved, but hope withal","The self-same gods that armed the Queen of Troy","With opportunity of sharp revenge","Upon the Thracian tyrant in his tent","May favour Tamora, the queen of Goths,","(When Goths were Goths and Tamora was queen)","To quit the bloody wrongs upon her foes."],"id":28,"m":"Don't compare Scythia with ambitious Rome. Alarbus goes to his rest, and we live on to tremble under Titus's threatening look. So, madam, stand firm, but hope as well that the same gods who gave Hecuba, Queen of Troy, the chance of sharp revenge on the Thracian tyrant in his tent may favour Tamora, Queen of the Goths (when the Goths were still Goths and Tamora was queen), to repay her enemies for these bloody wrongs."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the sons of Andronicus again with bloody swords.","id":29},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["See, lord and father, how we have performed","Our Roman rites. Alarbus' limbs are lopped,","And entrails feed the sacrificing fire,","Whose smoke like incense doth perfume the sky.","Remaineth naught but to inter our brethren,","And with loud 'larums welcome them to Rome."],"id":30,"m":"See, my lord and father, how we have carried out our Roman rites. Alarbus's limbs have been cut off, and his entrails feed the sacrificial fire, whose smoke perfumes the sky like incense. All that is left is to bury our brothers and welcome them to Rome with loud trumpet calls."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Let it be so; and let Andronicus","Make this his latest farewell to their souls."],"id":31,"m":"Let it be so. And let Andronicus say his last farewell to their souls."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound trumpets, and lay the coffin in the tomb.","id":32},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["In peace and honour rest you here, my sons;","Rome's readiest champions, repose you here in rest,","Secure from worldly chances and mishaps.","Here lurks no treason, here no envy swells,","Here grow no damned drugs; here are no storms,","No noise, but silence and eternal sleep.","In peace and honour rest you here, my sons."],"id":33,"m":"Rest here in peace and honour, my sons. Rome's readiest champions, lie here at rest, safe from the chances and accidents of the world. No treason lurks here, no envy swells, no cursed poisons grow; there are no storms here, no noise, only silence and eternal sleep. Rest here in peace and honour, my sons."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lavinia.","id":34},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["In peace and honour live Lord Titus long;","My noble lord and father, live in fame.","Lo, at this tomb my tributary tears","I render for my brethren's obsequies;","And at thy feet I kneel, with tears of joy","Shed on this earth for thy return to Rome.","O, bless me here with thy victorious hand,","Whose fortunes Rome's best citizens applaud."],"id":35,"m":"May Lord Titus live long in peace and honour. My noble lord and father, live on in fame. Look, at this tomb I offer my tears as a tribute at my brothers' funeral. And I kneel at your feet with tears of joy, shed on this ground for your return to Rome. O, bless me here with your victorious hand, whose achievements Rome's best citizens applaud."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Kind Rome, that hast thus lovingly reserved","The cordial of mine age to glad my heart!","Lavinia, live; outlive thy father's days,","And fame's eternal date, for virtue's praise."],"id":36,"m":"Kind Rome, which has kept safe for me the comfort of my old age to cheer my heart! Lavinia, live. Outlive your father's days and the everlasting span of fame, through praise of your virtue."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Marcus Andronicus and Tribunes; re-enter Saturninus, Bassianus and others.","id":37},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Long live Lord Titus, my beloved brother,","Gracious triumpher in the eyes of Rome."],"id":38,"m":"Long live Lord Titus, my beloved brother, a gracious conqueror in the eyes of Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Thanks, gentle tribune, noble brother Marcus."],"id":39,"m":"Thank you, kind tribune, my noble brother Marcus."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["And welcome, nephews, from successful wars,","You that survive, and you that sleep in fame.","Fair lords, your fortunes are alike in all,","That in your country's service drew your swords;","But safer triumph is this funeral pomp","That hath aspired to Solon's happiness","And triumphs over chance in honour's bed.","Titus Andronicus, the people of Rome,","Whose friend in justice thou hast ever been,","Send thee by me, their tribune and their trust,","This palliament of white and spotless hue,","And name thee in election for the empire","With these our late-deceased emperor's sons.","Be candidatus then, and put it on,","And help to set a head on headless Rome."],"id":40,"m":"And welcome, nephews, home from successful wars: those of you who survive, and those who sleep in glory. Noble lords, your fortunes are equal in this, that you all drew your swords in your country's service. But this funeral procession is a safer triumph. It has reached the happiness Solon spoke of, which no one can call happy until death, and it triumphs over chance in the bed of honour. Titus Andronicus, the people of Rome, whose friend in justice you have always been, send you this white and spotless robe by me, their tribune and the one they trust. They name you as a candidate for emperor alongside these sons of our late emperor. Be a candidate, then, and put it on, and help to give headless Rome a head."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["A better head her glorious body fits","Than his that shakes for age and feebleness.","What, should I don this robe and trouble you?","Be chosen with proclamations today,","Tomorrow yield up rule, resign my life,","And set abroad new business for you all?","Rome, I have been thy soldier forty years,","And led my country's strength successfully,","And buried one and twenty valiant sons,","Knighted in field, slain manfully in arms,","In right and service of their noble country.","Give me a staff of honour for mine age,","But not a sceptre to control the world.","Upright he held it, lords, that held it last."],"id":41,"m":"A better head than mine suits her glorious body, a head that does not shake with age and weakness. What, should I put on this robe and trouble you? Be chosen with proclamations today, give up power and die tomorrow, and leave you all with fresh business? Rome, I have been your soldier for forty years. I have led my country's armies successfully, and I have buried twenty-one brave sons, knighted on the battlefield and killed bravely in arms, fighting for their noble country. Give me a staff of honour for my old age, but not a sceptre to rule the world. The man who held it last, lords, held it with honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Titus, thou shalt obtain and ask the empery."],"id":42,"m":"Titus, you only have to ask, and you will have the empire."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Proud and ambitious tribune, canst thou tell?"],"id":43,"m":"Proud, ambitious tribune, how can you know that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Patience, Prince Saturninus."],"id":44,"m":"Patience, Prince Saturninus."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Romans, do me right.","Patricians, draw your swords, and sheathe them not","Till Saturninus be Rome's emperor.","Andronicus, would thou were shipped to hell","Rather than rob me of the people's hearts!"],"id":45,"m":"Romans, give me justice. Lords, draw your swords and do not put them away until Saturninus is Rome's emperor. Andronicus, I wish you were shipped to hell rather than that you should steal the people's hearts from me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Proud Saturnine, interrupter of the good","That noble-minded Titus means to thee!"],"id":46,"m":"Proud Saturnine, you are getting in the way of the good that noble Titus means to do for you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Content thee, prince; I will restore to thee","The people's hearts, and wean them from themselves."],"id":47,"m":"Calm yourself, prince. I will give the people's hearts back to you, and wean them away from their own choice."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Andronicus, I do not flatter thee,","But honour thee, and will do till I die.","My faction if thou strengthen with thy friends,","I will most thankful be; and thanks to men","Of noble minds is honourable meed."],"id":48,"m":"Andronicus, I do not flatter you, but I honour you and will do until I die. If you strengthen my party with your friends, I will be very grateful, and to men of noble minds thanks is an honourable reward."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["People of Rome, and people's tribunes here,","I ask your voices and your suffrages.","Will you bestow them friendly on Andronicus?"],"id":49,"m":"People of Rome, and tribunes of the people here, I ask for your voices and your votes. Will you give them as friends to Andronicus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRIBUNES","lines":["To gratify the good Andronicus,","And gratulate his safe return to Rome,","The people will accept whom he admits."],"id":50,"m":"To please the good Andronicus and to celebrate his safe return to Rome, the people will accept whoever he approves."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Tribunes, I thank you; and this suit I make,","That you create your emperor's eldest son,","Lord Saturnine; whose virtues will, I hope,","Reflect on Rome as Titan's rays on earth,","And ripen justice in this commonweal.","Then, if you will elect by my advice,","Crown him, and say \"Long live our emperor!\""],"id":51,"m":"Tribunes, I thank you. And this is my request: that you make our late emperor's eldest son, Lord Saturnine, emperor. I hope his virtues will shine on Rome as the sun's rays shine on the earth, and ripen justice in this state. So, if you will elect on my advice, crown him and say 'Long live our emperor!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["With voices and applause of every sort,","Patricians and plebeians, we create","Lord Saturninus Rome's great emperor,","And say \"Long live our Emperor Saturnine!\""],"id":52,"m":"With the voices and applause of every rank, lords and common people, we make Lord Saturninus Rome's great emperor, and say 'Long live our Emperor Saturnine!'"},{"t":"sd","text":"A long flourish.","id":53},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Titus Andronicus, for thy favours done","To us in our election this day,","I give thee thanks in part of thy deserts,","And will with deeds requite thy gentleness.","And for an onset, Titus, to advance","Thy name and honourable family,","Lavinia will I make my empress,","Rome's royal mistress, mistress of my heart,","And in the sacred Pantheon her espouse.","Tell me, Andronicus, doth this motion please thee?"],"id":54,"m":"Titus Andronicus, for the favours you have done me in my election today, I thank you as part of what you deserve, and I will repay your kindness with deeds. And to begin with, Titus, to raise up your name and your honourable family, I will make Lavinia my empress, Rome's royal mistress and mistress of my heart, and marry her in the sacred Pantheon. Tell me, Andronicus, does this proposal please you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["It doth, my worthy lord, and in this match","I hold me highly honoured of your grace;","And here in sight of Rome, to Saturnine,","King and commander of our commonweal,","The wide world's emperor, do I consecrate","My sword, my chariot, and my prisoners;","Presents well worthy Rome's imperious lord.","Receive them then, the tribute that I owe,","Mine honour's ensigns humbled at thy feet."],"id":55,"m":"It does, my worthy lord, and I consider myself greatly honoured by your grace in this match. And here, in the sight of Rome, I dedicate to Saturnine, king and ruler of our state and emperor of the wide world, my sword, my chariot and my prisoners: gifts well worthy of Rome's commanding lord. Receive them, then, the tribute that I owe, the signs of my honour laid humbly at your feet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Thanks, noble Titus, father of my life.","How proud I am of thee and of thy gifts","Rome shall record, and when I do forget","The least of these unspeakable deserts,","Romans, forget your fealty to me."],"id":56,"m":"Thank you, noble Titus, father of my new life. Rome shall record how proud I am of you and your gifts, and when I forget the smallest of these immense services, Romans, forget your loyalty to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["[To Tamora] Now, madam, are you prisoner to an emperor;","To him that for your honour and your state","Will use you nobly and your followers."],"id":57,"m":"(To Tamora) Now, madam, you are the prisoner of an emperor, who, for the sake of your honour and your rank, will treat you and your followers nobly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["A goodly lady, trust me, of the hue","That I would choose, were I to choose anew.","Clear up, fair queen, that cloudy countenance.","Though chance of war hath wrought this change of cheer,","Thou com'st not to be made a scorn in Rome.","Princely shall be thy usage every way.","Rest on my word, and let not discontent","Daunt all your hopes. Madam, he comforts you","Can make you greater than the Queen of Goths.","Lavinia, you are not displeased with this?"],"id":58,"m":"A fine lady, believe me, with the very looks I would choose if I were choosing again. Brighten up, fair queen, and clear that cloudy face. Though the fortunes of war have brought about this change in your mood, you have not come to be mocked in Rome. You will be treated like a princess in every way. Trust my word, and do not let unhappiness crush all your hopes. Madam, the man who comforts you can make you greater than the Queen of the Goths. Lavinia, you are not offended by this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["Not I, my lord, sith true nobility","Warrants these words in princely courtesy."],"id":59,"m":"Not I, my lord, since true nobility guarantees that these words are only royal courtesy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Thanks, sweet Lavinia. Romans, let us go.","Ransomless here we set our prisoners free.","Proclaim our honours, lords, with trump and drum."],"id":60,"m":"Thank you, sweet Lavinia. Romans, let us go. We set our prisoners free here without ransom. Lords, proclaim our honours with trumpet and drum."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Saturninus and his Guards exit, with Drums and Trumpets. Tribunes and Senators exit aloft.","id":61},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Lord Titus, by your leave, this maid is mine."],"id":62,"m":"Lord Titus, with your permission, this girl is mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["How, sir? Are you in earnest then, my lord?"],"id":63,"m":"What, sir? Do you mean it, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Ay, noble Titus; and resolved withal","To do myself this reason and this right."],"id":64,"m":"Yes, noble Titus, and I am determined to claim what is reasonable and right for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Suum cuique is our Roman justice.","This prince in justice seizeth but his own."],"id":65,"m":"'To each his own' is our Roman justice. This prince is only taking what is rightfully his."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["And that he will and shall, if Lucius live."],"id":66,"m":"And he will, and shall, as long as Lucius is alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Traitors, avaunt! Where is the emperor's guard?"],"id":67,"m":"Traitors, get back! Where is the emperor's guard?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Saturninus and his Guards.","id":68},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["Treason, my lord, Lavinia is surprised."],"id":69,"m":"Treason, my lord! Lavinia has been seized."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Surprised? By whom?"],"id":70,"m":"Seized? By whom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["By him that justly may","Bear his betrothed from all the world away."],"id":71,"m":"By the man who has every right to carry off his promised bride, away from all the world."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bassianus and Marcus with Lavinia.","id":72},{"t":"sp","who":"MUTIUS","lines":["Brothers, help to convey her hence away,","And with my sword I'll keep this door safe."],"id":73,"m":"Brothers, help to get her away from here, and I'll guard this door with my sword."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lucius, Quintus and Martius.","id":74},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Follow, my lord, and I'll soon bring her back."],"id":75,"m":"Follow, my lord, and I'll soon bring her back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Saturninus, Tamora, Demetrius, Chiron, Aaron, and Guards.","id":76},{"t":"sp","who":"MUTIUS","lines":["My lord, you pass not here."],"id":77,"m":"My lord, you shall not pass here."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["What, villain boy,","Barr'st me my way in Rome?"],"id":78,"m":"What, you villain boy, do you block my way in Rome?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Stabbing Mutius.","id":79},{"t":"sp","who":"MUTIUS","lines":["Help, Lucius, help!"],"id":80,"m":"Help, Lucius, help!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":81},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Lucius.","id":82},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["My lord, you are unjust, and more than so,","In wrongful quarrel you have slain your son."],"id":83,"m":"My lord, you are unjust, and worse than that: you have killed your son in a wrongful quarrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Nor thou nor he are any sons of mine;","My sons would never so dishonour me.","Traitor, restore Lavinia to the Emperor."],"id":84,"m":"Neither you nor he is any son of mine. My sons would never dishonour me like this. Traitor, give Lavinia back to the emperor."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Dead, if you will; but not to be his wife,","That is another's lawful promised love."],"id":85,"m":"Dead, if you like, but not to be his wife, when she is lawfully promised to another man."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":86},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter aloft the Emperor Saturninus with Tamora and her two sons and Aaron the Moor.","id":87},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["No, Titus, no; the emperor needs her not,","Nor her, nor thee, nor any of thy stock.","I'll trust by leisure him that mocks me once;","Thee never, nor thy traitorous haughty sons,","Confederates all thus to dishonour me.","Was none in Rome to make a stale","But Saturnine? Full well, Andronicus,","Agree these deeds with that proud brag of thine","That said'st I begged the empire at thy hands."],"id":88,"m":"No, Titus, no. The emperor does not need her, nor her, nor you, nor anyone of your family. I'll take my time before I trust a man who has made a fool of me once, and you I will never trust, nor your treacherous, arrogant sons, all plotting together to dishonour me. Was there no one in Rome to make a laughing stock of except Saturnine? These deeds fit very well, Andronicus, with your proud boast that I begged the empire from your hands."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["O monstrous! What reproachful words are these?"],"id":89,"m":"Monstrous! What insulting words are these?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["But go thy ways; go, give that changing piece","To him that flourished for her with his sword.","A valiant son-in-law thou shalt enjoy;","One fit to bandy with thy lawless sons,","To ruffle in the commonwealth of Rome."],"id":90,"m":"But go on your way. Go, give that fickle piece of goods to the man who waved his sword to win her. You'll have a brave son-in-law, a fit one to brawl with your lawless sons and swagger about causing trouble in the state of Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["These words are razors to my wounded heart."],"id":91,"m":"These words are razors to my wounded heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["And therefore, lovely Tamora, Queen of Goths,","That like the stately Phœbe 'mongst her nymphs","Dost overshine the gallant'st dames of Rome,","If thou be pleased with this my sudden choice,","Behold, I choose thee, Tamora, for my bride,","And will create thee Empress of Rome.","Speak, Queen of Goths, dost thou applaud my choice?","And here I swear by all the Roman gods,","Sith priest and holy water are so near,","And tapers burn so bright, and everything","In readiness for Hymenæus stand,","I will not re-salute the streets of Rome,","Or climb my palace, till from forth this place","I lead espoused my bride along with me."],"id":92,"m":"And so, lovely Tamora, Queen of the Goths, who outshine the finest ladies of Rome like the stately moon goddess among her nymphs: if you are pleased with this sudden choice of mine, look, I choose you, Tamora, to be my bride, and will make you Empress of Rome. Speak, Queen of the Goths, do you approve my choice? And here I swear by all the Roman gods, since the priest and holy water are so near, and the candles are burning so brightly, and everything stands ready for the god of marriage, that I will not greet the streets of Rome again or climb up to my palace until I lead my bride away from this place as my wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["And here in sight of heaven to Rome I swear,","If Saturnine advance the Queen of Goths,","She will a handmaid be to his desires,","A loving nurse, a mother to his youth."],"id":93,"m":"And here, in the sight of heaven, I swear to Rome that if Saturnine raises up the Queen of the Goths, she will be a servant to his wishes, a loving nurse, and a mother to his youth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Ascend, fair queen, Pantheon. Lords, accompany","Your noble emperor and his lovely bride,","Sent by the heavens for Prince Saturnine,","Whose wisdom hath her fortune conquered.","There shall we consummate our spousal rites."],"id":94,"m":"Fair queen, go up to the Pantheon. Lords, go with your noble emperor and his lovely bride, sent by the heavens for Prince Saturnine, who has wisely won her fortune for himself. There we will complete our marriage rites."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Titus.","id":95},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["I am not bid to wait upon this bride.","Titus, when wert thou wont to walk alone,","Dishonoured thus, and challenged of wrongs?"],"id":96,"m":"I am not invited to attend this bride. Titus, when did you ever walk alone like this, dishonoured and accused of wrongs?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Marcus, Lucius, Quintus and Martius.","id":97},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["O Titus, see, O, see what thou hast done!","In a bad quarrel slain a virtuous son."],"id":98,"m":"O Titus, look, look what you have done! You have killed a good son in a bad quarrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["No, foolish tribune, no; no son of mine,","Nor thou, nor these, confederates in the deed","That hath dishonoured all our family.","Unworthy brother and unworthy sons!"],"id":99,"m":"No, foolish tribune, no. He's no son of mine, and neither are you, nor these, who joined together in the deed that has dishonoured our whole family. Unworthy brother and unworthy sons!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["But let us give him burial, as becomes;","Give Mutius burial with our brethren."],"id":100,"m":"But let us bury him properly. Let us bury Mutius with our brothers."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Traitors, away! He rests not in this tomb.","This monument five hundred years hath stood,","Which I have sumptuously re-edified.","Here none but soldiers and Rome's servitors","Repose in fame; none basely slain in brawls.","Bury him where you can, he comes not here."],"id":101,"m":"Traitors, get away! He shall not rest in this tomb. This monument has stood for five hundred years, and I have rebuilt it at great cost. Here only soldiers and servants of Rome lie in glory, no one killed shamefully in a brawl. Bury him where you like; he is not coming in here."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["My lord, this is impiety in you.","My nephew Mutius' deeds do plead for him;","He must be buried with his brethren."],"id":102,"m":"My lord, this is a sin against the gods on your part. My nephew Mutius's deeds speak for him. He must be buried with his brothers."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["And shall, or him we will accompany."],"id":103,"m":"And he shall be, or we will go with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["\"And shall\"? What villain was it spake that word?"],"id":104,"m":"'And he shall be'? What villain spoke those words?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["He that would vouch it in any place but here."],"id":105,"m":"One who would stand by them anywhere but here."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["What, would you bury him in my despite?"],"id":106,"m":"What, would you bury him in spite of me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["No, noble Titus, but entreat of thee","To pardon Mutius and to bury him."],"id":107,"m":"No, noble Titus, but we beg you to forgive Mutius and to bury him."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Marcus, even thou hast struck upon my crest,","And with these boys mine honour thou hast wounded.","My foes I do repute you every one;","So trouble me no more, but get you gone."],"id":108,"m":"Marcus, even you have struck at my helmet, and with these boys you have wounded my honour. I count every one of you as my enemy. So trouble me no more, but go away."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["He is not with himself; let us withdraw."],"id":109,"m":"He is not himself. Let us leave."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["Not I, till Mutius' bones be buried."],"id":110,"m":"Not I, until Mutius's bones are buried."},{"t":"sd","text":"Marcus and the sons of Titus kneel.","id":111},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Brother, for in that name doth nature plead,—"],"id":112,"m":"Brother, for nature pleads in that name—"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["Father, and in that name doth nature speak,—"],"id":113,"m":"Father, and nature speaks in that name—"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Speak thou no more, if all the rest will speed."],"id":114,"m":"Say no more, if the rest of you want to succeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Renowned Titus, more than half my soul,—"],"id":115,"m":"Famous Titus, more than half my soul—"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Dear father, soul and substance of us all,—"],"id":116,"m":"Dear father, the soul and body of us all—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Suffer thy brother Marcus to inter","His noble nephew here in virtue's nest,","That died in honour and Lavinia's cause.","Thou art a Roman; be not barbarous.","The Greeks upon advice did bury Ajax,","That slew himself; and wise Laertes' son","Did graciously plead for his funerals.","Let not young Mutius, then, that was thy joy,","Be barred his entrance here."],"id":117,"m":"Let your brother Marcus bury his noble nephew here, in this nest of virtue, since he died with honour and for Lavinia's cause. You are a Roman: do not be a barbarian. After due thought the Greeks buried Ajax, though he killed himself, and Ulysses, the wise son of Laertes, kindly argued for his funeral. Then do not let young Mutius, who was your joy, be shut out of here."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Rise, Marcus, rise.","The dismall'st day is this that e'er I saw,","To be dishonoured by my sons in Rome!","Well, bury him, and bury me the next."],"id":118,"m":"Rise, Marcus, rise. This is the most miserable day I have ever seen: to be dishonoured by my own sons in Rome! Well, bury him, and bury me next."},{"t":"sd","text":"They put Mutius in the tomb.","id":119},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["There lie thy bones, sweet Mutius, with thy friends,","Till we with trophies do adorn thy tomb."],"id":120,"m":"Lie there, sweet Mutius, with your loved ones, until we decorate your tomb with trophies."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["[Kneeling] No man shed tears for noble Mutius;","He lives in fame that died in virtue's cause."],"id":121,"m":"(Kneeling) Let no one shed tears for noble Mutius. He who died in the cause of virtue lives on in fame."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["My lord, to step out of these dreary dumps,","How comes it that the subtle Queen of Goths","Is of a sudden thus advanced in Rome?"],"id":122,"m":"My lord, to come out of this gloom: how is it that the cunning Queen of the Goths has suddenly been raised so high in Rome?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["I know not, Marcus, but I know it is.","Whether by device or no, the heavens can tell.","Is she not then beholding to the man","That brought her for this high good turn so far?","Yes, and will nobly him remunerate."],"id":123,"m":"I don't know, Marcus, but I know it is so. Whether by scheming or not, heaven only knows. Isn't she in debt, then, to the man who brought her so far, to this great good fortune? Yes, and she will repay him nobly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter the Emperor Saturninus, Tamora and her two sons, with Aaron the Moor. Drums and Trumpets, at one door. Enter at the other door Bassianus and Lavinia with others.","id":124},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["So, Bassianus, you have played your prize.","God give you joy, sir, of your gallant bride."],"id":125,"m":"So, Bassianus, you have won your prize. God give you joy, sir, of your fine bride."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["And you of yours, my lord. I say no more,","Nor wish no less; and so I take my leave."],"id":126,"m":"And you of yours, my lord. I say no more, and wish no less, and so I take my leave."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Traitor, if Rome have law or we have power,","Thou and thy faction shall repent this rape."],"id":127,"m":"Traitor, if Rome has laws or I have power, you and your party will regret this abduction."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Rape call you it, my lord, to seize my own,","My true betrothed love, and now my wife?","But let the laws of Rome determine all;","Meanwhile am I possessed of that is mine."],"id":128,"m":"Do you call it abduction, my lord, to take what is mine, my true promised love and now my wife? But let the laws of Rome decide everything. Meanwhile I have what belongs to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["'Tis good, sir. You are very short with us;","But if we live, we'll be as sharp with you."],"id":129,"m":"Very well, sir. You are very curt with us, but if we live, we'll be just as sharp with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["My lord, what I have done, as best I may,","Answer I must, and shall do with my life.","Only thus much I give your grace to know:","By all the duties that I owe to Rome,","This noble gentleman, Lord Titus here,","Is in opinion and in honour wronged,","That, in the rescue of Lavinia,","With his own hand did slay his youngest son,","In zeal to you, and highly moved to wrath","To be controlled in that he frankly gave.","Receive him then to favour, Saturnine,","That hath expressed himself in all his deeds","A father and a friend to thee and Rome."],"id":130,"m":"My lord, I must answer for what I have done as best I can, and I will, with my life. I only want your grace to know this much: by all the duty I owe Rome, this noble gentleman, Lord Titus here, has been wronged in his reputation and his honour. To rescue Lavinia for you, he killed his youngest son with his own hand, out of loyalty to you, and furious at being thwarted in the gift he freely gave. So take him back into favour, Saturnine. In all his deeds he has shown himself a father and a friend to you and to Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Prince Bassianus, leave to plead my deeds.","'Tis thou, and those, that have dishonoured me.","Rome and the righteous heavens be my judge","How I have loved and honoured Saturnine."],"id":131,"m":"Prince Bassianus, stop pleading on my behalf. It is you and these others who have dishonoured me. Let Rome and the just heavens judge how much I have loved and honoured Saturnine."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["My worthy lord, if ever Tamora","Were gracious in those princely eyes of thine,","Then hear me speak indifferently for all;","And at my suit, sweet, pardon what is past."],"id":132,"m":"My worthy lord, if Tamora was ever pleasing in those princely eyes of yours, then hear me speak fairly for all of them, and at my request, my sweet, forgive what is past."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["What, madam, be dishonoured openly,","And basely put it up without revenge?"],"id":133,"m":"What, madam, be publicly dishonoured and meekly swallow it without revenge?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Not so, my lord; the gods of Rome forfend","I should be author to dishonour you!","But on mine honour dare I undertake","For good Lord Titus' innocence in all,","Whose fury not dissembled speaks his griefs.","Then at my suit look graciously on him;","Lose not so noble a friend on vain suppose,","Nor with sour looks afflict his gentle heart.","[Aside] My lord, be ruled by me, be won at last;","Dissemble all your griefs and discontents.","You are but newly planted in your throne;","Lest, then, the people, and patricians too,","Upon a just survey take Titus' part,","And so supplant you for ingratitude,","Which Rome reputes to be a heinous sin,","Yield at entreats, and then let me alone.","I'll find a day to massacre them all,","And raze their faction and their family,","The cruel father and his traitorous sons,","To whom I sued for my dear son's life;","And make them know what 'tis to let a queen","Kneel in the streets and beg for grace in vain.","[Aloud] Come, come, sweet emperor; come, Andronicus;","Take up this good old man, and cheer the heart","That dies in tempest of thy angry frown."],"id":134,"m":"Not so, my lord. The gods of Rome forbid that I should be the cause of your dishonour! But I dare to vouch on my honour that good Lord Titus is innocent in all this. His fury, which he does not hide, shows how much he is grieving. So at my request look kindly on him. Do not lose so noble a friend over an empty suspicion, or hurt his gentle heart with sour looks. (Aside, to Saturninus) My lord, take my advice. Let yourself be won over at last. Hide all your grievances and resentment. You have only just been planted on your throne. In case the people, and the lords too, look carefully at the matter, take Titus's side, and overthrow you for ingratitude, which Rome thinks a terrible sin, give way to our pleading, and leave the rest to me. I'll find a day to massacre them all, and wipe out their party and their family: the cruel father and his treacherous sons, whom I begged for my dear son's life. I'll make them learn what it means to let a queen kneel in the streets and beg for mercy in vain. (Aloud) Come, come, sweet emperor. Come, Andronicus. Raise up this good old man, and cheer the heart that is dying in the storm of your angry frown."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Rise, Titus, rise; my empress hath prevailed."],"id":135,"m":"Rise, Titus, rise. My empress has won me over."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["I thank your majesty and her, my lord.","These words, these looks, infuse new life in me."],"id":136,"m":"I thank your majesty, and her, my lord. These words and looks pour new life into me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Titus, I am incorporate in Rome,","A Roman now adopted happily,","And must advise the emperor for his good.","This day all quarrels die, Andronicus;","And let it be mine honour, good my lord,","That I have reconciled your friends and you.","For you, Prince Bassianus, I have passed","My word and promise to the emperor","That you will be more mild and tractable.","And fear not, lords, and you, Lavinia.","By my advice, all humbled on your knees,","You shall ask pardon of his majesty."],"id":137,"m":"Titus, I am now part of Rome, a Roman happily adopted, and I must advise the emperor for his own good. All quarrels die today, Andronicus. And let it be to my credit, my good lord, that I have reconciled your friends and you. As for you, Prince Bassianus, I have given my word and promise to the emperor that you will be gentler and more obedient. And do not be afraid, lords, nor you, Lavinia. On my advice, you shall all kneel humbly and ask his majesty's pardon."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["We do, and vow to heaven and to his highness","That what we did was mildly as we might,","Tend'ring our sister's honour and our own."],"id":138,"m":"We do, and we swear to heaven and to his highness that what we did was as gentle as we could make it, in protecting our sister's honour and our own."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["That on mine honour here do I protest."],"id":139,"m":"I swear to that here on my honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Away, and talk not; trouble us no more."],"id":140,"m":"Go away, and say nothing. Don't trouble us any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Nay, nay, sweet emperor, we must all be friends.","The tribune and his nephews kneel for grace;","I will not be denied. Sweet heart, look back."],"id":141,"m":"No, no, sweet emperor, we must all be friends. The tribune and his nephews are kneeling for pardon. I will not be refused. Sweetheart, look round."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Marcus, for thy sake, and thy brother's here,","And at my lovely Tamora's entreats,","I do remit these young men's heinous faults.","Stand up.","Lavinia, though you left me like a churl,","I found a friend, and sure as death I swore","I would not part a bachelor from the priest.","Come, if the emperor's court can feast two brides,","You are my guest, Lavinia, and your friends.","This day shall be a love-day, Tamora."],"id":142,"m":"Marcus, for your sake, and your brother's here, and at the request of my lovely Tamora, I forgive these young men's terrible offences. Stand up. Lavinia, though you left me like a peasant, I found a friend, and I swore as surely as death that I would not leave the priest a bachelor. Come, if the emperor's court can feast two brides, you are my guest, Lavinia, and your friends too. This shall be a day of reconciliation, Tamora."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Tomorrow, an it please your majesty","To hunt the panther and the hart with me,","With horn and hound we'll give your grace bonjour."],"id":143,"m":"Tomorrow, if your majesty would like to hunt the panther and the deer with me, we'll wish your grace good morning with horn and hound."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Be it so, Titus, and gramercy too."],"id":144,"m":"So be it, Titus, and many thanks."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound trumpets. Exeunt all but Aaron.","id":145},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. Before the palace","id":146},{"t":"sd","text":"Aaron alone.","id":147},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Now climbeth Tamora Olympus' top,","Safe out of Fortune's shot, and sits aloft,","Secure of thunder's crack or lightning's flash,","Advanced above pale envy's threat'ning reach.","As when the golden sun salutes the morn,","And, having gilt the ocean with his beams,","Gallops the zodiac in his glistening coach,","And overlooks the highest-peering hills;","So Tamora.","Upon her wit doth earthly honour wait,","And virtue stoops and trembles at her frown.","Then, Aaron, arm thy heart and fit thy thoughts","To mount aloft with thy imperial mistress,","And mount her pitch, whom thou in triumph long","Hast prisoner held, fett'red in amorous chains,","And faster bound to Aaron's charming eyes","Than is Prometheus tied to Caucasus.","Away with slavish weeds and servile thoughts!","I will be bright, and shine in pearl and gold,","To wait upon this new-made empress.","To wait, said I? To wanton with this queen,","This goddess, this Semiramis, this nymph,","This siren, that will charm Rome's Saturnine,","And see his shipwrack and his commonweal's.","Holla! What storm is this?"],"id":148,"m":"Now Tamora climbs to the top of Olympus, safe from Fortune's arrows, and sits up high, out of reach of the crack of thunder or the flash of lightning, raised above the threatening reach of pale envy. Just as the golden sun greets the morning, gilds the ocean with its rays, gallops through the zodiac in its glittering chariot and looks down on the highest hills: so does Tamora. Earthly honour waits on her intelligence, and virtue bows and trembles when she frowns. Then, Aaron, steel your heart and shape your thoughts to rise high with your imperial mistress. Rise to her height, she whom you have long held prisoner in triumph, chained in the chains of love, and bound faster to Aaron's charming eyes than Prometheus is chained to the Caucasus. Away with the clothes and thoughts of a slave! I will be splendid, and shine in pearl and gold, to serve this new-made empress. Serve, did I say? To play with this queen, this goddess, this Semiramis, this nymph, this siren, who will bewitch Rome's Saturnine and watch him and his state wrecked. Hello! What storm is this?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Chiron and Demetrius braving.","id":149},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Chiron, thy years wants wit, thy wit wants edge","And manners, to intrude where I am graced,","And may, for aught thou knowest, affected be."],"id":150,"m":"Chiron, you are too young to have any sense, and your sense has no sharpness and no manners, if you push in where I am in favour, and may be loved, for all you know."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Demetrius, thou dost overween in all,","And so in this, to bear me down with braves.","'Tis not the difference of a year or two","Makes me less gracious or thee more fortunate.","I am as able and as fit as thou","To serve and to deserve my mistress' grace;","And that my sword upon thee shall approve,","And plead my passions for Lavinia's love."],"id":151,"m":"Demetrius, you are arrogant about everything, and so you are about this, trying to crush me with threats. A year or two's difference in age doesn't make me less attractive or you more lucky. I am as able and as fit as you are to serve my mistress and to deserve her favour, and my sword shall prove it on you and argue my passion for Lavinia's love."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["[Aside] Clubs, clubs! These lovers will not keep the peace."],"id":152,"m":"(Aside) Break it up! These lovers will not keep the peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Why, boy, although our mother, unadvised,","Gave you a dancing-rapier by your side,","Are you so desperate grown to threat your friends?","Go to; have your lath glued within your sheath","Till you know better how to handle it."],"id":153,"m":"Why, boy, just because our mother foolishly gave you a dancing sword to wear at your side, have you grown so reckless that you threaten your own family? Get away. Have your wooden toy glued into its sheath until you know better how to use it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Meanwhile, sir, with the little skill I have,","Full well shalt thou perceive how much I dare."],"id":154,"m":"Meanwhile, sir, with the little skill I have, you'll soon see how much I dare."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Ay, boy, grow ye so brave?"],"id":155,"m":"Yes, boy, are you so brave now?"},{"t":"sd","text":"They draw.","id":156},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Why, how now, lords!","So near the emperor's palace dare ye draw,","And maintain such a quarrel openly?","Full well I wot the ground of all this grudge.","I would not for a million of gold","The cause were known to them it most concerns;","Nor would your noble mother for much more","Be so dishonoured in the court of Rome.","For shame, put up."],"id":157,"m":"Why, what's this, lords! Do you dare to draw your swords so near the emperor's palace and keep up a quarrel like this in the open? I know very well what this feud is about. I wouldn't have the cause known to the people it most concerns for a million in gold, and your noble mother would not be so dishonoured in the court of Rome for much more. For shame, put your swords away."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Not I, till I have sheathed","My rapier in his bosom, and withal","Thrust those reproachful speeches down his throat","That he hath breathed in my dishonour here."],"id":158,"m":"Not I, until I have sheathed my sword in his chest, and with it rammed those insults he has spoken against my honour down his throat."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["For that I am prepared and full resolved,","Foul-spoken coward, that thund'rest with thy tongue,","And with thy weapon nothing dar'st perform."],"id":159,"m":"I am ready and fully determined for that, you foul-mouthed coward, who thunder with your tongue and dare do nothing with your weapon."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Away, I say!","Now, by the gods that warlike Goths adore,","This pretty brabble will undo us all.","Why, lords, and think you not how dangerous","It is to jet upon a prince's right?","What, is Lavinia then become so loose,","Or Bassianus so degenerate,","That for her love such quarrels may be broached","Without controlment, justice, or revenge?","Young lords, beware! And should the empress know","This discord's ground, the music would not please."],"id":160,"m":"Get apart, I say! Now, by the gods the warlike Goths worship, this silly squabble will ruin us all. Why, lords, don't you see how dangerous it is to trespass on a prince's rights? What, has Lavinia become so loose, or Bassianus so feeble, that quarrels can be started over her love without restraint, justice or revenge? Young lords, be careful! If the empress knew the cause of this discord, the tune would not please her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["I care not, I, knew she and all the world.","I love Lavinia more than all the world."],"id":161,"m":"I don't care if she and all the world knew it. I love Lavinia more than all the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Youngling, learn thou to make some meaner choice.","Lavina is thine elder brother's hope."],"id":162,"m":"Youngster, learn to make a humbler choice. Lavinia is your elder brother's hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Why, are ye mad? Or know ye not in Rome","How furious and impatient they be,","And cannot brook competitors in love?","I tell you, lords, you do but plot your deaths","By this device."],"id":163,"m":"Why, are you mad? Don't you know how fierce and hot-tempered they are in Rome, and how they cannot stand rivals in love? I tell you, lords, with this plan you are only plotting your own deaths."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Aaron, a thousand deaths","Would I propose to achieve her whom I love."],"id":164,"m":"Aaron, I would face a thousand deaths to win the woman I love."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["To achieve her! How?"],"id":165,"m":"To win her! How?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Why makes thou it so strange?","She is a woman, therefore may be wooed;","She is a woman, therefore may be won;","She is Lavinia, therefore must be loved.","What, man, more water glideth by the mill","Than wots the miller of; and easy it is","Of a cut loaf to steal a shive, we know.","Though Bassianus be the emperor's brother,","Better than he have worn Vulcan's badge."],"id":166,"m":"Why are you so surprised? She is a woman, so she may be wooed. She is a woman, so she may be won. She is Lavinia, so she must be loved. What, man, more water slides past the mill than the miller knows about, and it's easy, we know, to steal a slice from a loaf that has already been cut. Though Bassianus is the emperor's brother, better men than he have worn the cuckold's horns."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["[Aside] Ay, and as good as Saturninus may."],"id":167,"m":"(Aside) Yes, and men as good as Saturninus may too."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Then why should he despair that knows to court it","With words, fair looks, and liberality?","What, hast not thou full often struck a doe,","And borne her cleanly by the keeper's nose?"],"id":168,"m":"Then why should a man despair, if he knows how to court a woman with words, good looks and generous gifts? What, haven't you often shot a deer and carried her off neatly under the gamekeeper's nose?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Why, then, it seems some certain snatch or so","Would serve your turns."],"id":169,"m":"Why, then it seems a quick grab or something like it would serve your purpose."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Ay, so the turn were served."],"id":170,"m":"Yes, if the purpose were served."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Aaron, thou hast hit it."],"id":171,"m":"Aaron, you've hit it."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Would you had hit it too!","Then should not we be tired with this ado.","Why, hark ye, hark ye, and are you such fools","To square for this? Would it offend you then","That both should speed?"],"id":172,"m":"I wish you had hit it too! Then we wouldn't be wearied with all this fuss. Why, listen, listen, are you such fools as to fight over this? Would it offend you if you both succeeded?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Faith, not me."],"id":173,"m":"Honestly, not me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Nor me, so I were one."],"id":174,"m":"Nor me, as long as I was one of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["For shame, be friends, and join for that you jar.","'Tis policy and stratagem must do","That you affect; and so must you resolve","That what you cannot as you would achieve,","You must perforce accomplish as you may.","Take this of me: Lucrece was not more chaste","Than this Lavinia, Bassianus' love.","A speedier course than ling'ring languishment","Must we pursue, and I have found the path.","My lords, a solemn hunting is in hand;","There will the lovely Roman ladies troop.","The forest walks are wide and spacious,","And many unfrequented plots there are","Fitted by kind for rape and villainy.","Single you thither, then, this dainty doe,","And strike her home by force, if not by words.","This way, or not at all, stand you in hope.","Come, come, our empress, with her sacred wit","To villainy and vengeance consecrate,","Will we acquaint with all what we intend;","And she shall file our engines with advice","That will not suffer you to square yourselves,","But to your wishes' height advance you both.","The emperor's court is like the house of Fame,","The palace full of tongues, of eyes and ears;","The woods are ruthless, dreadful, deaf, and dull.","There speak and strike, brave boys, and take your turns;","There serve your lust, shadowed from heaven's eye,","And revel in Lavinia's treasury."],"id":175,"m":"For shame, be friends, and join together for what you are quarrelling over. Cunning and strategy must get you what you want. You must make up your minds that what you cannot achieve the way you would like, you must achieve however you can. Take it from me: Lucrece was not more chaste than this Lavinia, Bassianus's love. We must take a quicker route than lingering lovesickness, and I have found the way. My lords, a grand hunt is being planned. The lovely Roman ladies will flock there. The forest paths are wide and roomy, and there are many lonely places that nature has made fit for rape and villainy. Pick out this dainty doe there, then, and bring her down by force if not by words. This way, or not at all, is your hope. Come, come, we will tell our empress everything we intend. Her mind is devoted to villainy and revenge, and she will sharpen our plans with advice that will stop you fighting each other and lift you both to the height of your wishes. The emperor's court is like the House of Rumour: the palace is full of tongues, eyes and ears. The woods are pitiless, dreadful, deaf and silent. There, brave boys, speak and strike and take your turns. There satisfy your lust, hidden from heaven's eye, and feast on Lavinia's treasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Thy counsel, lad, smells of no cowardice."],"id":176,"m":"Your advice, lad, doesn't smell of cowardice."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Sit fas aut nefas, till I find the stream","To cool this heat, a charm to calm these fits,","Per Stygia, per manes vehor."],"id":177,"m":"Right or wrong, until I find the stream to cool this heat and a spell to calm these fits, I am carried through the Styx and among the ghosts of the dead."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":178},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"A Forest near Rome; a Lodge seen at a distance. Horns and cry of hounds heard","id":179},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Titus Andronicus and his three sons, and Marcus, making a noise with hounds and horns.","id":180},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["The hunt is up, the morn is bright and grey,","The fields are fragrant, and the woods are green.","Uncouple here, and let us make a bay,","And wake the emperor and his lovely bride,","And rouse the prince, and ring a hunter's peal,","That all the court may echo with the noise.","Sons, let it be your charge, as it is ours,","To attend the emperor's person carefully.","I have been troubled in my sleep this night,","But dawning day new comfort hath inspired."],"id":181,"m":"The hunt is on, the morning is bright and grey, the fields smell sweet and the woods are green. Let the hounds loose here, and let us make them bark, and wake the emperor and his lovely bride, and rouse the prince, and ring out a hunting peal so the whole court echoes with the noise. Sons, make it your duty, as it is ours, to look after the emperor's person carefully. My sleep was troubled last night, but the dawning day has given me new comfort."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here a cry of hounds, and wind horns in a peal. Then enter Saturninus, Tamora, Bassianus, Lavinia, Chiron, Demetrius, and their Attendants.","id":182},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["Many good morrows to your majesty;","Madam, to you as many and as good.","I promised your grace a hunter's peal."],"id":183,"m":"Many good mornings to your majesty. Madam, as many to you, and just as good. I promised your grace a hunting peal."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["And you have rung it lustily, my lords;","Somewhat too early for new-married ladies."],"id":184,"m":"And you have rung it heartily, my lords: a little too early for newly married ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Lavinia, how say you?"],"id":185,"m":"Lavinia, what do you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["I say no;","I have been broad awake two hours and more."],"id":186,"m":"I say no. I have been wide awake for more than two hours."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Come on then; horse and chariots let us have,","And to our sport. [To Tamora] Madam, now shall ye see","Our Roman hunting."],"id":187,"m":"Come on, then. Let's have horses and chariots, and off to our sport. (To Tamora) Madam, now you shall see our Roman hunting."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["I have dogs, my lord,","Will rouse the proudest panther in the chase,","And climb the highest promontory top."],"id":188,"m":"I have dogs, my lord, that will flush out the proudest panther in the hunt and climb to the top of the highest cliff."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["And I have horse will follow where the game","Makes way, and run like swallows o'er the plain."],"id":189,"m":"And I have horses that will follow wherever the game runs, and race across the plain like swallows."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Chiron, we hunt not, we, with horse nor hound,","But hope to pluck a dainty doe to ground."],"id":190,"m":"Chiron, we are not hunting with horse or hound, but hoping to pull a dainty doe to the ground."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":191},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"A lonely part of the Forest","id":192},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aaron, alone, carrying a bag of gold.","id":193},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["He that had wit would think that I had none,","To bury so much gold under a tree,","And never after to inherit it.","Let him that thinks of me so abjectly","Know that this gold must coin a stratagem,","Which, cunningly effected, will beget","A very excellent piece of villainy.","And so repose, sweet gold, for their unrest","That have their alms out of the empress' chest."],"id":194,"m":"A clever man would think I had no sense, burying so much gold under a tree and never getting to enjoy it afterwards. Let anyone who thinks so poorly of me know that this gold must mint a scheme which, carried out cunningly, will produce a really excellent piece of villainy. So rest, sweet gold, to bring trouble on those who get their charity out of the empress's purse."},{"t":"sd","text":"He hides the bag.","id":195},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Tamora alone to the Moor.","id":196},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["My lovely Aaron, wherefore look'st thou sad","When everything doth make a gleeful boast?","The birds chant melody on every bush,","The snakes lie rolled in the cheerful sun,","The green leaves quiver with the cooling wind,","And make a chequered shadow on the ground.","Under their sweet shade, Aaron, let us sit,","And whilst the babbling echo mocks the hounds,","Replying shrilly to the well-tuned horns,","As if a double hunt were heard at once,","Let us sit down and mark their yelping noise;","And after conflict such as was supposed","The wand'ring prince and Dido once enjoyed,","When with a happy storm they were surprised,","And curtained with a counsel-keeping cave,","We may, each wreathed in the other's arms,","Our pastimes done, possess a golden slumber,","Whiles hounds and horns and sweet melodious birds","Be unto us as is a nurse's song","Of lullaby to bring her babe asleep."],"id":197,"m":"My lovely Aaron, why do you look sad when everything is showing off its happiness? The birds sing on every bush, the snakes lie coiled in the cheerful sun, the green leaves quiver in the cool breeze and make a chequered shadow on the ground. Let us sit in their sweet shade, Aaron, and while the chattering echo mocks the hounds, answering the tuneful horns so shrilly that it sounds like two hunts at once, let us sit and listen to their yelping. And after a struggle like the one the wandering prince Aeneas and Dido are said to have enjoyed, when a lucky storm caught them and they were hidden in a cave that kept their secret, we may lie wrapped in each other's arms, our games over, and fall into a golden sleep, while hounds and horns and sweet singing birds are to us like a nurse's lullaby that sends her baby to sleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Madam, though Venus govern your desires,","Saturn is dominator over mine.","What signifies my deadly-standing eye,","My silence and my cloudy melancholy,","My fleece of woolly hair that now uncurls","Even as an adder when she doth unroll","To do some fatal execution?","No, madam, these are no venereal signs.","Vengeance is in my heart, death in my hand,","Blood and revenge are hammering in my head.","Hark, Tamora, the empress of my soul,","Which never hopes more heaven than rests in thee,","This is the day of doom for Bassianus;","His Philomel must lose her tongue today,","Thy sons make pillage of her chastity,","And wash their hands in Bassianus' blood.","Seest thou this letter? Take it up, I pray thee,","And give the king this fatal-plotted scroll.","Now question me no more; we are espied;","Here comes a parcel of our hopeful booty,","Which dreads not yet their lives' destruction."],"id":198,"m":"Madam, though Venus rules your desires, Saturn rules mine. What do you think my deadly fixed stare means, my silence and my gloomy melancholy, my fleece of woolly hair uncurling like an adder unrolling to make a fatal strike? No, madam, these are not signs of love. Vengeance is in my heart and death in my hand. Blood and revenge are hammering in my head. Listen, Tamora, empress of my soul, which hopes for no more heaven than it finds in you: this is the day of doom for Bassianus. His Philomel must lose her tongue today. Your sons will plunder her chastity and wash their hands in Bassianus's blood. Do you see this letter? Please pick it up and give the king this deadly scroll. Now ask me nothing more. We are being watched. Here comes some of the loot we are hoping for, and they have no idea their lives are about to be destroyed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bassianus and Lavinia.","id":199},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Ah, my sweet Moor, sweeter to me than life!"],"id":200,"m":"Ah, my sweet Moor, sweeter to me than life!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["No more, great empress. Bassianus comes.","Be cross with him; and I'll go fetch thy sons","To back thy quarrels, whatsoe'er they be."],"id":201,"m":"No more, great empress. Bassianus is coming. Pick a quarrel with him, and I'll go and fetch your sons to support you, whatever the quarrel is."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":202},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Who have we here? Rome's royal empress,","Unfurnished of her well-beseeming troop?","Or is it Dian, habited like her,","Who hath abandoned her holy groves","To see the general hunting in this forest?"],"id":203,"m":"Who is this? Rome's royal empress, without her proper company? Or is it Diana, dressed like her, who has left her holy groves to watch the public hunt in this forest?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Saucy controller of my private steps!","Had I the power that some say Dian had,","Thy temples should be planted presently","With horns, as was Actaeon's; and the hounds","Should drive upon thy new-transformed limbs,","Unmannerly intruder as thou art."],"id":204,"m":"You insolent spy on my private walks! If I had the power some say Diana had, your forehead would sprout horns at once, like Actaeon's, and the hounds would leap on your newly transformed body, rude intruder that you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["Under your patience, gentle empress,","'Tis thought you have a goodly gift in horning,","And to be doubted that your Moor and you","Are singled forth to try experiments.","Jove shield your husband from his hounds today!","'Tis pity they should take him for a stag."],"id":205,"m":"With respect, gentle empress, people think you have a fine talent for giving men horns, and it is suspected that you and your Moor have gone off alone to try some experiments. Jove protect your husband from his hounds today! It would be a pity if they took him for a stag."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["Believe me, queen, your swarthy Cimmerian","Doth make your honour of his body's hue,","Spotted, detested, and abominable.","Why are you sequestered from all your train,","Dismounted from your snow-white goodly steed,","And wandered hither to an obscure plot,","Accompanied but with a barbarous Moor,","If foul desire had not conducted you?"],"id":206,"m":"Believe me, queen, your dark-skinned Cimmerian is making your honour the colour of his body: stained, hated and disgusting. Why are you separated from all your attendants, off your fine snow-white horse, and wandering in this hidden spot with no one but a barbarous Moor, unless foul desire led you here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["And, being intercepted in your sport,","Great reason that my noble lord be rated","For sauciness. I pray you, let us hence,","And let her joy her raven-coloured love;","This valley fits the purpose passing well."],"id":207,"m":"And since we have interrupted your sport, there is good reason to scold my noble lord for his rudeness. Please, let's go, and let her enjoy her raven-coloured love. This valley suits the purpose extremely well."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSIANUS","lines":["The king my brother shall have notice of this."],"id":208,"m":"The king my brother shall hear about this."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["Ay, for these slips have made him noted long.","Good king, to be so mightily abused!"],"id":209,"m":"Yes, these slips have made him notorious for a long time. Good king, to be so badly deceived!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Why, I have patience to endure all this."],"id":210,"m":"Why, I have patience to put up with all this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Chiron and Demetrius.","id":211},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["How now, dear sovereign, and our gracious mother!","Why doth your highness look so pale and wan?"],"id":212,"m":"What's the matter, dear sovereign and our gracious mother? Why does your highness look so pale and drained?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Have I not reason, think you, to look pale?","These two have ticed me hither to this place,","A barren detested vale you see it is;","The trees, though summer, yet forlorn and lean,","Overcome with moss and baleful mistletoe.","Here never shines the sun, here nothing breeds,","Unless the nightly owl or fatal raven.","And when they showed me this abhorred pit,","They told me, here, at dead time of the night,","A thousand fiends, a thousand hissing snakes,","Ten thousand swelling toads, as many urchins,","Would make such fearful and confused cries","As any mortal body hearing it","Should straight fall mad, or else die suddenly.","No sooner had they told this hellish tale","But straight they told me they would bind me here","Unto the body of a dismal yew,","And leave me to this miserable death.","And then they called me foul adulteress,","Lascivious Goth, and all the bitterest terms","That ever ear did hear to such effect.","And had you not by wondrous fortune come,","This vengeance on me had they executed.","Revenge it, as you love your mother's life,","Or be ye not henceforth called my children."],"id":213,"m":"Don't you think I have reason to look pale? These two lured me here to this place. You can see it is a barren, hateful valley. The trees, though it is summer, are bare and thin, smothered in moss and deadly mistletoe. The sun never shines here, nothing breeds here, except the owl of the night and the raven of death. And when they showed me this horrible pit, they told me that here, in the dead of night, a thousand devils, a thousand hissing snakes, ten thousand swelling toads and as many hedgehogs make such terrifying, confused cries that any living person who hears them would go mad at once or die suddenly. As soon as they had told this hellish story, they told me they would tie me here to the trunk of a gloomy yew tree and leave me to this miserable death. And then they called me a foul adulteress, a lustful Goth, and all the bitterest names ever heard of that kind. If you had not come here by wonderful luck, they would have carried out this vengeance on me. Revenge it, if you love your mother's life, or never call yourselves my children again."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["This is a witness that I am thy son."],"id":214,"m":"This is proof that I am your son."},{"t":"sd","text":"Stabs Bassianus.","id":215},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["And this for me, struck home to show my strength."],"id":216,"m":"And this is for me, struck deep to show my strength."},{"t":"sd","text":"Also stabs Bassianus, who dies.","id":217},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["Ay, come, Semiramis, nay, barbarous Tamora,","For no name fits thy nature but thy own!"],"id":218,"m":"Yes, come, Semiramis, no, barbarous Tamora, for no name fits your nature but your own!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Give me thy poniard; you shall know, my boys,","Your mother's hand shall right your mother's wrong."],"id":219,"m":"Give me your dagger. You shall see, my boys, that your mother's hand will avenge your mother's wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Stay, madam, here is more belongs to her.","First thrash the corn, then after burn the straw.","This minion stood upon her chastity,","Upon her nuptial vow, her loyalty,","And with that painted hope braves your mightiness;","And shall she carry this unto her grave?"],"id":220,"m":"Wait, madam, there is more owed to her. First thresh the corn, then burn the straw. This little madam made a great deal of her chastity, her marriage vow and her loyalty, and with that pretty show she defies your greatness. Shall she carry it with her to the grave?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["And if she do, I would I were an eunuch.","Drag hence her husband to some secret hole,","And make his dead trunk pillow to our lust."],"id":221,"m":"If she does, I wish I were a eunuch. Drag her husband away to some hidden hole, and make his dead body a pillow for our lust."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["But when ye have the honey ye desire,","Let not this wasp outlive, us both to sting."],"id":222,"m":"But when you have the honey you want, do not let this wasp live on to sting us both."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["I warrant you, madam, we will make that sure.","Come, mistress, now perforce we will enjoy","That nice-preserved honesty of yours."],"id":223,"m":"I promise you, madam, we will make sure of that. Come, mistress, now we will enjoy by force that carefully guarded chastity of yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["O Tamora, thou bearest a woman's face,—"],"id":224,"m":"O Tamora, you have a woman's face—"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["I will not hear her speak; away with her!"],"id":225,"m":"I won't hear her speak. Take her away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["Sweet lords, entreat her hear me but a word."],"id":226,"m":"Sweet lords, beg her to hear just one word from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Listen, fair madam: let it be your glory","To see her tears; but be your heart to them","As unrelenting flint to drops of rain."],"id":227,"m":"Listen, fair madam. Let it be your glory to see her tears, but let your heart be to them as hard as flint is to drops of rain."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["When did the tiger's young ones teach the dam?","O, do not learn her wrath; she taught it thee;","The milk thou suck'st from her did turn to marble;","Even at thy teat thou hadst thy tyranny.","Yet every mother breeds not sons alike.","[To Chiron] Do thou entreat her show a woman's pity."],"id":228,"m":"When did a tiger's cubs ever teach their mother? O, don't learn anger from her: she taught it to you. The milk you sucked from her turned to marble. You got your cruelty at her breast. Yet not every mother has sons who are alike. (To Chiron) You beg her to show a woman's pity."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["What, wouldst thou have me prove myself a bastard?"],"id":229,"m":"What, would you have me prove I'm a bastard?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["'Tis true the raven doth not hatch a lark.","Yet have I heard—O, could I find it now!—","The lion, moved with pity, did endure","To have his princely paws pared all away.","Some say that ravens foster forlorn children,","The whilst their own birds famish in their nests.","O, be to me, though thy hard heart say no,","Nothing so kind, but something pitiful."],"id":230,"m":"It's true a raven does not hatch a lark. Yet I have heard (O, if only I could find it true now!) that a lion, moved by pity, let his royal claws be clipped away. Some say ravens feed abandoned children while their own young starve in the nest. O, though your hard heart says no, be, not so kind to me, but just a little merciful."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["I know not what it means; away with her!"],"id":231,"m":"I don't know what that means. Take her away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["O, let me teach thee! For my father's sake,","That gave thee life when well he might have slain thee,","Be not obdurate, open thy deaf ears."],"id":232,"m":"O, let me teach you! For the sake of my father, who spared your life when he could easily have killed you, don't be so hard. Open your deaf ears."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Hadst thou in person ne'er offended me,","Even for his sake am I pitiless.","Remember, boys, I poured forth tears in vain","To save your brother from the sacrifice,","But fierce Andronicus would not relent.","Therefore away with her, and use her as you will;","The worse to her, the better loved of me."],"id":233,"m":"Even if you had never offended me yourself, I would be pitiless for his sake. Remember, boys, I poured out tears in vain to save your brother from the sacrifice, but fierce Andronicus would not give way. So take her away, and do what you like with her. The worse for her, the more I'll love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["O Tamora, be called a gentle queen,","And with thine own hands kill me in this place!","For 'tis not life that I have begged so long;","Poor I was slain when Bassianus died."],"id":234,"m":"O Tamora, earn the name of a gentle queen, and kill me here with your own hands! For it is not life I have been begging for all this time. Poor me, I died when Bassianus died."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["What begg'st thou, then? Fond woman, let me go."],"id":235,"m":"What are you begging for, then? Foolish woman, let me go."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["'Tis present death I beg; and one thing more","That womanhood denies my tongue to tell.","O, keep me from their worse than killing lust,","And tumble me into some loathsome pit,","Where never man's eye may behold my body.","Do this, and be a charitable murderer."],"id":236,"m":"It is instant death I beg for, and one thing more that my modesty will not let my tongue name. O, save me from their lust, which is worse than killing, and throw me into some foul pit where no man will ever see my body. Do this, and be a merciful murderer."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["So should I rob my sweet sons of their fee.","No, let them satisfy their lust on thee."],"id":237,"m":"Then I'd be robbing my sweet sons of their reward. No, let them satisfy their lust on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Away, for thou hast stayed us here too long."],"id":238,"m":"Come on, you have kept us here too long."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAVINIA","lines":["No grace, no womanhood? Ah, beastly creature,","The blot and enemy to our general name!","Confusion fall—"],"id":239,"m":"No grace, no womanhood? Ah, you beastly creature, the shame and enemy of all women! May destruction fall—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Nay, then I'll stop your mouth. Bring thou her husband.","This is the hole where Aaron bid us hide him."],"id":240,"m":"No, then I'll stop your mouth. You bring her husband. This is the hole where Aaron told us to hide him."},{"t":"sd","text":"They put Bassianus's body in the pit and exit, carrying off Lavinia.","id":241},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Farewell, my sons. See that you make her sure.","Ne'er let my heart know merry cheer indeed","Till all the Andronici be made away.","Now will I hence to seek my lovely Moor,","And let my spleenful sons this trull deflower."],"id":242,"m":"Farewell, my sons. Make sure of her. My heart will never know real happiness until all the Andronici are done away with. Now I'll go and find my lovely Moor, and let my angry sons rape this whore."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":243},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aaron with two of Titus' sons, Quintus and Martius.","id":244},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Come on, my lords, the better foot before.","Straight will I bring you to the loathsome pit","Where I espied the panther fast asleep."],"id":245,"m":"Come on, my lords, best foot forward. I'll take you straight to the horrible pit where I saw the panther fast asleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["My sight is very dull, whate'er it bodes."],"id":246,"m":"My eyes are very heavy, whatever that means."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["And mine, I promise you. Were it not for shame,","Well could I leave our sport to sleep awhile."],"id":247,"m":"Mine too, I promise you. If it weren't shameful, I could happily leave the hunt and sleep for a while."},{"t":"sd","text":"He falls into the pit.","id":248},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["What, art thou fallen? What subtle hole is this,","Whose mouth is covered with rude-growing briers,","Upon whose leaves are drops of new-shed blood","As fresh as morning dew distilled on flowers?","A very fatal place it seems to me.","Speak, brother, hast thou hurt thee with the fall?"],"id":249,"m":"What, have you fallen? What hidden hole is this, its mouth covered with wild-growing brambles, with drops of freshly spilt blood on their leaves, as fresh as morning dew on flowers? It seems a deadly place to me. Speak, brother, have you hurt yourself in the fall?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["O brother, with the dismall'st object hurt","That ever eye with sight made heart lament!"],"id":250,"m":"O brother, I am hurt by the most dreadful sight that ever made a heart grieve!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["[Aside] Now will I fetch the king to find them here,","That he thereby may have a likely guess","How these were they that made away his brother."],"id":251,"m":"(Aside) Now I'll fetch the king to find them here, so that he can make a likely guess that these are the ones who killed his brother."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":252},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["Why dost not comfort me, and help me out","From this unhallowed and blood-stained hole?"],"id":253,"m":"Why don't you comfort me and help me out of this unholy, bloodstained hole?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["I am surprised with an uncouth fear;","A chilling sweat o'er-runs my trembling joints.","My heart suspects more than mine eye can see."],"id":254,"m":"I am gripped by a strange fear. A cold sweat runs over my trembling limbs. My heart suspects more than my eyes can see."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["To prove thou hast a true-divining heart,","Aaron and thou look down into this den,","And see a fearful sight of blood and death."],"id":255,"m":"To prove your heart has guessed right, you and Aaron look down into this den, and see a terrible sight of blood and death."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["Aaron is gone, and my compassionate heart","Will not permit mine eyes once to behold","The thing whereat it trembles by surmise.","O, tell me who it is; for ne'er till now","Was I a child to fear I know not what."],"id":256,"m":"Aaron has gone, and my pitying heart will not let my eyes look at the thing it trembles to imagine. O, tell me who it is, for until now I was never so childish as to be afraid of I don't know what."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["Lord Bassianus lies berayed in blood,","All on a heap, like to a slaughtered lamb,","In this detested, dark, blood-drinking pit."],"id":257,"m":"Lord Bassianus lies here smeared in blood, all in a heap like a slaughtered lamb, in this hateful, dark, blood-drinking pit."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["If it be dark, how dost thou know 'tis he?"],"id":258,"m":"If it's dark, how do you know it's him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["Upon his bloody finger he doth wear","A precious ring that lightens all the hole,","Which, like a taper in some monument,","Doth shine upon the dead man's earthy cheeks,","And shows the ragged entrails of the pit.","So pale did shine the moon on Pyramus","When he by night lay bathed in maiden blood.","O brother, help me with thy fainting hand,","If fear hath made thee faint, as me it hath,","Out of this fell devouring receptacle,","As hateful as Cocytus' misty mouth."],"id":259,"m":"On his bloody finger he wears a precious ring that lights up the whole hole. Like a candle in a tomb, it shines on the dead man's earthy cheeks and shows the jagged insides of the pit. The moon shone just as pale on Pyramus when he lay at night bathed in a maiden's blood. O brother, if fear has made you faint, as it has me, help me with your fainting hand out of this cruel, devouring hole, as hateful as the misty mouth of the river Cocytus in hell."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["Reach me thy hand, that I may help thee out,","Or, wanting strength to do thee so much good,","I may be plucked into the swallowing womb","Of this deep pit, poor Bassianus' grave.","I have no strength to pluck thee to the brink."],"id":260,"m":"Give me your hand so that I can help you out, or, if I haven't the strength to do you that much good, be pulled down myself into the swallowing belly of this deep pit, poor Bassianus's grave. I haven't the strength to pull you to the edge."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["Nor I no strength to climb without thy help."],"id":261,"m":"And I haven't the strength to climb out without your help."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINTUS","lines":["Thy hand once more; I will not loose again,","Till thou art here aloft, or I below.","Thou canst not come to me. I come to thee."],"id":262,"m":"Your hand once more. I won't let go again until you are up here or I am down there. You can't come to me: I'll come to you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Falls in.","id":263},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Emperor Saturninus and Aaron the Moor.","id":264},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Along with me! I'll see what hole is here,","And what he is that now is leapt into it.","Say, who art thou that lately didst descend","Into this gaping hollow of the earth?"],"id":265,"m":"Come with me! I'll see what hole this is, and who it is that has just jumped into it. Tell me, who are you who just went down into this gaping hollow in the earth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["The unhappy sons of old Andronicus,","Brought hither in a most unlucky hour,","To find thy brother Bassianus dead."],"id":266,"m":"The unlucky sons of old Andronicus, brought here at a most unlucky hour to find your brother Bassianus dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["My brother dead! I know thou dost but jest.","He and his lady both are at the lodge","Upon the north side of this pleasant chase;","'Tis not an hour since I left them there."],"id":267,"m":"My brother dead! I know you are only joking. He and his lady are both at the lodge on the north side of this pleasant hunting ground. I left them there less than an hour ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARTIUS","lines":["We know not where you left them all alive;","But, out, alas, here have we found him dead."],"id":268,"m":"We don't know where you left them alive, but, alas, we have found him dead here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Tamora, Titus Andronicus and Lucius.","id":269},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Where is my lord the king?"],"id":270,"m":"Where is my lord the king?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Here, Tamora; though grieved with killing grief."],"id":271,"m":"Here, Tamora, though stricken with a killing grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Where is thy brother Bassianus?"],"id":272,"m":"Where is your brother Bassianus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Now to the bottom dost thou search my wound.","Poor Bassianus here lies murdered."],"id":273,"m":"Now you are probing to the bottom of my wound. Poor Bassianus lies here murdered."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Then all too late I bring this fatal writ,","The complot of this timeless tragedy;","And wonder greatly that man's face can fold","In pleasing smiles such murderous tyranny."],"id":274,"m":"Then I bring this deadly letter far too late: the plot of this untimely tragedy. And I am amazed that a man's face can hide such murderous cruelty behind pleasant smiles."},{"t":"sd","text":"She giveth Saturnine a letter.","id":275},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["[Reads] An if we miss to meet him handsomely,","Sweet huntsman, Bassianus 'tis we mean,","Do thou so much as dig the grave for him;","Thou know'st our meaning. Look for thy reward","Among the nettles at the elder-tree","Which overshades the mouth of that same pit","Where we decreed to bury Bassianus.","Do this, and purchase us thy lasting friends.","O Tamora, was ever heard the like?","This is the pit, and this the elder-tree.","Look, sirs, if you can find the huntsman out","That should have murdered Bassianus here."],"id":276,"m":"(Reads) 'If we fail to catch him neatly, sweet huntsman (it's Bassianus we mean), you just dig the grave for him. You know what we mean. Look for your reward among the nettles at the elder tree which overhangs the mouth of the same pit where we decided to bury Bassianus. Do this, and make us your friends for ever.' O Tamora, did anyone ever hear the like? This is the pit, and this is the elder tree. Look, sirs, and see if you can find the huntsman who was meant to murder Bassianus here."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["My gracious lord, here is the bag of gold."],"id":277,"m":"My gracious lord, here is the bag of gold."},{"t":"sd","text":"Showing it.","id":278},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["[To Titus] Two of thy whelps, fell curs of bloody kind,","Have here bereft my brother of his life.","Sirs, drag them from the pit unto the prison.","There let them bide until we have devised","Some never-heard-of torturing pain for them."],"id":279,"m":"(To Titus) Two of your puppies, savage curs of a bloody breed, have taken my brother's life here. Sirs, drag them from the pit to prison. Let them stay there until we have thought up some never-heard-of torture for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["What, are they in this pit? O wondrous thing!","How easily murder is discovered!"],"id":280,"m":"What, are they in this pit? How amazing! How easily murder comes to light!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["High emperor, upon my feeble knee","I beg this boon, with tears not lightly shed,","That this fell fault of my accursed sons,","Accursed if the fault be proved in them—"],"id":281,"m":"Mighty emperor, on my weak knee I beg this favour, with tears not easily shed: that this cruel crime of my cursed sons, cursed if the crime is proved against them—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["If it be proved! You see it is apparent.","Who found this letter? Tamora, was it you?"],"id":282,"m":"If it's proved! You can see it is obvious. Who found this letter? Tamora, was it you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Andronicus himself did take it up."],"id":283,"m":"Andronicus himself picked it up."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["I did, my lord, yet let me be their bail;","For by my fathers' reverend tomb I vow","They shall be ready at your highness' will","To answer their suspicion with their lives."],"id":284,"m":"I did, my lord, yet let me stand bail for them. For I swear by my fathers' sacred tomb that they will be ready whenever your highness wishes to answer the suspicion with their lives."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Thou shalt not bail them. See thou follow me.","Some bring the murdered body, some the murderers.","Let them not speak a word; the guilt is plain;","For, by my soul, were there worse end than death,","That end upon them should be executed."],"id":285,"m":"You shall not stand bail for them. Make sure you follow me. Some of you bring the murdered body, some the murderers. Don't let them speak a word: the guilt is plain. For, by my soul, if there were a worse end than death, they would suffer it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Andronicus, I will entreat the king.","Fear not thy sons; they shall do well enough."],"id":286,"m":"Andronicus, I will plead with the king. Don't worry about your sons; they'll be all right."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Come, Lucius, come; stay not to talk with them."],"id":287,"m":"Come, Lucius, come. Don't stay to talk with them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt severally. Attendants bearing the body.","id":288},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"Another part of the Forest","id":289},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the empress' sons, Demetrius and Chiron with Lavinia, her hands cut off, and her tongue cut out, and ravished.","id":290},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["So, now go tell, an if thy tongue can speak,","Who 'twas that cut thy tongue and ravished thee."],"id":291,"m":"So, now go and tell, if your tongue can speak, who it was that cut out your tongue and raped you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Write down thy mind, bewray thy meaning so,","An if thy stumps will let thee play the scribe."],"id":292,"m":"Write down your thoughts and reveal your meaning that way, if your stumps will let you play the writer."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["See how with signs and tokens she can scrowl."],"id":293,"m":"See how she can scrawl with signs and gestures."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Go home, call for sweet water, wash thy hands."],"id":294,"m":"Go home, call for scented water, and wash your hands."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["She hath no tongue to call, nor hands to wash;","And so let's leave her to her silent walks."],"id":295,"m":"She has no tongue to call with and no hands to wash, so let's leave her to her silent walks."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["An 'twere my cause, I should go hang myself."],"id":296,"m":"If it were me, I would go and hang myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["If thou hadst hands to help thee knit the cord."],"id":297,"m":"If you had hands to help you tie the rope."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Chiron and Demetrius.","id":298},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Marcus, from hunting.","id":299},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Who is this? My niece, that flies away so fast?","Cousin, a word; where is your husband?","If I do dream, would all my wealth would wake me!","If I do wake, some planet strike me down,","That I may slumber an eternal sleep!","Speak, gentle niece, what stern ungentle hands","Hath lopped and hewed and made thy body bare","Of her two branches, those sweet ornaments","Whose circling shadows kings have sought to sleep in,","And might not gain so great a happiness","As half thy love? Why dost not speak to me?","Alas, a crimson river of warm blood,","Like to a bubbling fountain stirred with wind,","Doth rise and fall between thy rosed lips,","Coming and going with thy honey breath.","But sure some Tereus hath deflowered thee,","And, lest thou shouldst detect him, cut thy tongue.","Ah, now thou turn'st away thy face for shame,","And notwithstanding all this loss of blood,","As from a conduit with three issuing spouts,","Yet do thy cheeks look red as Titan's face","Blushing to be encountered with a cloud.","Shall I speak for thee, shall I say 'tis so?","O, that I knew thy heart, and knew the beast,","That I might rail at him to ease my mind.","Sorrow concealed, like an oven stopped,","Doth burn the heart to cinders where it is.","Fair Philomela, why she but lost her tongue,","And in a tedious sampler sewed her mind;","But, lovely niece, that mean is cut from thee;","A craftier Tereus, cousin, hast thou met,","And he hath cut those pretty fingers off","That could have better sewed than Philomel.","O, had the monster seen those lily hands","Tremble like aspen leaves upon a lute,","And make the silken strings delight to kiss them,","He would not then have touched them for his life.","Or had he heard the heavenly harmony","Which that sweet tongue hath made,","He would have dropped his knife, and fell asleep,","As Cerberus at the Thracian poet's feet.","Come, let us go, and make thy father blind,","For such a sight will blind a father's eye.","One hour's storm will drown the fragrant meads;","What will whole months of tears thy father's eyes?","Do not draw back, for we will mourn with thee.","O, could our mourning ease thy misery!"],"id":300,"m":"Who is this? My niece, running away so fast? Niece, a word. Where is your husband? If I am dreaming, I would give all my wealth to wake up! If I am awake, may some planet strike me down so that I may sleep for ever! Speak, gentle niece: what harsh, cruel hands have lopped and hacked your body and stripped it of its two branches, those sweet ornaments whose embracing shade kings have longed to sleep in, and could not win so great a happiness as half your love? Why don't you speak to me? Alas, a crimson river of warm blood, like a bubbling fountain stirred by the wind, rises and falls between your rosy lips, coming and going with your sweet breath. Surely some Tereus has raped you, and cut out your tongue so that you could not expose him. Ah, now you turn your face away in shame. And in spite of all this loss of blood, pouring as if from a fountain with three spouts, your cheeks still look as red as the sun's face blushing when it meets a cloud. Shall I speak for you? Shall I say that is what happened? O, if only I knew your heart, and knew the beast, so that I could rage at him to ease my mind. Hidden sorrow, like a closed oven, burns the heart where it is to cinders. Fair Philomela only lost her tongue, and she stitched her story into a laborious piece of needlework. But, lovely niece, that way has been cut off from you. You have met a craftier Tereus, niece, and he has cut off those pretty fingers that could have sewn better than Philomela. O, if the monster had seen those lily-white hands tremble like aspen leaves on a lute and make the silk strings delight to kiss them, he would not have touched them to save his life. Or if he had heard the heavenly music that sweet tongue made, he would have dropped his knife and fallen asleep, as Cerberus did at the feet of Orpheus, the Thracian poet. Come, let us go and make your father blind, for such a sight will blind a father's eyes. One hour's storm will flood the sweet meadows: what will months of tears do to your father's eyes? Don't draw back, for we will mourn with you. O, if only our mourning could ease your misery!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":301},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. A street","id":302},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Judges and Senators, with Titus' two sons Quintus and Martius bound, passing on the stage to the place of execution, and Titus going before, pleading.","id":303},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Hear me, grave fathers; noble tribunes, stay!","For pity of mine age, whose youth was spent","In dangerous wars whilst you securely slept;","For all my blood in Rome's great quarrel shed,","For all the frosty nights that I have watched,","And for these bitter tears, which now you see","Filling the aged wrinkles in my cheeks,","Be pitiful to my condemned sons,","Whose souls are not corrupted as 'tis thought.","For two and twenty sons I never wept,","Because they died in honour's lofty bed."],"id":304,"m":"Hear me, wise fathers! Noble tribunes, stop! Out of pity for my age, whose youth was spent in dangerous wars while you slept safely; for all the blood I have shed in Rome's great cause; for all the frosty nights I have kept watch; and for these bitter tears that you now see filling the old wrinkles in my cheeks: have mercy on my condemned sons, whose souls are not as corrupt as people think. I never wept for twenty-two sons, because they died in honour's noble bed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Andronicus lieth down, and the Judges pass by him.","id":305},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt with the prisoners as Titus continues speaking.","id":306},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["For these, tribunes, in the dust I write","My heart's deep languor and my soul's sad tears.","Let my tears staunch the earth's dry appetite;","My sons' sweet blood will make it shame and blush.","O earth, I will befriend thee more with rain","That shall distil from these two ancient urns,","Than youthful April shall with all his showers.","In summer's drought I'll drop upon thee still;","In winter with warm tears I'll melt the snow,","And keep eternal spring-time on thy face,","So thou refuse to drink my dear sons' blood."],"id":307,"m":"For these sons, tribunes, I write my heart's deep grief and my soul's sad tears in the dust. Let my tears satisfy the earth's dry hunger; my sons' sweet blood would make it blush with shame. O earth, I will water you more with rain dripping from these two old eyes than young April with all his showers. In summer's drought I'll keep dripping on you. In winter I'll melt the snow with warm tears and keep your face in eternal spring, if only you refuse to drink my dear sons' blood."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius with his weapon drawn.","id":308},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["O reverend tribunes! O gentle aged men!","Unbind my sons, reverse the doom of death;","And let me say, that never wept before,","My tears are now prevailing orators."],"id":309,"m":"O respected tribunes! O kind old men! Unbind my sons, overturn the sentence of death, and let me, who never wept before, say that my tears are now persuasive speakers."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["O noble father, you lament in vain.","The tribunes hear you not, no man is by;","And you recount your sorrows to a stone."],"id":310,"m":"O noble father, you are lamenting for nothing. The tribunes can't hear you. No one is here, and you are telling your sorrows to a stone."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Ah, Lucius, for thy brothers let me plead.","Grave tribunes, once more I entreat of you—"],"id":311,"m":"Ah, Lucius, let me plead for your brothers. Wise tribunes, once more I beg you—"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["My gracious lord, no tribune hears you speak."],"id":312,"m":"My gracious lord, no tribune hears you speaking."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Why, 'tis no matter, man. If they did hear,","They would not mark me; if they did mark,","They would not pity me, yet plead I must,","And bootless unto them.","Therefore I tell my sorrows to the stones,","Who, though they cannot answer my distress,","Yet in some sort they are better than the tribunes,","For that they will not intercept my tale.","When I do weep, they humbly at my feet","Receive my tears, and seem to weep with me;","And were they but attired in grave weeds,","Rome could afford no tribunes like to these.","A stone is soft as wax, tribunes more hard than stones;","A stone is silent, and offendeth not,","And tribunes with their tongues doom men to death.","But wherefore stand'st thou with thy weapon drawn?"],"id":313,"m":"Why, it doesn't matter, man. If they did hear, they would not take notice of me. If they took notice, they would not pity me. Yet I must plead, uselessly, to them. So I tell my sorrows to the stones. Though they cannot answer my distress, they are in some ways better than the tribunes, because they will not interrupt my story. When I weep, they humbly take my tears at my feet and seem to weep with me. If only they were dressed in solemn robes, Rome could not provide tribunes as good as these. A stone is soft as wax; tribunes are harder than stones. A stone is silent and does no harm, and tribunes condemn men to death with their tongues. But why are you standing there with your sword drawn?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["To rescue my two brothers from their death;","For which attempt the judges have pronounced","My everlasting doom of banishment."],"id":314,"m":"To rescue my two brothers from death. For trying it, the judges have sentenced me to banishment for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["O happy man, they have befriended thee.","Why, foolish Lucius, dost thou not perceive","That Rome is but a wilderness of tigers?","Tigers must prey, and Rome affords no prey","But me and mine. How happy art thou then,","From these devourers to be banished!","But who comes with our brother Marcus here?"],"id":315,"m":"O lucky man, they have done you a favour. Why, foolish Lucius, can't you see that Rome is nothing but a wilderness of tigers? Tigers must hunt, and Rome has no prey for them but me and my family. How lucky you are, then, to be banished from these devourers! But who is coming here with our brother Marcus?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Marcus with Lavinia.","id":316},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Titus, prepare thy aged eyes to weep;","Or if not so, thy noble heart to break.","I bring consuming sorrow to thine age."],"id":317,"m":"Titus, get your old eyes ready to weep, or, if not, your noble heart to break. I am bringing a sorrow that will consume your old age."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Will it consume me? Let me see it then."],"id":318,"m":"Will it consume me? Then let me see it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["This was thy daughter."],"id":319,"m":"This was your daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Why, Marcus, so she is."],"id":320,"m":"Why, Marcus, so she is."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Ay me, this object kills me!"],"id":321,"m":"Oh no, this sight is killing me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Faint-hearted boy, arise, and look upon her.","Speak, Lavinia, what accursed hand","Hath made thee handless in thy father's sight?","What fool hath added water to the sea,","Or brought a faggot to bright-burning Troy?","My grief was at the height before thou cam'st,","And now like Nilus it disdaineth bounds.","Give me a sword, I'll chop off my hands too;","For they have fought for Rome, and all in vain;","And they have nursed this woe in feeding life;","In bootless prayer have they been held up,","And they have served me to effectless use.","Now all the service I require of them","Is that the one will help to cut the other.","'Tis well, Lavinia, that thou hast no hands,","For hands to do Rome service is but vain."],"id":322,"m":"Faint-hearted boy, get up and look at her. Speak, Lavinia: what cursed hand has made you handless in your father's sight? What fool has added water to the sea, or brought a bundle of sticks to Troy as it burned? My grief was at its height before you came, and now, like the Nile, it overflows its banks. Give me a sword. I'll chop off my hands too, for they have fought for Rome, all for nothing. They kept me alive to nurse this misery. They have been raised in useless prayer, and they have served me to no purpose. Now the only service I want from them is for one to help cut off the other. It is just as well, Lavinia, that you have no hands, for hands that serve Rome are useless."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Speak, gentle sister, who hath martyred thee?"],"id":323,"m":"Speak, gentle sister, who has tortured you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["O, that delightful engine of her thoughts,","That blabbed them with such pleasing eloquence,","Is torn from forth that pretty hollow cage,","Where, like a sweet melodious bird, it sung","Sweet varied notes, enchanting every ear."],"id":324,"m":"O, that delightful instrument of her thoughts, which told them with such pleasing eloquence, has been torn out of that pretty hollow cage where it sang sweet, varied notes like a sweet singing bird, enchanting every ear."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["O, say thou for her, who hath done this deed?"],"id":325,"m":"O, you say it for her: who has done this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["O, thus I found her straying in the park,","Seeking to hide herself, as doth the deer","That hath received some unrecuring wound."],"id":326,"m":"O, I found her like this, wandering in the park, trying to hide herself, like a deer that has been given an incurable wound."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["It was my dear, and he that wounded her","Hath hurt me more than had he killed me dead.","For now I stand as one upon a rock,","Environed with a wilderness of sea,","Who marks the waxing tide grow wave by wave,","Expecting ever when some envious surge","Will in his brinish bowels swallow him.","This way to death my wretched sons are gone;","Here stands my other son, a banished man,","And here my brother, weeping at my woes.","But that which gives my soul the greatest spurn","Is dear Lavinia, dearer than my soul.","Had I but seen thy picture in this plight","It would have madded me. What shall I do","Now I behold thy lively body so?","Thou hast no hands to wipe away thy tears,","Nor tongue to tell me who hath martyred thee.","Thy husband he is dead, and for his death","Thy brothers are condemned, and dead by this.","Look, Marcus! Ah, son Lucius, look on her!","When I did name her brothers, then fresh tears","Stood on her cheeks, as doth the honey-dew","Upon a gathered lily almost withered."],"id":327,"m":"She was my dear, and whoever wounded her has hurt me more than if he had killed me. For now I am like a man standing on a rock, surrounded by a wilderness of sea, who watches the rising tide grow wave by wave, always waiting for some spiteful wave to swallow him in its salty belly. This way my wretched sons have gone to their deaths. Here stands my other son, a banished man, and here my brother, weeping at my sorrows. But what wounds my soul most is dear Lavinia, dearer than my soul. If I had only seen a picture of you in this state, it would have driven me mad. What shall I do, now that I see your living body like this? You have no hands to wipe away your tears, no tongue to tell me who has tortured you. Your husband is dead, and for his death your brothers are condemned, and dead by now. Look, Marcus! Ah, son Lucius, look at her! When I mentioned her brothers, fresh tears stood on her cheeks, like honeydew on a picked lily that is almost withered."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Perchance she weeps because they killed her husband;","Perchance because she knows them innocent."],"id":328,"m":"Perhaps she is weeping because they killed her husband; perhaps because she knows they are innocent."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["If they did kill thy husband, then be joyful,","Because the law hath ta'en revenge on them.","No, no, they would not do so foul a deed;","Witness the sorrow that their sister makes.","Gentle Lavinia, let me kiss thy lips,","Or make some sign how I may do thee ease.","Shall thy good uncle, and thy brother Lucius,","And thou, and I, sit round about some fountain,","Looking all downwards to behold our cheeks","How they are stained, like meadows yet not dry,","With miry slime left on them by a flood?","And in the fountain shall we gaze so long","Till the fresh taste be taken from that clearness,","And made a brine-pit with our bitter tears?","Or shall we cut away our hands like thine?","Or shall we bite our tongues, and in dumb shows","Pass the remainder of our hateful days?","What shall we do? Let us that have our tongues","Plot some device of further misery,","To make us wondered at in time to come."],"id":329,"m":"If they did kill your husband, then be glad, because the law has taken revenge on them. No, no, they would never do such a foul deed: look at how their sister grieves. Gentle Lavinia, let me kiss your lips, or make some sign of how I can comfort you. Shall your good uncle and your brother Lucius and you and I sit round some fountain, all looking down to see how our cheeks are stained, like meadows not yet dry, with muddy slime left by a flood? And shall we stare into the fountain so long that its fresh taste is spoiled and our bitter tears turn it into a pool of brine? Or shall we cut off our hands like yours? Or bite off our tongues, and spend the rest of our hateful days in dumb show? What shall we do? Let those of us who still have tongues plan some further misery, to make people marvel at us in times to come."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Sweet father, cease your tears; for at your grief","See how my wretched sister sobs and weeps."],"id":330,"m":"Sweet father, stop crying, for look how my poor sister sobs and weeps at your grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Patience, dear niece. Good Titus, dry thine eyes."],"id":331,"m":"Be patient, dear niece. Good Titus, dry your eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Ah, Marcus, Marcus! Brother, well I wot","Thy napkin cannot drink a tear of mine,","For thou, poor man, hast drowned it with thine own."],"id":332,"m":"Ah, Marcus, Marcus! Brother, I know very well that your handkerchief cannot soak up a single tear of mine, because you, poor man, have drowned it with your own."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Ah, my Lavinia, I will wipe thy cheeks."],"id":333,"m":"Ah, my Lavinia, I will wipe your cheeks."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Mark, Marcus, mark! I understand her signs.","Had she a tongue to speak, now would she say","That to her brother which I said to thee.","His napkin, with his true tears all bewet,","Can do no service on her sorrowful cheeks.","O, what a sympathy of woe is this,","As far from help as limbo is from bliss."],"id":334,"m":"Look, Marcus, look! I understand her signs. If she had a tongue to speak, she would say to her brother now what I just said to you: his handkerchief, soaked with his own true tears, is no use on her sorrowful cheeks. O, what a sharing of grief this is, as far from help as limbo is from heaven."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aaron the Moor, alone.","id":335},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Titus Andronicus, my lord the emperor","Sends thee this word, that, if thou love thy sons,","Let Marcus, Lucius, or thyself, old Titus,","Or any one of you, chop off your hand","And send it to the king; he for the same","Will send thee hither both thy sons alive,","And that shall be the ransom for their fault."],"id":336,"m":"Titus Andronicus, my lord the emperor sends you this message: if you love your sons, let Marcus, Lucius or yourself, old Titus, or any one of you, chop off your hand and send it to the king. In return he will send both your sons back here alive, and that will be the ransom for their crime."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["O gracious emperor! O gentle Aaron!","Did ever raven sing so like a lark","That gives sweet tidings of the sun's uprise?","With all my heart I'll send the emperor my hand.","Good Aaron, wilt thou help to chop it off?"],"id":337,"m":"O gracious emperor! O kind Aaron! Did a raven ever sing so like a lark, bringing the sweet news of sunrise? With all my heart I'll send the emperor my hand. Good Aaron, will you help to chop it off?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Stay, father, for that noble hand of thine,","That hath thrown down so many enemies,","Shall not be sent. My hand will serve the turn.","My youth can better spare my blood than you;","And therefore mine shall save my brothers' lives."],"id":338,"m":"Wait, father, for that noble hand of yours, which has struck down so many enemies, shall not be sent. My hand will do the job. Being young, I can spare my blood better than you, and so mine shall save my brothers' lives."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Which of your hands hath not defended Rome,","And reared aloft the bloody battle-axe,","Writing destruction on the enemy's castle?","O, none of both but are of high desert.","My hand hath been but idle; let it serve","To ransom my two nephews from their death;","Then have I kept it to a worthy end."],"id":339,"m":"Which of your hands has not defended Rome and raised the bloody battle-axe high, writing destruction on the enemy's castle? O, both of you have hands that have earned great honour. My hand has done nothing. Let it serve to ransom my two nephews from death; then I will have kept it for a worthy purpose."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Nay, come, agree whose hand shall go along,","For fear they die before their pardon come."],"id":340,"m":"No, come, agree whose hand is going, in case they die before their pardon comes."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["My hand shall go."],"id":341,"m":"My hand shall go."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["By heaven, it shall not go!"],"id":342,"m":"By heaven, it shall not!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Sirs, strive no more. Such withered herbs as these","Are meet for plucking up, and therefore mine."],"id":343,"m":"Sirs, stop arguing. Withered weeds like these are the right ones to pull up, and so it must be mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Sweet father, if I shall be thought thy son,","Let me redeem my brothers both from death."],"id":344,"m":"Sweet father, if I am to be thought your son, let me save both my brothers from death."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["And for our father's sake and mother's care,","Now let me show a brother's love to thee."],"id":345,"m":"And for the sake of our father and our mother's care, let me show you a brother's love now."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Agree between you; I will spare my hand."],"id":346,"m":"Settle it between you. I will keep my hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Then I'll go fetch an axe."],"id":347,"m":"Then I'll go and fetch an axe."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["But I will use the axe."],"id":348,"m":"But I will be the one to use it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lucius and Marcus.","id":349},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Come hither, Aaron; I'll deceive them both.","Lend me thy hand, and I will give thee mine."],"id":350,"m":"Come here, Aaron. I'll trick them both. Lend me your hand, and I will give you mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["[Aside] If that be called deceit, I will be honest,","And never whilst I live deceive men so.","But I'll deceive you in another sort,","And that you'll say ere half an hour pass."],"id":351,"m":"(Aside) If that is called deceit, I will be honest, and never deceive men like that as long as I live. But I'll deceive you in another way, as you'll admit within half an hour."},{"t":"sd","text":"He cuts off Titus's hand.","id":352},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius and Marcus again.","id":353},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Now stay your strife. What shall be is dispatched.","Good Aaron, give his majesty my hand.","Tell him it was a hand that warded him","From thousand dangers, bid him bury it;","More hath it merited, that let it have.","As for my sons, say I account of them","As jewels purchased at an easy price;","And yet dear too, because I bought mine own."],"id":354,"m":"Now stop your quarrel. What had to be done is done. Good Aaron, give his majesty my hand. Tell him it was a hand that protected him from a thousand dangers, and ask him to bury it. It has deserved more; let it have that much. As for my sons, tell him I count them as jewels bought cheaply, and yet dearly too, because I have only bought back what was mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["I go, Andronicus; and for thy hand","Look by and by to have thy sons with thee.","[Aside] Their heads, I mean. O, how this villainy","Doth fat me with the very thoughts of it!","Let fools do good, and fair men call for grace,","Aaron will have his soul black like his face."],"id":355,"m":"I'm going, Andronicus, and in return for your hand expect to have your sons with you soon. (Aside) Their heads, I mean. O, how this villainy fattens me just to think of it! Let fools do good, and fair-skinned men pray for grace: Aaron will have his soul as black as his face."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":356},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["O, here I lift this one hand up to heaven,","And bow this feeble ruin to the earth.","If any power pities wretched tears,","To that I call! [To Lavinia] What, wouldst thou kneel with me?","Do, then, dear heart; for heaven shall hear our prayers,","Or with our sighs we'll breathe the welkin dim,","And stain the sun with fog, as sometime clouds","When they do hug him in their melting bosoms."],"id":357,"m":"O, here I lift this one hand up to heaven, and bow this feeble ruin of a body to the ground. If any power pities wretched tears, I call on it! (To Lavinia) What, would you kneel with me? Do, then, dear heart, for heaven shall hear our prayers, or we'll darken the sky with our sighs and smear the sun with fog, as clouds sometimes do when they hug him in their melting arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["O brother, speak with possibility,","And do not break into these deep extremes."],"id":358,"m":"O brother, talk of what is possible, and do not burst into these extremes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Is not my sorrow deep, having no bottom?","Then be my passions bottomless with them."],"id":359,"m":"Isn't my sorrow deep, when it has no bottom? Then let my passions be as bottomless."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["But yet let reason govern thy lament."],"id":360,"m":"But still, let reason rule your grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["If there were reason for these miseries,","Then into limits could I bind my woes.","When heaven doth weep, doth not the earth o'erflow?","If the winds rage, doth not the sea wax mad,","Threatening the welkin with his big-swol'n face?","And wilt thou have a reason for this coil?","I am the sea. Hark how her sighs doth flow!","She is the weeping welkin, I the earth.","Then must my sea be moved with her sighs;","Then must my earth with her continual tears","Become a deluge, overflowed and drowned;","For why my bowels cannot hide her woes,","But like a drunkard must I vomit them.","Then give me leave, for losers will have leave","To ease their stomachs with their bitter tongues."],"id":361,"m":"If there were any reason for these miseries, then I could keep my sorrows within limits. When heaven weeps, doesn't the earth flood? If the winds rage, doesn't the sea go mad, threatening the sky with its great swollen face? And do you want a reason for this turmoil? I am the sea. Listen to how her sighs blow! She is the weeping sky, and I am the earth. So my sea must be stirred by her sighs. So my earth must be flooded and drowned by her endless tears. For my insides cannot hold her sorrows, but must vomit them up like a drunkard. So give me licence, for losers are allowed to ease their stomachs with bitter words."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger with two heads and a hand.","id":362},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Worthy Andronicus, ill art thou repaid","For that good hand thou sent'st the emperor.","Here are the heads of thy two noble sons,","And here's thy hand, in scorn to thee sent back.","Thy grief their sports, thy resolution mocked;","That woe is me to think upon thy woes,","More than remembrance of my father's death."],"id":363,"m":"Worthy Andronicus, you are badly repaid for the good hand you sent the emperor. Here are the heads of your two noble sons, and here is your hand, sent back to you in scorn. Your grief is their entertainment, and your courage is mocked. It grieves me more to think of your sorrows than to remember my own father's death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":364},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Now let hot Etna cool in Sicily,","And be my heart an ever-burning hell!","These miseries are more than may be borne.","To weep with them that weep doth ease some deal,","But sorrow flouted at is double death."],"id":365,"m":"Now let hot Etna in Sicily grow cold, and let my heart be a hell that burns for ever! These miseries are more than anyone can bear. Weeping with those who weep gives some relief, but sorrow that is mocked is a double death."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Ah, that this sight should make so deep a wound,","And yet detested life not shrink thereat!","That ever death should let life bear his name,","Where life hath no more interest but to breathe!"],"id":366,"m":"Ah, that this sight should make so deep a wound, and yet hateful life does not shrink away from it! That death should ever let life go by his name, when life has no part left in it except breathing!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Lavinia kisses Titus.","id":367},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Alas, poor heart, that kiss is comfortless","As frozen water to a starved snake."],"id":368,"m":"Alas, poor heart, that kiss is as little comfort as frozen water to a starving snake."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["When will this fearful slumber have an end?"],"id":369,"m":"When will this terrible nightmare end?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Now farewell, flattery; die, Andronicus;","Thou dost not slumber. See thy two sons' heads,","Thy warlike hand, thy mangled daughter here;","Thy other banished son with this dear sight","Struck pale and bloodless; and thy brother, I,","Even like a stony image, cold and numb.","Ah, now no more will I control thy griefs.","Rent off thy silver hair, thy other hand","Gnawing with thy teeth; and be this dismal sight","The closing up of our most wretched eyes.","Now is a time to storm; why art thou still?"],"id":370,"m":"Now goodbye to comforting lies. Die, Andronicus: you are not dreaming. Look at your two sons' heads, your warrior's hand, your mutilated daughter here, your other son, banished, struck pale and bloodless by this dreadful sight, and your brother, me, cold and numb like a statue. Ah, I won't try to restrain your grief any more. Tear out your silver hair, gnaw your other hand with your teeth, and let this dreadful sight be the last thing our wretched eyes see before they close. Now is the time to rage. Why are you so quiet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Ha, ha, ha!"],"id":371,"m":"Ha, ha, ha!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Why dost thou laugh? It fits not with this hour."],"id":372,"m":"Why are you laughing? It doesn't suit this moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Why, I have not another tear to shed.","Besides, this sorrow is an enemy,","And would usurp upon my watery eyes,","And make them blind with tributary tears.","Then which way shall I find Revenge's cave?","For these two heads do seem to speak to me,","And threat me I shall never come to bliss","Till all these mischiefs be returned again","Even in their throats that have committed them.","Come, let me see what task I have to do.","You heavy people, circle me about,","That I may turn me to each one of you,","And swear unto my soul to right your wrongs.","The vow is made. Come, brother, take a head;","And in this hand the other will I bear.","And, Lavinia, thou shalt be employed in these arms.","Bear thou my hand, sweet wench, between thy teeth.","As for thee, boy, go, get thee from my sight;","Thou art an exile, and thou must not stay.","Hie to the Goths, and raise an army there.","And if you love me, as I think you do,","Let's kiss and part, for we have much to do."],"id":373,"m":"Why, I haven't another tear to shed. Besides, this sorrow is an enemy that wants to take over my watering eyes and blind them with the tears they pay it. Then how shall I find the cave of Revenge? For these two heads seem to speak to me, and threaten that I shall never reach heaven until all these crimes are thrust back down the throats of those who committed them. Come, let me see what work I have to do. You sorrowing people, stand in a circle round me, so that I can turn to each of you and swear on my soul to right your wrongs. The vow is made. Come, brother, take a head, and I will carry the other in this hand. And, Lavinia, you shall have a job too. Carry my hand between your teeth, sweet girl. As for you, boy, go, get out of my sight. You are an exile, and you must not stay. Hurry to the Goths and raise an army there. And if you love me, as I think you do, let's kiss and part, for we have much to do."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Titus, Marcus and Lavinia.","id":374},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Farewell, Andronicus, my noble father,","The woefull'st man that ever lived in Rome.","Farewell, proud Rome, till Lucius come again;","He loves his pledges dearer than his life.","Farewell, Lavinia, my noble sister;","O, would thou wert as thou tofore hast been!","But now nor Lucius nor Lavinia lives","But in oblivion and hateful griefs.","If Lucius live, he will requite your wrongs,","And make proud Saturnine and his empress","Beg at the gates, like Tarquin and his queen.","Now will I to the Goths, and raise a power","To be revenged on Rome and Saturnine."],"id":375,"m":"Farewell, Andronicus, my noble father, the most miserable man who ever lived in Rome. Farewell, proud Rome, until Lucius comes again: he leaves behind those he loves more than his life. Farewell, Lavinia, my noble sister. O, if only you were as you used to be! But now neither Lucius nor Lavinia lives, except in being forgotten and in hateful grief. If Lucius lives, he will repay your wrongs, and make proud Saturnine and his empress beg at the gates, like Tarquin and his queen. Now I'll go to the Goths and raise an army to take revenge on Rome and Saturnine."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":376},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Rome. A Room in Titus's House. A banquet set out","id":377},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Titus Andronicus, Marcus, Lavinia and the boy Young Lucius.","id":378},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["So so; now sit; and look you eat no more","Than will preserve just so much strength in us","As will revenge these bitter woes of ours.","Marcus, unknit that sorrow-wreathen knot.","Thy niece and I, poor creatures, want our hands,","And cannot passionate our tenfold grief","With folded arms. This poor right hand of mine","Is left to tyrannize upon my breast;","Who when my heart, all mad with misery,","Beats in this hollow prison of my flesh,","Then thus I thump it down.","Thou map of woe, that thus dost talk in signs,","When thy poor heart beats with outrageous beating,","Thou canst not strike it thus to make it still.","Wound it with sighing, girl, kill it with groans;","Or get some little knife between thy teeth,","And just against thy heart make thou a hole,","That all the tears that thy poor eyes let fall","May run into that sink, and, soaking in,","Drown the lamenting fool in sea-salt tears."],"id":379,"m":"There, there. Now sit down, and make sure you eat no more than will keep just enough strength in us to avenge these bitter sorrows of ours. Marcus, undo that sad knot of folded arms. Your niece and I, poor creatures, have no hands, and cannot show our tenfold grief by folding our arms. This poor right hand of mine is left to beat cruelly on my breast, and when my heart, mad with misery, pounds in this hollow prison of my flesh, I thump it down like this. (To Lavinia) You picture of grief, who talk in signs: when your poor heart beats wildly, you cannot strike it like this to quieten it. Wound it with sighs, girl, kill it with groans. Or take some little knife between your teeth and make a hole just over your heart, so that all the tears your poor eyes shed may run into that drain and soak in, and drown the grieving fool in salt tears."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Fie, brother, fie! Teach her not thus to lay","Such violent hands upon her tender life."],"id":380,"m":"For shame, brother! Don't teach her to lay such violent hands on her own young life."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["How now! Has sorrow made thee dote already?","Why, Marcus, no man should be mad but I.","What violent hands can she lay on her life?","Ah, wherefore dost thou urge the name of hands,","To bid Æneas tell the tale twice o'er","How Troy was burnt and he made miserable?","O, handle not the theme, to talk of hands,","Lest we remember still that we have none.","Fie, fie, how frantically I square my talk,","As if we should forget we had no hands,","If Marcus did not name the word of hands!","Come, let's fall to; and, gentle girl, eat this.","Here is no drink! Hark, Marcus, what she says;","I can interpret all her martyred signs.","She says she drinks no other drink but tears,","Brewed with her sorrow, meshed upon her cheeks.","Speechless complainer, I will learn thy thought;","In thy dumb action will I be as perfect","As begging hermits in their holy prayers.","Thou shalt not sigh, nor hold thy stumps to heaven,","Nor wink, nor nod, nor kneel, nor make a sign,","But I of these will wrest an alphabet,","And by still practice learn to know thy meaning."],"id":381,"m":"What's this! Has sorrow made you senile already? Why, Marcus, no one should be mad but me. What violent hands can she lay on her life? Ah, why do you keep on about hands? It's like asking Aeneas to tell the story twice of how Troy was burnt and he was made miserable. O, don't touch the subject of hands, in case we keep remembering that we have none. Ugh, how madly I am shaping my words, as if we would forget we had no hands if Marcus did not say the word! Come, let's start eating. And, gentle girl, eat this. There's nothing to drink! Listen, Marcus, to what she says. I can interpret all her tortured signs. She says she drinks no drink but tears, brewed from her sorrow and mashed on her cheeks. Speechless complainer, I will learn your thoughts. I will be as perfect in your silent gestures as begging hermits are in their holy prayers. You shall not sigh, or hold your stumps up to heaven, or wink, nod, kneel or make a sign, but I will wring an alphabet out of it and by constant practice learn to know what you mean."},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["Good grandsire, leave these bitter deep laments.","Make my aunt merry with some pleasing tale."],"id":382,"m":"Good grandfather, stop these bitter, deep laments. Cheer my aunt up with some nice story."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Alas, the tender boy, in passion moved,","Doth weep to see his grandsire's heaviness."],"id":383,"m":"Alas, the tender boy is so moved that he weeps to see his grandfather's sadness."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Peace, tender sapling; thou art made of tears,","And tears will quickly melt thy life away."],"id":384,"m":"Quiet, tender young tree. You are made of tears, and tears will soon melt your life away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Marcus strikes the dish with a knife.","id":385},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["What dost thou strike at, Marcus, with thy knife?"],"id":386,"m":"What are you striking at with your knife, Marcus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["At that that I have killed, my lord, a fly."],"id":387,"m":"At something I have killed, my lord: a fly."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Out on thee, murderer! Thou kill'st my heart;","Mine eyes are cloyed with view of tyranny;","A deed of death done on the innocent","Becomes not Titus' brother. Get thee gone;","I see thou art not for my company."],"id":388,"m":"Shame on you, murderer! You are killing my heart. My eyes have seen too much cruelty. A deed of death done to the innocent does not suit Titus's brother. Go away. I can see you're not fit for my company."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Alas, my lord, I have but killed a fly."],"id":389,"m":"Alas, my lord, I have only killed a fly."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["\"But\"? How if that fly had a father and mother?","How would he hang his slender gilded wings","And buzz lamenting doings in the air!","Poor harmless fly,","That with his pretty buzzing melody,","Came here to make us merry, and thou hast killed him."],"id":390,"m":"'Only'? What if that fly had a father and mother? How he would droop his slender golden wings and buzz his grief in the air! Poor harmless fly, who came here with his pretty buzzing tune to cheer us up, and you have killed him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Pardon me, sir; 'twas a black ill-favoured fly,","Like to the empress' Moor; therefore I killed him."],"id":391,"m":"Forgive me, sir. It was a black, ugly fly, like the empress's Moor. That's why I killed him."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["O, O, O!","Then pardon me for reprehending thee,","For thou hast done a charitable deed.","Give me thy knife, I will insult on him,","Flattering myself as if it were the Moor","Come hither purposely to poison me.","There's for thyself, and that's for Tamora.","Ah, sirrah!","Yet, I think, we are not brought so low","But that between us we can kill a fly","That comes in likeness of a coal-black Moor."],"id":392,"m":"O, O, O! Then forgive me for scolding you, for you have done a good deed. Give me your knife. I will gloat over him, pretending to myself that it is the Moor, come here on purpose to poison me. There's one for you, and that's for Tamora. Ah, you rascal! And yet I think we are not brought so low that between us we can't kill a fly that comes looking like a coal-black Moor."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Alas, poor man, grief has so wrought on him,","He takes false shadows for true substances."],"id":393,"m":"Alas, poor man, grief has worked on him so much that he mistakes false shadows for real things."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Come, take away. Lavinia, go with me.","I'll to thy closet, and go read with thee","Sad stories chanced in the times of old.","Come, boy, and go with me. Thy sight is young,","And thou shalt read when mine begin to dazzle."],"id":394,"m":"Come, clear the table. Lavinia, come with me. I'll go to your room and read with you sad stories of things that happened long ago. Come, boy, come with me. Your eyes are young, and you shall read when mine start to blur."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":395},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. Before Titus's House","id":396},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Young Lucius and Lavinia running after him, and the boy flies from her with his books under his arm. Enter Titus and Marcus.","id":397},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["Help, grandsire, help! My aunt Lavinia","Follows me everywhere, I know not why.","Good uncle Marcus, see how swift she comes!","Alas, sweet aunt, I know not what you mean."],"id":398,"m":"Help, grandfather, help! My aunt Lavinia follows me everywhere, I don't know why. Good uncle Marcus, see how fast she's coming! Oh, sweet aunt, I don't know what you mean."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Stand by me, Lucius. Do not fear thine aunt."],"id":399,"m":"Stand by me, Lucius. Don't be afraid of your aunt."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["She loves thee, boy, too well to do thee harm."],"id":400,"m":"She loves you too well to hurt you, boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["Ay, when my father was in Rome she did."],"id":401,"m":"Yes, when my father was in Rome, she did."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["What means my niece Lavinia by these signs?"],"id":402,"m":"What does my niece Lavinia mean by these signs?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Fear her not, Lucius. Somewhat doth she mean.","See, Lucius, see how much she makes of thee.","Somewhither would she have thee go with her.","Ah, boy, Cornelia never with more care","Read to her sons than she hath read to thee","Sweet poetry and Tully's Orator."],"id":403,"m":"Don't be afraid of her, Lucius. She means something. See, Lucius, how fond she is of you. She wants you to go somewhere with her. Ah, boy, Cornelia never read to her sons with more care than Lavinia has read sweet poetry and Cicero's 'Orator' to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Canst thou not guess wherefore she plies thee thus?"],"id":404,"m":"Can't you guess why she keeps after you like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["My lord, I know not, I, nor can I guess,","Unless some fit or frenzy do possess her;","For I have heard my grandsire say full oft,","Extremity of griefs would make men mad;","And I have read that Hecuba of Troy","Ran mad for sorrow. That made me to fear,","Although, my lord, I know my noble aunt","Loves me as dear as e'er my mother did,","And would not, but in fury, fright my youth;","Which made me down to throw my books, and fly,","Causeless, perhaps. But pardon me, sweet aunt.","And, madam, if my uncle Marcus go,","I will most willingly attend your ladyship."],"id":405,"m":"My lord, I don't know, and I can't guess, unless some fit or frenzy has taken hold of her. For I have often heard my grandfather say that extreme grief can drive people mad, and I have read that Hecuba of Troy went mad with sorrow. That made me afraid. Though, my lord, I know my noble aunt loves me as dearly as my mother ever did, and would not frighten me, being young, unless she were mad. That made me throw down my books and run, without cause, perhaps. Forgive me, sweet aunt. And, madam, if my uncle Marcus will come, I will gladly go with your ladyship."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Lucius, I will."],"id":406,"m":"Lucius, I will."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lavinia turns over with her stumps the books which Lucius has let fall.","id":407},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["How now, Lavinia? Marcus, what means this?","Some book there is that she desires to see.","Which is it, girl, of these? Open them, boy.","But thou art deeper read and better skilled.","Come and take choice of all my library,","And so beguile thy sorrow, till the heavens","Reveal the damned contriver of this deed.","Why lifts she up her arms in sequence thus?"],"id":408,"m":"What is it, Lavinia? Marcus, what does this mean? There is some book here she wants to see. Which of these is it, girl? Open them, boy. (To Lavinia) But you are more widely read and more skilled. Come and choose from my whole library, and so distract yourself from your sorrow until heaven reveals the damned plotter of this deed. Why does she lift up her arms one after the other like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["I think she means that there were more than one","Confederate in the fact. Ay, more there was,","Or else to heaven she heaves them for revenge."],"id":409,"m":"I think she means that more than one person was involved in the crime. Yes, there was more than one. Or else she is raising them to heaven for revenge."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Lucius, what book is that she tosseth so?"],"id":410,"m":"Lucius, what book is that she is tossing about?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["Grandsire, 'tis Ovid's Metamorphosis.","My mother gave it me."],"id":411,"m":"Grandfather, it's Ovid's Metamorphoses. My mother gave it to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["For love of her that's gone,","Perhaps, she culled it from among the rest."],"id":412,"m":"Perhaps she picked it out from the rest out of love for her who is gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Soft! So busily she turns the leaves!","Help her! What would she find? Lavinia, shall I read?","This is the tragic tale of Philomel,","And treats of Tereus' treason and his rape;","And rape, I fear, was root of thy annoy."],"id":413,"m":"Wait! How busily she turns the pages! Help her! What does she want to find? Lavinia, shall I read? This is the tragic tale of Philomela, and it tells of Tereus's treachery and his rape. And rape, I fear, was the root of your suffering."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["See, brother, see! Note how she quotes the leaves."],"id":414,"m":"See, brother, see! Look how she points to the pages."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Lavinia, wert thou thus surprised, sweet girl,","Ravished and wronged, as Philomela was,","Forced in the ruthless, vast, and gloomy woods?","See, see!","Ay, such a place there is where we did hunt,—","O, had we never, never hunted there!—","Patterned by that the poet here describes,","By nature made for murders and for rapes."],"id":415,"m":"Lavinia, were you caught like this, sweet girl, raped and wronged as Philomela was, taken by force in the pitiless, vast and gloomy woods? Look, look! Yes, there is such a place where we went hunting (O, if only we had never, never hunted there!), just like the one the poet describes here, made by nature for murders and rapes."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["O, why should nature build so foul a den,","Unless the gods delight in tragedies?"],"id":416,"m":"O, why would nature build such a foul den, unless the gods enjoy tragedies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Give signs, sweet girl, for here are none but friends,","What Roman lord it was durst do the deed.","Or slunk not Saturnine, as Tarquin erst,","That left the camp to sin in Lucrece' bed?"],"id":417,"m":"Sweet girl, make signs to tell us, for there are only friends here, which Roman lord it was who dared to do this. Or did Saturnine creep off, as Tarquin once did when he left the camp to sin in Lucrece's bed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Sit down, sweet niece. Brother, sit down by me.","Apollo, Pallas, Jove, or Mercury,","Inspire me, that I may this treason find!","My lord, look here. Look here, Lavinia.","This sandy plot is plain; guide, if thou canst,","This after me. I have writ my name"],"id":418,"m":"Sit down, sweet niece. Brother, sit down beside me. Apollo, Pallas, Jove or Mercury, inspire me so that I can uncover this treachery! My lord, look here. Look here, Lavinia. This patch of sand is smooth. Copy this after me, if you can. I have written my name"},{"t":"sd","text":"He writes his name with his staff and guides it with feet and mouth.","id":419},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","cont":1,"lines":["Without the help of any hand at all.","Cursed be that heart that forced us to this shift!","Write thou, good niece, and here display at last","What God will have discovered for revenge.","Heaven guide thy pen to print thy sorrows plain,","That we may know the traitors and the truth!"],"id":420,"m":"without the help of any hand at all. Cursed be the heart that forced us to this trick! Write, good niece, and show here at last what God wants revealed for revenge. May heaven guide your pen to write your sorrows plainly, so that we may know the traitors and the truth!"},{"t":"sd","text":"She takes the staff in her mouth, and guides it with her stumps and writes.","id":421},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","cont":1,"lines":["O, do ye read, my lord, what she hath writ?"],"id":422,"m":"O, do you read, my lord, what she has written?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["\"Stuprum. Chiron. Demetrius.\""],"id":423,"m":"'Rape. Chiron. Demetrius.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["What, what! The lustful sons of Tamora","Performers of this heinous bloody deed?"],"id":424,"m":"What, what! Tamora's lustful sons did this terrible, bloody deed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Magni Dominator poli,","Tam lentus audis scelera, tam lentus vides?"],"id":425,"m":"Great ruler of the heavens, are you so slow to hear crimes, so slow to see them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["O, calm thee, gentle lord, although I know","There is enough written upon this earth","To stir a mutiny in the mildest thoughts","And arm the minds of infants to exclaims.","My lord, kneel down with me; Lavinia, kneel;","And kneel, sweet boy, the Roman Hector's hope;","And swear with me, as, with the woeful fere","And father of that chaste dishonoured dame,","Lord Junius Brutus sware for Lucrece' rape,","That we will prosecute, by good advice","Mortal revenge upon these traitorous Goths,","And see their blood, or die with this reproach."],"id":426,"m":"O, calm yourself, gentle lord, though I know there is enough written on this ground to start a rebellion in the mildest mind and arm the minds of babies with outcries. My lord, kneel down with me. Lavinia, kneel. And kneel, sweet boy, the hope of Rome's Hector. And swear with me, as Lord Junius Brutus swore with the grieving husband and the father of that chaste, dishonoured lady after the rape of Lucrece, that with careful planning we will pursue deadly revenge on these treacherous Goths, and see their blood, or die with this disgrace."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["'Tis sure enough, an you knew how.","But if you hunt these bear-whelps, then beware;","The dam will wake, and if she wind you once.","She's with the lion deeply still in league,","And lulls him whilst she playeth on her back,","And when he sleeps will she do what she list.","You are a young huntsman, Marcus; let alone;","And come, I will go get a leaf of brass,","And with a gad of steel will write these words,","And lay it by. The angry northern wind","Will blow these sands like Sibyl's leaves abroad,","And where's our lesson, then? Boy, what say you?"],"id":427,"m":"That's sure enough, if you knew how. But if you hunt these bear cubs, be careful: the mother will wake if she once gets your scent. She is still in close league with the lion, and lulls him while she lies on her back, and when he sleeps she does whatever she likes. You are a young huntsman, Marcus. Leave it alone. And come, I will get a sheet of brass and carve these words into it with a steel point, and keep it safe. The angry north wind will scatter these sands like the Sibyl's leaves, and where's our lesson then? Boy, what do you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["I say, my lord, that if I were a man,","Their mother's bedchamber should not be safe","For these base bondmen to the yoke of Rome."],"id":428,"m":"I say, my lord, that if I were a man, their mother's bedroom would not be safe for these low slaves of the Roman yoke."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Ay, that's my boy! Thy father hath full oft","For his ungrateful country done the like."],"id":429,"m":"Yes, that's my boy! Your father has often done the same for his ungrateful country."},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["And, uncle, so will I, an if I live."],"id":430,"m":"And, uncle, so will I, if I live."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Come, go with me into mine armoury.","Lucius, I'll fit thee; and withal, my boy,","Shall carry from me to the empress' sons","Presents that I intend to send them both.","Come, come; thou'lt do my message, wilt thou not?"],"id":431,"m":"Come with me into my armoury. Lucius, I'll equip you, and you shall also carry presents from me to the empress's sons, which I mean to send them both. Come, come, you'll carry my message, won't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["Ay, with my dagger in their bosoms, grandsire."],"id":432,"m":"Yes, with my dagger in their hearts, grandfather."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["No, boy, not so. I'll teach thee another course.","Lavinia, come. Marcus, look to my house.","Lucius and I'll go brave it at the court;","Ay, marry, will we, sir; and we'll be waited on."],"id":433,"m":"No, boy, not that. I'll teach you another way. Lavinia, come. Marcus, look after my house. Lucius and I will go and make a show at court. Yes, indeed we will, sir, and we'll be attended to."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Titus, Lavinia and Young Lucius.","id":434},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["O heavens, can you hear a good man groan","And not relent, or not compassion him?","Marcus, attend him in his ecstasy,","That hath more scars of sorrow in his heart","Than foemen's marks upon his battered shield,","But yet so just that he will not revenge.","Revenge ye heavens for old Andronicus!"],"id":435,"m":"O heavens, can you hear a good man groan and not soften or pity him? Marcus, look after him in his madness. He has more scars of sorrow in his heart than enemy marks on his battered shield, and yet he is so just that he will not take revenge. Heavens, take revenge for old Andronicus!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":436},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Rome. A Room in the Palace","id":437},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aaron, Chiron and Demetrius at one door, and at the other door Young Lucius and another, with a bundle of weapons and verses writ upon them.","id":438},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Demetrius, here's the son of Lucius;","He hath some message to deliver us."],"id":439,"m":"Demetrius, here's Lucius's son. He has some message to give us."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Ay, some mad message from his mad grandfather."],"id":440,"m":"Yes, some mad message from his mad grandfather."},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["My lords, with all the humbleness I may,","I greet your honours from Andronicus;","[Aside] And pray the Roman gods confound you both."],"id":441,"m":"My lords, with all the humility I can, I greet your honours from Andronicus. (Aside) And I pray the Roman gods destroy you both."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Gramercy, lovely Lucius. What's the news?"],"id":442,"m":"Thank you, lovely Lucius. What's the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["[Aside] That you are both deciphered, that's the news,","For villains marked with rape. [Aloud] May it please you,","My grandsire, well advised, hath sent by me","The goodliest weapons of his armoury","To gratify your honourable youth,","The hope of Rome; for so he bid me say;","And so I do, and with his gifts present","Your lordships, that, whenever you have need,","You may be armed and appointed well.","And so I leave you both, [Aside] like bloody villains."],"id":443,"m":"(Aside) That you have both been found out as villains branded with rape: that's the news. (Aloud) If you please, my grandfather, after careful thought, has sent by me the finest weapons in his armoury as a present for your honourable youth, the hope of Rome. That's what he told me to say, and so I do, and I present your lordships with his gifts, so that whenever you need to, you may be well armed and equipped. And so I leave you both, (Aside) like bloody villains."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Young Lucius and Attendant.","id":444},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["What's here? A scroll; and written round about?","Let's see:","[Reads] Integer vitae, scelerisque purus,","Non eget Mauri iaculis, nec arcu."],"id":445,"m":"What's this? A scroll, with writing all round it? Let's see. (Reads) 'The man of upright life, free from crime, needs no Moorish javelins or bow.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["O, 'tis a verse in Horace; I know it well.","I read it in the grammar long ago."],"id":446,"m":"O, it's a verse from Horace. I know it well. I read it in my Latin grammar long ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Ay, just; a verse in Horace; right, you have it.","[Aside] Now, what a thing it is to be an ass!","Here's no sound jest! The old man hath found their guilt,","And sends them weapons wrapped about with lines,","That wound, beyond their feeling, to the quick.","But were our witty empress well afoot,","She would applaud Andronicus' conceit.","But let her rest in her unrest awhile.—","And now, young lords, was't not a happy star","Led us to Rome, strangers, and more than so,","Captives, to be advanced to this height?","It did me good before the palace gate","To brave the tribune in his brother's hearing."],"id":447,"m":"Yes, exactly, a verse from Horace. Right, you've got it. (Aside) Now, what a thing it is to be an ass! This is no joke! The old man has found out their guilt, and sends them weapons wrapped in verses that cut them to the quick without their feeling it. If our clever empress were up and about, she would applaud Andronicus's idea. But let her rest in her restlessness for a while. (To them) And now, young lords, wasn't it a lucky star that led us to Rome as strangers, and worse, as captives, to be raised to this height? It did me good to defy the tribune in front of the palace gate, where his brother could hear."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["But me more good to see so great a lord","Basely insinuate and send us gifts."],"id":448,"m":"But it did me more good to see so great a lord crawl humbly and send us gifts."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Had he not reason, Lord Demetrius?","Did you not use his daughter very friendly?"],"id":449,"m":"Hadn't he reason to, Lord Demetrius? Didn't you treat his daughter in a very friendly way?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["I would we had a thousand Roman dames","At such a bay, by turn to serve our lust."],"id":450,"m":"I wish we had a thousand Roman ladies cornered like that, to take turns serving our lust."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["A charitable wish, and full of love."],"id":451,"m":"A charitable wish, and full of love."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Here lacks but your mother for to say amen."],"id":452,"m":"All we need now is your mother to say amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["And that would she for twenty thousand more."],"id":453,"m":"And she would, for twenty thousand more."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Come, let us go and pray to all the gods","For our beloved mother in her pains."],"id":454,"m":"Come, let us go and pray to all the gods for our beloved mother in her labour pains."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["[Aside] Pray to the devils; the gods have given us over."],"id":455,"m":"(Aside) Pray to the devils: the gods have given up on us."},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets sound.","id":456},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Why do the emperor's trumpets flourish thus?"],"id":457,"m":"Why are the emperor's trumpets sounding like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Belike for joy the emperor hath a son."],"id":458,"m":"Probably for joy that the emperor has a son."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Soft, who comes here?"],"id":459,"m":"Wait, who is this?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Nurse with a blackamoor Child in her arms.","id":460},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["Good morrow, lords.","O, tell me, did you see Aaron the Moor?"],"id":461,"m":"Good morning, lords. O, tell me, have you seen Aaron the Moor?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Well, more or less, or ne'er a whit at all,","Here Aaron is; and what with Aaron now?"],"id":462,"m":"Well, more or less, or not a bit at all, here Aaron is. And what about Aaron now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["O gentle Aaron, we are all undone!","Now help, or woe betide thee evermore!"],"id":463,"m":"O gentle Aaron, we are all ruined! Help now, or misery will fall on you for ever!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Why, what a caterwauling dost thou keep!","What dost thou wrap and fumble in thy arms?"],"id":464,"m":"Why, what a caterwauling you are making! What's that you are wrapping and fumbling in your arms?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["O, that which I would hide from heaven's eye,","Our empress' shame and stately Rome's disgrace.","She is delivered, lords, she is delivered."],"id":465,"m":"O, something I would hide from heaven's eye: our empress's shame and noble Rome's disgrace. She has been delivered, lords, she has been delivered."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["To whom?"],"id":466,"m":"Delivered to whom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["I mean, she's brought a-bed."],"id":467,"m":"I mean she has given birth."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Well, God give her good rest! What hath he sent her?"],"id":468,"m":"Well, God give her a good rest! What has he sent her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["A devil."],"id":469,"m":"A devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Why, then she is the devil's dam. A joyful issue."],"id":470,"m":"Why, then she is the devil's mother. A happy birth."},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["A joyless, dismal, black, and sorrowful issue.","Here is the babe, as loathsome as a toad","Amongst the fair-faced breeders of our clime.","The empress sends it thee, thy stamp, thy seal,","And bids thee christen it with thy dagger's point."],"id":471,"m":"A joyless, dreadful, black and sorrowful birth. Here is the baby, as loathsome as a toad among the fair-faced children of our land. The empress sends it to you, your stamp, your seal, and tells you to christen it with the point of your dagger."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Zounds, ye whore, is black so base a hue?","Sweet blowse, you are a beauteous blossom sure."],"id":472,"m":"God's wounds, you whore, is black such a low colour? (To the baby) Sweet chubby-cheeks, you are a beautiful flower for sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Villain, what hast thou done?"],"id":473,"m":"Villain, what have you done?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["That which thou canst not undo."],"id":474,"m":"Something you can't undo."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Thou hast undone our mother."],"id":475,"m":"You have ruined our mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Villain, I have done thy mother."],"id":476,"m":"Villain, I have done your mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["And therein, hellish dog, thou hast undone her.","Woe to her chance, and damned her loathed choice!","Accursed the offspring of so foul a fiend!"],"id":477,"m":"And by doing that, you hellish dog, you have ruined her. Curse her luck, and damn her hateful choice! Cursed be the child of so foul a devil!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["It shall not live."],"id":478,"m":"It shall not live."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["It shall not die."],"id":479,"m":"It shall not die."},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["Aaron, it must; the mother wills it so."],"id":480,"m":"Aaron, it must. Its mother wants it so."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["What, must it, nurse? Then let no man but I","Do execution on my flesh and blood."],"id":481,"m":"What, must it, nurse? Then let no one but me put my own flesh and blood to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["I'll broach the tadpole on my rapier's point.","Nurse, give it me; my sword shall soon dispatch it."],"id":482,"m":"I'll spit the tadpole on the point of my sword. Nurse, give it to me. My sword will soon finish it off."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Sooner this sword shall plough thy bowels up."],"id":483,"m":"This sword will plough up your guts first."},{"t":"sd","text":"Taking the baby.","id":484},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","cont":1,"lines":["Stay, murderous villains, will you kill your brother?","Now, by the burning tapers of the sky","That shone so brightly when this boy was got,","He dies upon my scimitar's sharp point","That touches this my first-born son and heir.","I tell you, younglings, not Enceladus,","With all his threatening band of Typhon's brood,","Nor great Alcides, nor the god of war,","Shall seize this prey out of his father's hands.","What, what, ye sanguine, shallow-hearted boys!","Ye white-limed walls, ye alehouse-painted signs!","Coal-black is better than another hue","In that it scorns to bear another hue;","For all the water in the ocean","Can never turn the swan's black legs to white,","Although she lave them hourly in the flood.","Tell the empress from me, I am of age","To keep mine own, excuse it how she can."],"id":485,"m":"Stop, murderous villains, would you kill your brother? Now, by the burning candles of the sky that shone so brightly when this boy was conceived, whoever touches my first-born son and heir dies on the sharp point of my scimitar. I tell you, youngsters, not Enceladus with all his threatening gang of giants, not great Hercules, not the god of war himself, shall snatch this prey out of his father's hands. What, what, you red-faced, shallow-hearted boys! You whitewashed walls, you painted alehouse signs! Coal-black is better than any other colour, because it refuses to take on any other colour. For all the water in the ocean can never turn the swan's black legs white, though she washes them in the waves every hour. Tell the empress from me that I am old enough to keep what is mine. Let her explain it however she can."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Wilt thou betray thy noble mistress thus?"],"id":486,"m":"Will you betray your noble mistress like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["My mistress is my mistress; this my self;","The vigour and the picture of my youth.","This before all the world do I prefer;","This maugre all the world will I keep safe,","Or some of you shall smoke for it in Rome."],"id":487,"m":"My mistress is my mistress; this is myself, the strength and image of my youth. I put this before all the world. I will keep this safe in spite of all the world, or some of you will burn for it in Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["By this our mother is for ever shamed."],"id":488,"m":"Our mother is shamed for ever by this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Rome will despise her for this foul escape."],"id":489,"m":"Rome will despise her for this foul slip."},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["The emperor in his rage will doom her death."],"id":490,"m":"The emperor in his rage will sentence her to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["I blush to think upon this ignomy."],"id":491,"m":"I blush to think of this disgrace."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Why, there's the privilege your beauty bears.","Fie, treacherous hue, that will betray with blushing","The close enacts and counsels of thy heart!","Here's a young lad framed of another leer.","Look how the black slave smiles upon the father,","As who should say \"Old lad, I am thine own.\"","He is your brother, lords, sensibly fed","Of that self blood that first gave life to you;","And from your womb where you imprisoned were","He is enfranchised and come to light.","Nay, he is your brother by the surer side,","Although my seal be stamped in his face."],"id":492,"m":"Why, that's the privilege your fair skin gives you. Shame on that treacherous colour, which betrays the secret deeds and thoughts of your heart by blushing! Here's a young lad of a different complexion. Look how the black rascal smiles at his father, as if to say, 'Old lad, I am your own.' He is your brother, lords, plainly fed by the same blood that first gave you life, and he has been set free from the same womb where you were imprisoned and has come into the light. Indeed, he is your brother on the surer side, his mother's, though my seal is stamped on his face."},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["Aaron, what shall I say unto the empress?"],"id":493,"m":"Aaron, what shall I tell the empress?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Advise thee, Aaron, what is to be done,","And we will all subscribe to thy advice.","Save thou the child, so we may all be safe."],"id":494,"m":"Think, Aaron, what must be done, and we will all agree to your advice. Save the child, as long as we can all be safe."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Then sit we down, and let us all consult.","My son and I will have the wind of you.","Keep there. Now talk at pleasure of your safety."],"id":495,"m":"Then let's sit down and all discuss it. My son and I will keep downwind of you. Stay there. Now talk as you please about your safety."},{"t":"sd","text":"They sit.","id":496},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["How many women saw this child of his?"],"id":497,"m":"How many women saw this child of his?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Why, so, brave lords! When we join in league,","I am a lamb; but if you brave the Moor,","The chafed boar, the mountain lioness,","The ocean swells not so as Aaron storms.","But say again, how many saw the child?"],"id":498,"m":"Why, that's the way, brave lords! When we join together, I am a lamb. But if you defy the Moor, the angry boar, the mountain lioness and the swelling ocean do not rage as Aaron does. But tell me again, how many saw the child?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NURSE","lines":["Cornelia the midwife and myself,","And no one else but the delivered empress."],"id":499,"m":"Cornelia the midwife and I, and no one else except the empress who gave birth."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["The empress, the midwife, and yourself.","Two may keep counsel when the third's away.","Go to the empress; tell her this I said."],"id":500,"m":"The empress, the midwife and yourself. Two can keep a secret when the third is gone. Go to the empress, and tell her I said this."},{"t":"sd","text":"He kills her.","id":501},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","cont":1,"lines":["\"Wheak, wheak!\" So cries a pig prepared to the spit."],"id":502,"m":"'Wheak, wheak!' That's how a pig squeals when it's put on the spit."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["What mean'st thou, Aaron? Wherefore didst thou this?"],"id":503,"m":"What do you mean, Aaron? Why did you do this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["O Lord, sir, 'tis a deed of policy.","Shall she live to betray this guilt of ours,","A long-tongued babbling gossip? No, lords, no.","And now be it known to you my full intent.","Not far, one Muliteus lives, my countryman;","His wife but yesternight was brought to bed.","His child is like to her, fair as you are.","Go pack with him, and give the mother gold,","And tell them both the circumstance of all,","And how by this their child shall be advanced,","And be received for the emperor's heir,","And substituted in the place of mine,","To calm this tempest whirling in the court;","And let the emperor dandle him for his own.","Hark ye, lords; ye see I have given her physic,"],"id":504,"m":"O Lord, sir, it was a deed of good sense. Should she live to betray our guilt, a long-tongued, babbling gossip? No, lords, no. And now let me tell you my whole plan. Not far from here lives a countryman of mine called Muliteus. His wife gave birth only last night. Their child looks like her, as fair as you are. Go and do a deal with him. Give the mother gold, tell them both the whole story, and how their child will be raised up by this and accepted as the emperor's heir, put in the place of mine, to calm this storm whirling round the court. And let the emperor dandle him as his own. Listen, lords. You see I have given her medicine,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Indicating the Nurse.","id":505},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","cont":1,"lines":["And you must needs bestow her funeral;","The fields are near, and you are gallant grooms.","This done, see that you take no longer days,","But send the midwife presently to me.","The midwife and the nurse well made away,","Then let the ladies tattle what they please."],"id":506,"m":"and you must give her a funeral. The fields are near, and you are fine strong lads. When that's done, don't waste any time, but send the midwife to me at once. Once the midwife and the nurse are well out of the way, let the ladies gossip as much as they like."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Aaron, I see thou wilt not trust the air","With secrets."],"id":507,"m":"Aaron, I see you won't even trust the air with secrets."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["For this care of Tamora,","Herself and hers are highly bound to thee."],"id":508,"m":"For this care of Tamora, she and her family owe you a great deal."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Demetrius and Chiron, carrying the Nurse's body.","id":509},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Now to the Goths, as swift as swallow flies,","There to dispose this treasure in mine arms,","And secretly to greet the empress' friends.","Come on, you thick-lipped slave, I'll bear you hence;","For it is you that puts us to our shifts.","I'll make you feed on berries and on roots,","And feed on curds and whey, and suck the goat,","And cabin in a cave, and bring you up","To be a warrior and command a camp."],"id":510,"m":"Now to the Goths, as fast as a swallow flies, to take care of this treasure in my arms and secretly greet the empress's friends. Come on, you thick-lipped rascal, I'll carry you away, for it is you who forces us into these tricks. I'll make you live on berries and roots, on curds and whey, and suck at a goat, and live in a cave, and I'll bring you up to be a warrior and command an army."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":511},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Rome. A public Place","id":512},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Titus, old Marcus, his son Publius, Young Lucius, and other gentlemen with bows, and Titus bears the arrows with letters on the ends of them.","id":513},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Come, Marcus, come. Kinsmen, this is the way.","Sir boy, let me see your archery.","Look ye draw home enough, and 'tis there straight.","Terras Astraea reliquit.","Be you remembered, Marcus, she's gone, she's fled.","Sirs, take you to your tools. You, cousins, shall","Go sound the ocean and cast your nets;","Happily you may catch her in the sea;","Yet there's as little justice as at land.","No; Publius and Sempronius, you must do it;","'Tis you must dig with mattock and with spade,","And pierce the inmost centre of the earth.","Then, when you come to Pluto's region,","I pray you, deliver him this petition;","Tell him it is for justice and for aid,","And that it comes from old Andronicus,","Shaken with sorrows in ungrateful Rome.","Ah, Rome! Well, well, I made thee miserable","What time I threw the people's suffrages","On him that thus doth tyrannize o'er me.","Go, get you gone; and pray be careful all,","And leave you not a man-of-war unsearched.","This wicked emperor may have shipped her hence;","And, kinsmen, then we may go pipe for justice."],"id":514,"m":"Come, Marcus, come. Kinsmen, this is the way. Young sir, let me see your archery. Make sure you draw the bow back far enough, and it will go straight there. 'Astraea has left the earth.' Remember, Marcus, she's gone, she's fled. Sirs, take up your tools. You, kinsmen, go and sound the depths of the ocean and cast your nets. Perhaps you may catch her in the sea, though there is as little justice there as on land. No, Publius and Sempronius, you must do it. You must dig with pick and spade and bore into the very centre of the earth. Then, when you reach Pluto's kingdom, please give him this petition. Tell him it is for justice and help, and that it comes from old Andronicus, shaken with sorrows in ungrateful Rome. Ah, Rome! Well, well, I made you miserable when I threw the people's votes to the man who now tyrannises over me like this. Go, off you go, and please, all of you, be careful, and don't leave a warship unsearched. This wicked emperor may have shipped her away, and then, kinsmen, we can whistle for justice."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["O Publius, is not this a heavy case,","To see thy noble uncle thus distract?"],"id":515,"m":"O Publius, isn't this a sad case, to see your noble uncle so distracted?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUBLIUS","lines":["Therefore, my lords, it highly us concerns","By day and night to attend him carefully,","And feed his humour kindly as we may,","Till time beget some careful remedy."],"id":516,"m":"That is why, my lords, it is important for us to watch over him carefully day and night, and humour him as kindly as we can, until time brings some remedy for his cares."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Kinsmen, his sorrows are past remedy,","But . . . .","Join with the Goths, and with revengeful war","Take wreak on Rome for this ingratitude,","And vengeance on the traitor Saturnine."],"id":517,"m":"Kinsmen, his sorrows are past cure. But (a line is missing here) join with the Goths, and with a war of revenge punish Rome for this ingratitude, and take vengeance on the traitor Saturnine."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Publius, how now? How now, my masters?","What, have you met with her?"],"id":518,"m":"Publius, well? Well, my good men? What, have you found her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUBLIUS","lines":["No, my good lord; but Pluto sends you word,","If you will have Revenge from hell, you shall.","Marry, for Justice, she is so employed,","He thinks, with Jove in heaven, or somewhere else,","So that perforce you must needs stay a time."],"id":519,"m":"No, my good lord, but Pluto sends you word that if you want Revenge from hell, you shall have it. As for Justice, he thinks she is busy with Jove in heaven, or somewhere else, so you must wait a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["He doth me wrong to feed me with delays.","I'll dive into the burning lake below,","And pull her out of Acheron by the heels.","Marcus, we are but shrubs, no cedars we,","No big-boned men framed of the Cyclops' size;","But metal, Marcus, steel to the very back,","Yet wrung with wrongs more than our backs can bear;","And sith there's no justice in earth nor hell,","We will solicit heaven and move the gods","To send down Justice for to wreak our wrongs.","Come, to this gear. You are a good archer, Marcus."],"id":520,"m":"He wrongs me by fobbing me off with delays. I'll dive into the burning lake down there and pull her out of the river Acheron by the heels. Marcus, we are only shrubs, not cedars, not big-boned men built to the size of the Cyclops. But we are metal, Marcus, steel right through to the back, and yet weighed down with more wrongs than our backs can bear. And since there is no justice on earth or in hell, we will petition heaven and move the gods to send Justice down to avenge our wrongs. Come, to work. You are a good archer, Marcus."},{"t":"sd","text":"He gives them the arrows.","id":521},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["\"Ad Jovem,\" that's for you; here, \"Ad Apollinem\";","\"Ad Martem,\" that's for myself;","Here, boy, \"to Pallas\"; here, \"to Mercury\";","\"To Saturn,\" Caius, not to Saturnine;","You were as good to shoot against the wind.","To it, boy.—Marcus, loose when I bid.—","Of my word, I have written to effect;","There's not a god left unsolicited."],"id":522,"m":"'To Jove,' that's for you. Here, 'To Apollo.' 'To Mars,' that's for myself. Here, boy, 'To Pallas.' Here, 'To Mercury.' 'To Saturn,' Caius, not to Saturnine: you might as well shoot against the wind. Go on, boy. Marcus, let fly when I say. On my word, I have written to the purpose. There's not a god left unasked."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Kinsmen, shoot all your shafts into the court.","We will afflict the emperor in his pride."],"id":523,"m":"Kinsmen, shoot all your arrows into the court. We will torment the emperor in his pride."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Now, masters, draw. [They shoot] O, well said, Lucius!","Good boy, in Virgo's lap! Give it Pallas."],"id":524,"m":"Now, masters, draw your bows. (They shoot) O, well done, Lucius! Good boy, right in Virgo's lap! Give it to Pallas."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["My lord, I aim a mile beyond the moon.","Your letter is with Jupiter by this."],"id":525,"m":"My lord, I am aiming a mile beyond the moon. Your letter is with Jupiter by now."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Ha! ha! Publius, Publius, what hast thou done?","See, see, thou hast shot off one of Taurus' horns."],"id":526,"m":"Ha, ha! Publius, Publius, what have you done? Look, look, you have shot off one of Taurus's horns."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["This was the sport, my lord; when Publius shot,","The Bull, being galled, gave Aries such a knock","That down fell both the Ram's horns in the court;","And who should find them but the empress' villain?","She laughed, and told the Moor he should not choose","But give them to his master for a present."],"id":527,"m":"That was the fun of it, my lord. When Publius shot, the Bull was hurt and gave Aries such a knock that both the Ram's horns fell down into the court. And who should find them but the empress's villain? She laughed, and told the Moor he had no choice but to give them to his master as a present."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Why, there it goes. God give his lordship joy!"],"id":528,"m":"Why, there it goes. God give his lordship joy!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Clown with a basket and two pigeons in it.","id":529},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["News, news from heaven! Marcus, the post is come.","Sirrah, what tidings? Have you any letters?","Shall I have justice? What says Jupiter?"],"id":530,"m":"News, news from heaven! Marcus, the messenger has come. Fellow, what news? Have you any letters? Shall I have justice? What does Jupiter say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Ho, the gibbet-maker? He says that he hath taken them down again, for","the man must not be hanged till the next week."],"id":531,"m":"Oh, the gallows-maker? He says he has taken them down again, because the man isn't to be hanged until next week."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["But what says Jupiter, I ask thee?"],"id":532,"m":"But what does Jupiter say, I'm asking you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Alas, sir, I know not Jubiter; I never drank with him in all my life."],"id":533,"m":"Sorry, sir, I don't know Jubiter. I've never had a drink with him in my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Why, villain, art not thou the carrier?"],"id":534,"m":"Why, you rascal, aren't you the carrier?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Ay, of my pigeons, sir; nothing else."],"id":535,"m":"Yes, of my pigeons, sir. Nothing else."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Why, didst thou not come from heaven?"],"id":536,"m":"Why, didn't you come from heaven?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["From heaven? Alas, sir, I never came there. God forbid I should be so","bold to press to heaven in my young days. Why, I am going with my","pigeons to the tribunal plebs, to take up a matter of brawl betwixt my","uncle and one of the emperal's men."],"id":537,"m":"From heaven? Oh no, sir, I've never been there. God forbid I should be so bold as to push my way into heaven while I'm young. Why, I'm going with my pigeons to the people's court, to settle a fight between my uncle and one of the emperor's men."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Why, sir, that is as fit as can be to serve for your oration; and let","him deliver the pigeons to the emperor from you."],"id":538,"m":"Why, sir, that is just the thing to serve for your speech. Let him deliver the pigeons to the emperor from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Tell me, can you deliver an oration to the emperor with a grace?"],"id":539,"m":"Tell me, can you deliver a speech to the emperor gracefully?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Nay, truly, sir, I could never say grace in all my life."],"id":540,"m":"No, honestly, sir, I've never been able to say grace in my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Sirrah, come hither. Make no more ado,","But give your pigeons to the emperor.","By me thou shalt have justice at his hands.","Hold, hold; meanwhile here's money for thy charges.","Give me pen and ink.","Sirrah, can you with a grace deliver up a supplication?"],"id":541,"m":"Fellow, come here. Don't make any more fuss, but give your pigeons to the emperor. Through me you shall have justice from him. Wait, wait. Meanwhile, here's money for your trouble. Give me pen and ink. Fellow, can you hand over a petition gracefully?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Ay, sir."],"id":542,"m":"Yes, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Then here is a supplication for you. And when you come to him, at the","first approach you must kneel; then kiss his foot; then deliver up your","pigeons; and then look for your reward. I'll be at hand, sir; see you","do it bravely."],"id":543,"m":"Then here is a petition for you. And when you come to him, as soon as you approach, you must kneel, then kiss his foot, then hand over your pigeons, and then wait for your reward. I'll be nearby, sir. See that you do it well."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I warrant you, sir; let me alone."],"id":544,"m":"I promise you, sir. Leave it to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Sirrah, hast thou a knife? Come let me see it.","Here, Marcus, fold it in the oration;","For thou hast made it like a humble suppliant.","And when thou hast given it to the emperor,","Knock at my door, and tell me what he says."],"id":545,"m":"Fellow, have you a knife? Come, let me see it. Here, Marcus, wrap it up in the speech, for you have written it like a humble petitioner. (To the Clown) And when you have given it to the emperor, knock at my door and tell me what he says."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["God be with you, sir; I will."],"id":546,"m":"God be with you, sir. I will."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":547},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Come, Marcus, let us go. Publius, follow me."],"id":548,"m":"Come, Marcus, let us go. Publius, follow me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":549},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"Rome. Before the Palace","id":550},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Emperor Saturninus and Empress Tamora and her two sons Chiron and Demetrius, with Attendants. The Emperor brings the arrows in his hand that Titus shot at him.","id":551},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Why, lords, what wrongs are these! Was ever seen","An emperor in Rome thus overborne,","Troubled, confronted thus; and, for the extent","Of legal justice, used in such contempt?","My lords, you know, as know the mightful gods,","However these disturbers of our peace","Buzz in the people's ears, there naught hath passed","But even with law against the wilful sons","Of old Andronicus. And what an if","His sorrows have so overwhelmed his wits?","Shall we be thus afflicted in his wreaks,","His fits, his frenzy, and his bitterness?","And now he writes to heaven for his redress!","See, here's \"to Jove,\" and this \"to Mercury,\"","This \"to Apollo,\" this to the god of war.","Sweet scrolls to fly about the streets of Rome!","What's this but libelling against the senate,","And blazoning our injustice everywhere?","A goodly humour, is it not, my lords?","As who would say, in Rome no justice were.","But if I live, his feigned ecstasies","Shall be no shelter to these outrages;","But he and his shall know that justice lives","In Saturninus' health; whom, if she sleep,","He'll so awake as he in fury shall","Cut off the proud'st conspirator that lives."],"id":552,"m":"Why, lords, what insults are these! Was an emperor in Rome ever so overpowered, troubled and defied, and treated with such contempt for applying lawful justice? My lords, you know, as the mighty gods know, that whatever these disturbers of our peace whisper in the people's ears, nothing has been done except by law against the wilful sons of old Andronicus. And what if his sorrows have overwhelmed his wits? Must we be tormented by his acts of revenge, his fits, his frenzy and his bitterness? And now he writes to heaven for justice! Look, here's one 'To Jove', and this 'To Mercury', this 'To Apollo', this to the god of war. Sweet scrolls to fly about the streets of Rome! What is this but slandering the senate and proclaiming our injustice everywhere? A fine joke, isn't it, my lords? As if to say there is no justice in Rome. But if I live, his pretended madness will not protect these outrages. He and his family shall learn that justice lives in Saturninus's health, and if she sleeps, he will wake her so that in her fury she will cut down the proudest conspirator alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["My gracious lord, my lovely Saturnine,","Lord of my life, commander of my thoughts,","Calm thee, and bear the faults of Titus' age,","Th' effects of sorrow for his valiant sons,","Whose loss hath pierced him deep and scarred his heart;","And rather comfort his distressed plight","Than prosecute the meanest or the best","For these contempts. [Aside] Why, thus it shall become","High-witted Tamora to gloze with all.","But, Titus, I have touched thee to the quick;","Thy life-blood out, if Aaron now be wise,","Then is all safe, the anchor in the port."],"id":553,"m":"My gracious lord, my lovely Saturnine, lord of my life, ruler of my thoughts, calm yourself, and put up with the faults of Titus's age, the effects of grief for his brave sons, whose loss has pierced him deeply and scarred his heart. Comfort him in his distress, rather than prosecute the lowest or the highest of them for these insults. (Aside) Why, it suits clever Tamora to smooth everyone over like this. But, Titus, I have wounded you to the quick. Once your life-blood is drained, if Aaron is wise now, then all is safe, the anchor is in the harbour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Clown.","id":554},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","cont":1,"lines":["How now, good fellow, wouldst thou speak with us?"],"id":555,"m":"Well, good fellow, do you want to speak with us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Yes, forsooth, an your mistresship be emperial."],"id":556,"m":"Yes, indeed, if your mistress-ship is emperial."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Empress I am, but yonder sits the emperor."],"id":557,"m":"I am the empress, but there sits the emperor."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["'Tis he. God and Saint Stephen give you good e'en. I have brought you a","letter and a couple of pigeons here."],"id":558,"m":"That's him. God and Saint Stephen give you good evening. I've brought you a letter and a couple of pigeons here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Saturninus reads the letter.","id":559},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Go take him away, and hang him presently."],"id":560,"m":"Go, take him away and hang him at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["How much money must I have?"],"id":561,"m":"How much money will I get?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Come, sirrah, you must be hanged."],"id":562,"m":"Come, fellow, you must be hanged."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Hanged! by'r Lady, then I have brought up a neck to a fair end."],"id":563,"m":"Hanged! By Our Lady, then I've raised a neck for a fine end."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit guarded.","id":564},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Despiteful and intolerable wrongs!","Shall I endure this monstrous villainy?","I know from whence this same device proceeds.","May this be borne as if his traitorous sons,","That died by law for murder of our brother,","Have by my means been butchered wrongfully?","Go, drag the villain hither by the hair;","Nor age nor honour shall shape privilege.","For this proud mock I'll be thy slaughterman,","Sly frantic wretch, that holp'st to make me great,","In hope thyself should govern Rome and me."],"id":565,"m":"Spiteful and unbearable insults! Must I put up with this monstrous villainy? I know where this trick comes from. Is this to be borne, as if his treacherous sons, who died lawfully for murdering our brother, had been wrongly butchered on my orders? Go, drag the villain here by the hair. Neither age nor honour shall give him special treatment. For this proud mockery I'll be your executioner, you sly, raving wretch, who helped to make me great in the hope that you would rule Rome and me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aemilius.","id":566},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","cont":1,"lines":["What news with thee, Aemilius?"],"id":567,"m":"What news do you bring, Aemilius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AEMILIUS","lines":["Arm, my lord! Rome never had more cause.","The Goths have gathered head, and with a power","Of high-resolved men, bent to the spoil,","They hither march amain, under conduct","Of Lucius, son to old Andronicus;","Who threats, in course of this revenge, to do","As much as ever Coriolanus did."],"id":568,"m":"To arms, my lord! Rome never had more reason. The Goths have raised an army, and with a force of determined men, bent on plunder, they are marching here at full speed, led by Lucius, son of old Andronicus. He threatens, in pursuing this revenge, to do as much as Coriolanus ever did."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Is warlike Lucius general of the Goths?","These tidings nip me, and I hang the head","As flowers with frost, or grass beat down with storms.","Ay, now begins our sorrows to approach.","'Tis he the common people love so much;","Myself hath often overheard them say,","When I have walked like a private man,","That Lucius' banishment was wrongfully,","And they have wished that Lucius were their emperor."],"id":569,"m":"Is warlike Lucius general of the Goths? This news blights me, and I hang my head like flowers in frost or grass beaten down by storms. Yes, now our sorrows are beginning to close in. He is the one the common people love so much. I have often overheard them say myself, when I have walked about in disguise, that Lucius's banishment was unjust, and they have wished that Lucius were their emperor."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Why should you fear? Is not your city strong?"],"id":570,"m":"Why should you be afraid? Isn't your city strong?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Ay, but the citizens favour Lucius,","And will revolt from me to succour him."],"id":571,"m":"Yes, but the citizens favour Lucius, and will rebel against me to help him."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["King, be thy thoughts imperious like thy name.","Is the sun dimmed, that gnats do fly in it?","The eagle suffers little birds to sing,","And is not careful what they mean thereby,","Knowing that with the shadow of his wings","He can at pleasure stint their melody;","Even so mayest thou the giddy men of Rome.","Then cheer thy spirit; for know, thou emperor,","I will enchant the old Andronicus","With words more sweet, and yet more dangerous,","Than baits to fish or honey-stalks to sheep,","Whenas the one is wounded with the bait,","The other rotted with delicious feed."],"id":572,"m":"King, let your thoughts be as commanding as your title. Is the sun dimmed because gnats fly in it? The eagle lets little birds sing and does not care what they mean by it, knowing that he can stop their song whenever he likes with the shadow of his wings. You can do the same with the fickle men of Rome. So cheer up. For know, my emperor, I will bewitch old Andronicus with words sweeter, and yet more dangerous, than bait to fish or clover to sheep: the fish is hooked by the bait, and the sheep is made sick by its delicious food."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["But he will not entreat his son for us."],"id":573,"m":"But he will not plead with his son for us."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["If Tamora entreat him, then he will,","For I can smooth and fill his aged ears","With golden promises, that, were his heart","Almost impregnable, his old ears deaf,","Yet should both ear and heart obey my tongue.","[to Aemilius] Go thou before, be our ambassador.","Say that the emperor requests a parley","Of warlike Lucius, and appoint the meeting","Even at his father's house, the old Andronicus."],"id":574,"m":"If Tamora pleads with him, then he will. For I can flatter him and fill his old ears with golden promises, so that even if his heart were almost impossible to storm and his old ears deaf, both ear and heart would obey my tongue. (To Aemilius) You go ahead and be our ambassador. Say that the emperor asks warlike Lucius for a meeting, and arrange it at his father's house, old Andronicus's."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Aemilius, do this message honourably,","And if he stand on hostage for his safety,","Bid him demand what pledge will please him best."],"id":575,"m":"Aemilius, deliver this message honourably, and if he insists on hostages for his safety, tell him to ask for whatever guarantee will please him best."},{"t":"sp","who":"AEMILIUS","lines":["Your bidding shall I do effectually."],"id":576,"m":"I will carry out your orders effectively."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":577},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Now will I to that old Andronicus,","And temper him with all the art I have,","To pluck proud Lucius from the warlike Goths.","And now, sweet emperor, be blithe again,","And bury all thy fear in my devices."],"id":578,"m":"Now I'll go to old Andronicus and work on him with all my skill, to pull proud Lucius away from the warlike Goths. And now, sweet emperor, be cheerful again, and bury all your fear in my plans."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Then go successantly, and plead to him."],"id":579,"m":"Then go, and may you succeed, and plead with him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":580},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Plains near Rome","id":581},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius with an army of Goths, with drums and soldiers.","id":582},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Approved warriors and my faithful friends,","I have received letters from great Rome","Which signifies what hate they bear their emperor","And how desirous of our sight they are.","Therefore, great lords, be, as your titles witness,","Imperious, and impatient of your wrongs;","And wherein Rome hath done you any scath,","Let him make treble satisfaction."],"id":583,"m":"Proven warriors and faithful friends, I have received letters from great Rome which tell me how much they hate their emperor and how eager they are to see us. So, great lords, be what your titles show you to be, commanding and unwilling to put up with wrongs. And wherever Rome has done you harm, let the emperor pay you back three times over."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GOTH","lines":["Brave slip, sprung from the great Andronicus,","Whose name was once our terror, now our comfort,","Whose high exploits and honourable deeds","Ingrateful Rome requites with foul contempt,","Be bold in us. We'll follow where thou lead'st,","Like stinging bees in hottest summer's day","Led by their master to the flowered fields,","And be avenged on cursed Tamora."],"id":584,"m":"Brave offshoot of the great Andronicus, whose name was once our terror and is now our comfort, whose high achievements and honourable deeds ungrateful Rome repays with foul contempt: be confident in us. We'll follow wherever you lead, like stinging bees on the hottest summer's day led by their king to the flowering fields, and take revenge on cursed Tamora."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOTHS","lines":["And as he saith, so say we all with him."],"id":585,"m":"And as he says, so we all say with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I humbly thank him, and I thank you all.","But who comes here, led by a lusty Goth?"],"id":586,"m":"I humbly thank him, and I thank you all. But who is this, led by a sturdy Goth?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Goth, leading of Aaron with his Child in his arms.","id":587},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GOTH","lines":["Renowned Lucius, from our troops I strayed","To gaze upon a ruinous monastery;","And as I earnestly did fix mine eye","Upon the wasted building, suddenly","I heard a child cry underneath a wall.","I made unto the noise, when soon I heard","The crying babe controlled with this discourse:","\"Peace, tawny slave, half me and half thy dame!","Did not thy hue bewray whose brat thou art,","Had nature lent thee but thy mother's look,","Villain, thou mightst have been an emperor.","But where the bull and cow are both milk-white,","They never do beget a coal-black calf.","Peace, villain, peace!\" even thus he rates the babe,","\"For I must bear thee to a trusty Goth,","Who, when he knows thou art the empress' babe,","Will hold thee dearly for thy mother's sake.\"","With this, my weapon drawn, I rushed upon him,","Surprised him suddenly, and brought him hither","To use as you think needful of the man."],"id":588,"m":"Famous Lucius, I wandered away from our troops to look at a ruined monastery. And as I was staring hard at the wrecked building, I suddenly heard a child crying underneath a wall. I went towards the noise, and soon I heard the crying baby being hushed with these words: 'Quiet, brown rascal, half me and half your mother! If your colour didn't give away whose brat you are, if nature had only given you your mother's looks, you villain, you might have been an emperor. But when the bull and cow are both milk-white, they never produce a coal-black calf. Quiet, villain, quiet!' That's how he scolded the baby. 'For I must carry you to a trusty Goth, who, when he knows you are the empress's baby, will treasure you for your mother's sake.' At that, I drew my weapon and rushed at him, took him by surprise, and brought him here for you to deal with as you think necessary."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["O worthy Goth, this is the incarnate devil","That robbed Andronicus of his good hand;","This is the pearl that pleased your empress' eye;","And here's the base fruit of her burning lust.","Say, wall-eyed slave, whither wouldst thou convey","This growing image of thy fiend-like face?","Why dost not speak? What, deaf? Not a word?","A halter, soldiers, hang him on this tree,","And by his side his fruit of bastardy."],"id":589,"m":"O worthy Goth, this is the devil in human form who robbed Andronicus of his good hand. This is the pearl that pleased your empress's eye, and here is the low fruit of her burning lust. Tell me, glaring slave, where were you taking this growing copy of your devilish face? Why don't you speak? What, deaf? Not a word? A rope, soldiers. Hang him on this tree, and his bastard beside him."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Touch not the boy, he is of royal blood."],"id":590,"m":"Don't touch the boy. He is of royal blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Too like the sire for ever being good.","First hang the child, that he may see it sprawl,","A sight to vex the father's soul withal.","Get me a ladder."],"id":591,"m":"Too like his father ever to be good. Hang the child first, so that he can see it kick, a sight to torment the father's soul. Get me a ladder."},{"t":"sd","text":"A ladder is brought, which Aaron is made to ascend.","id":592},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Lucius, save the child;","And bear it from me to the empress.","If thou do this, I'll show thee wondrous things","That highly may advantage thee to hear.","If thou wilt not, befall what may befall,","I'll speak no more but \"Vengeance rot you all!\""],"id":593,"m":"Lucius, save the child, and take it to the empress for me. If you do this, I'll reveal amazing things that will be very much to your advantage to hear. If you won't, then whatever happens, I'll say nothing more but 'Vengeance rot you all!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Say on, and if it please me which thou speak'st,","Thy child shall live, and I will see it nourished."],"id":594,"m":"Speak, and if what you say pleases me, your child shall live, and I will see that it is fed and cared for."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["And if it please thee? Why, assure thee, Lucius,","'Twill vex thy soul to hear what I shall speak;","For I must talk of murders, rapes, and massacres,","Acts of black night, abominable deeds,","Complots of mischief, treason, villainies,","Ruthful to hear, yet piteously performed.","And this shall all be buried in my death,","Unless thou swear to me my child shall live."],"id":595,"m":"If it pleases you? Why, I promise you, Lucius, it will torment your soul to hear what I have to say. For I must talk of murders, rapes and massacres, acts of black night, disgusting deeds, plots of evil, treason, villainies, pitiful to hear and yet pitilessly done. And all this will be buried with my death unless you swear to me that my child shall live."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Tell on thy mind; I say thy child shall live."],"id":596,"m":"Say what's on your mind. I tell you your child shall live."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Swear that he shall, and then I will begin."],"id":597,"m":"Swear that he shall, and then I'll begin."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Who should I swear by? Thou believ'st no god.","That granted, how canst thou believe an oath?"],"id":598,"m":"What should I swear by? You don't believe in any god. That being so, how can you believe an oath?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["What if I do not? As indeed I do not;","Yet, for I know thou art religious,","And hast a thing within thee called conscience,","With twenty popish tricks and ceremonies","Which I have seen thee careful to observe,","Therefore I urge thy oath; for that I know","An idiot holds his bauble for a god,","And keeps the oath which by that god he swears,","To that I'll urge him. Therefore thou shalt vow","By that same god, what god soe'er it be","That thou adorest and hast in reverence,","To save my boy, to nourish and bring him up;","Or else I will discover naught to thee."],"id":599,"m":"What if I don't? And indeed I don't. Yet because I know you are religious, and have a thing inside you called conscience, with twenty popish tricks and ceremonies that I have seen you careful to observe, I insist on your oath. For I know that an idiot takes his jester's stick for a god and keeps the oath he swears by that god, and I'll hold him to it. So you shall swear by that same god, whatever god it is that you worship and revere, to save my boy and feed him and bring him up, or else I will reveal nothing to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Even by my god I swear to thee I will."],"id":600,"m":"I swear to you by my god that I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["First know thou, I begot him on the empress."],"id":601,"m":"First, know that I fathered him on the empress."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["O most insatiate and luxurious woman!"],"id":602,"m":"O most insatiable and lustful woman!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Tut, Lucius, this was but a deed of charity","To that which thou shalt hear of me anon.","'Twas her two sons that murdered Bassianus;","They cut thy sister's tongue, and ravished her,","And cut her hands, and trimmed her as thou sawest."],"id":603,"m":"Tut, Lucius, that was only an act of charity compared with what you'll hear from me shortly. It was her two sons who murdered Bassianus. They cut out your sister's tongue and raped her, and cut off her hands, and trimmed her as you saw."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["O detestable villain, call'st thou that trimming?"],"id":604,"m":"O hateful villain, do you call that trimming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Why, she was washed, and cut, and trimmed; and 'twas","Trim sport for them which had the doing of it."],"id":605,"m":"Why, she was washed and cut and trimmed, and it was fine sport for the ones who did it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["O barbarous beastly villains, like thyself!"],"id":606,"m":"O barbarous, beastly villains, like you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Indeed, I was their tutor to instruct them.","That codding spirit had they from their mother,","As sure a card as ever won the set;","That bloody mind I think they learned of me,","As true a dog as ever fought at head.","Well, let my deeds be witness of my worth.","I trained thy brethren to that guileful hole","Where the dead corpse of Bassianus lay.","I wrote the letter that thy father found,","And hid the gold within that letter mentioned,","Confederate with the queen and her two sons.","And what not done, that thou hast cause to rue,","Wherein I had no stroke of mischief in't?","I played the cheater for thy father's hand,","And, when I had it, drew myself apart,","And almost broke my heart with extreme laughter.","I pried me through the crevice of a wall","When, for his hand, he had his two sons' heads;","Beheld his tears, and laughed so heartily","That both mine eyes were rainy like to his.","And when I told the empress of this sport,","She sounded almost at my pleasing tale,","And for my tidings gave me twenty kisses."],"id":607,"m":"Indeed, I was their tutor and taught them. They got that lustful spirit from their mother, as sure a card as ever won the game. That bloody mind I think they learned from me, as true a dog as ever went for the bull's head. Well, let my deeds prove what I am worth. I lured your brothers to that treacherous hole where the dead body of Bassianus lay. I wrote the letter your father found and hid the gold that the letter mentioned, in league with the queen and her two sons. And what has been done that you have cause to grieve over, in which I did not have a hand in the mischief? I tricked your father out of his hand, and when I had it, I went off on my own and almost broke my heart with laughing. I peeped through a crack in a wall when he got his two sons' heads in return for his hand. I watched his tears and laughed so heartily that both my eyes were as wet as his. And when I told the empress about this sport, she almost fainted at my pleasing story, and gave me twenty kisses for my news."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOTH","lines":["What, canst thou say all this and never blush?"],"id":608,"m":"What, can you say all this and never blush?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Ay, like a black dog, as the saying is."],"id":609,"m":"Yes, like a black dog, as the saying goes."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Art thou not sorry for these heinous deeds?"],"id":610,"m":"Aren't you sorry for these terrible deeds?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Ay, that I had not done a thousand more.","Even now I curse the day, and yet, I think,","Few come within the compass of my curse,","Wherein I did not some notorious ill,","As kill a man, or else devise his death;","Ravish a maid, or plot the way to do it;","Accuse some innocent, and forswear myself;","Set deadly enmity between two friends;","Make poor men's cattle break their necks;","Set fire on barns and haystalks in the night,","And bid the owners quench them with their tears.","Oft have I digged up dead men from their graves,","And set them upright at their dear friends' door,","Even when their sorrows almost was forgot,","And on their skins, as on the bark of trees,","Have with my knife carved in Roman letters,","\"Let not your sorrow die, though I am dead.\"","But I have done a thousand dreadful things","As willingly as one would kill a fly,","And nothing grieves me heartily indeed","But that I cannot do ten thousand more."],"id":611,"m":"Yes: sorry that I didn't do a thousand more. Even now I curse the day (and yet I think few days come within reach of my curse) in which I did not do some notorious evil: killed a man or plotted his death, raped a girl or planned how to do it, accused some innocent person and swore falsely, set deadly hatred between two friends, made poor men's cattle break their necks, set fire to barns and haystacks at night and told the owners to put them out with their tears. I have often dug dead men up from their graves and stood them upright at their dear friends' doors, just when their grief was almost forgotten, and carved on their skins with my knife, as on the bark of trees, in Roman letters: 'Let not your sorrow die, though I am dead.' Oh, I have done a thousand dreadful things as willingly as one would kill a fly, and nothing really grieves me except that I cannot do ten thousand more."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Bring down the devil, for he must not die","So sweet a death as hanging presently."],"id":612,"m":"Bring the devil down, for he must not die such a sweet death as a quick hanging."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["If there be devils, would I were a devil,","To live and burn in everlasting fire,","So I might have your company in hell","But to torment you with my bitter tongue!"],"id":613,"m":"If there are devils, I wish I were a devil, to live and burn in everlasting fire, if only I could have your company in hell to torment you with my bitter tongue!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Sirs, stop his mouth, and let him speak no more."],"id":614,"m":"Sirs, gag him, and let him speak no more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aemilius.","id":615},{"t":"sp","who":"GOTH","lines":["My lord, there is a messenger from Rome","Desires to be admitted to your presence."],"id":616,"m":"My lord, there is a messenger from Rome who asks to be let in to see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Let him come near.","Welcome, Aemilius. What's the news from Rome?"],"id":617,"m":"Let him come forward. Welcome, Aemilius. What's the news from Rome?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AEMILIUS","lines":["Lord Lucius, and you princes of the Goths,","The Roman emperor greets you all by me;","And, for he understands you are in arms,","He craves a parley at your father's house,","Willing you to demand your hostages,","And they shall be immediately delivered."],"id":618,"m":"Lord Lucius, and you princes of the Goths, the Roman emperor greets you all through me. And because he understands you are armed for war, he asks for a meeting at your father's house. He invites you to name your hostages, and they will be handed over immediately."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GOTH","lines":["What says our general?"],"id":619,"m":"What does our general say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Aemilius, let the emperor give his pledges","Unto my father and my uncle Marcus,","And we will come. March away."],"id":620,"m":"Aemilius, let the emperor give his hostages to my father and my uncle Marcus, and we will come. March on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":621},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Rome. Before Titus's House","id":622},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Tamora and her two sons, disguised.","id":623},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Thus, in this strange and sad habiliment,","I will encounter with Andronicus,","And say I am Revenge, sent from below","To join with him and right his heinous wrongs.","Knock at his study, where they say he keeps","To ruminate strange plots of dire revenge;","Tell him Revenge is come to join with him","And work confusion on his enemies."],"id":624,"m":"In this strange and sombre costume, I will meet Andronicus and say I am Revenge, sent up from the underworld to join with him and avenge his terrible wrongs. Knock at his study, where they say he stays to brood over strange plots of dreadful revenge. Tell him Revenge has come to join with him and bring ruin on his enemies."},{"t":"sd","text":"They knock.","id":625},{"t":"sd","text":"Titus above opens his study door.","id":626},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Who doth molest my contemplation?","Is it your trick to make me ope the door,","That so my sad decrees may fly away","And all my study be to no effect?","You are deceived; for what I mean to do","See here in bloody lines I have set down;","And what is written shall be executed."],"id":627,"m":"Who is disturbing my thoughts? Is it your trick to make me open the door, so that my sad decisions fly away and all my study comes to nothing? You are wrong. For see, I have written down here in lines of blood what I mean to do, and what is written shall be carried out."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Titus, I am come to talk with thee."],"id":628,"m":"Titus, I have come to talk with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["No, not a word; how can I grace my talk,","Wanting a hand to give it action?","Thou hast the odds of me; therefore no more."],"id":629,"m":"No, not a word. How can I give my words grace without a hand to make gestures? You have the advantage of me, so no more."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["If thou didst know me, thou wouldst talk with me."],"id":630,"m":"If you knew who I was, you would talk with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["I am not mad; I know thee well enough.","Witness this wretched stump, witness these crimson lines;","Witness these trenches made by grief and care;","Witness the tiring day and heavy night;","Witness all sorrow that I know thee well","For our proud empress, mighty Tamora.","Is not thy coming for my other hand?"],"id":631,"m":"I am not mad. I know you well enough. This wretched stump bears witness, these crimson lines bear witness, these furrows made by grief and care bear witness, the tiring day and the heavy night bear witness, all sorrow bears witness that I know you well: you are our proud empress, mighty Tamora. Haven't you come for my other hand?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Know thou, sad man, I am not Tamora;","She is thy enemy, and I thy friend.","I am Revenge, sent from th' infernal kingdom","To ease the gnawing vulture of thy mind","By working wreakful vengeance on thy foes.","Come down and welcome me to this world's light;","Confer with me of murder and of death.","There's not a hollow cave or lurking-place,","No vast obscurity or misty vale,","Where bloody murder or detested rape","Can couch for fear but I will find them out,","And in their ears tell them my dreadful name,","Revenge, which makes the foul offender quake."],"id":632,"m":"Know, sad man, that I am not Tamora. She is your enemy, and I am your friend. I am Revenge, sent from the kingdom of hell to ease the vulture gnawing at your mind by taking furious vengeance on your enemies. Come down and welcome me to the light of this world. Talk with me of murder and death. There is no hollow cave or hiding place, no vast darkness or misty valley, where bloody murder or hateful rape can crouch in fear, that I will not find them out and tell them my dreadful name in their ears: Revenge, which makes the foul offender tremble."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Art thou Revenge? And art thou sent to me","To be a torment to mine enemies?"],"id":633,"m":"Are you Revenge? And are you sent to me to be a torment to my enemies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["I am; therefore come down and welcome me."],"id":634,"m":"I am. So come down and welcome me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Do me some service ere I come to thee.","Lo, by thy side where Rape and Murder stands;","Now give some surance that thou art Revenge:","Stab them, or tear them on thy chariot wheels,","And then I'll come and be thy waggoner,","And whirl along with thee about the globe.","Provide thee two proper palfreys, black as jet,","To hale thy vengeful waggon swift away,","And find out murderers in their guilty caves.","And when thy car is loaden with their heads,","I will dismount, and by the waggon-wheel","Trot like a servile footman all day long,","Even from Hyperion's rising in the east","Until his very downfall in the sea.","And day by day I'll do this heavy task,","So thou destroy Rapine and Murder there."],"id":635,"m":"Do me some service before I come to you. Look, beside you stand Rape and Murder. Now give me some proof that you are Revenge. Stab them, or tear them apart on your chariot wheels, and then I'll come and be your driver and whirl round the world with you. Get yourself two fine horses, black as jet, to pull your chariot of vengeance swiftly away and find murderers in their guilty caves. And when your chariot is loaded with their heads, I will get down and trot beside the wheel like a humble servant all day long, from the moment the sun rises in the east until it sinks into the sea. And I'll do this heavy task day after day, if you destroy Rape and Murder there."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["These are my ministers, and come with me."],"id":636,"m":"These are my servants, and they come with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Are they thy ministers? What are they called?"],"id":637,"m":"Are they your servants? What are they called?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Rapine and Murder; therefore called so","'Cause they take vengeance of such kind of men."],"id":638,"m":"Rape and Murder, called so because they take revenge on men of that kind."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Good Lord, how like the empress' sons they are,","And you the empress! But we worldly men","Have miserable, mad, mistaking eyes.","O sweet Revenge, now do I come to thee;","And, if one arm's embracement will content thee,","I will embrace thee in it by and by."],"id":639,"m":"Good Lord, how like the empress's sons they are, and you like the empress! But we mortal men have miserable, mad, mistaken eyes. O sweet Revenge, now I am coming to you, and if a one-armed hug will satisfy you, I will hug you with it in a moment."},{"t":"sd","text":"He exits above.","id":640},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["This closing with him fits his lunacy.","Whate'er I forge to feed his brain-sick humours,","Do you uphold and maintain in your speeches,","For now he firmly takes me for Revenge;","And, being credulous in this mad thought,","I'll make him send for Lucius his son;","And whilst I at a banquet hold him sure,","I'll find some cunning practice out of hand","To scatter and disperse the giddy Goths,","Or, at the least, make them his enemies.","See, here he comes, and I must ply my theme."],"id":641,"m":"Going along with him like this suits his madness. Whatever I invent to feed his sick fancies, you back it up and keep it going in what you say. For now he firmly takes me for Revenge, and since he believes this mad idea, I'll make him send for his son Lucius. And while I keep him safely occupied at a banquet, I'll quickly find some clever trick to scatter and break up the fickle Goths, or at least turn them against him. Look, here he comes, and I must keep up my part."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Titus.","id":642},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Long have I been forlorn, and all for thee.","Welcome, dread Fury, to my woeful house.","Rapine and Murder, you are welcome too.","How like the empress and her sons you are!","Well are you fitted, had you but a Moor.","Could not all hell afford you such a devil?","For well I wot the empress never wags","But in her company there is a Moor;","And, would you represent our queen aright,","It were convenient you had such a devil.","But welcome as you are. What shall we do?"],"id":643,"m":"I have been lonely a long time, and all for you. Welcome, dreadful Fury, to my house of sorrow. Rape and Murder, you are welcome too. How like the empress and her sons you are! Your costumes would be complete if only you had a Moor. Couldn't all hell provide you with such a devil? For I know very well the empress never stirs without a Moor in her company, and if you want to play our queen properly, you ought to have such a devil. But welcome as you are. What shall we do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["What wouldst thou have us do, Andronicus?"],"id":644,"m":"What would you have us do, Andronicus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Show me a murderer, I'll deal with him."],"id":645,"m":"Show me a murderer, and I'll deal with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Show me a villain that hath done a rape,","And I am sent to be revenged on him."],"id":646,"m":"Show me a villain who has committed rape, and I am sent to take revenge on him."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Show me a thousand that hath done thee wrong,","And I will be revenged on them all."],"id":647,"m":"Show me a thousand who have wronged you, and I will take revenge on them all."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Look round about the wicked streets of Rome,","And when thou find'st a man that's like thyself,","Good Murder, stab him; he's a murderer.","Go thou with him; and when it is thy hap","To find another that is like to thee,","Good Rapine, stab him; he is a ravisher.","Go thou with them; and in the emperor's court","There is a queen, attended by a Moor;","Well shalt thou know her by thine own proportion,","For up and down she doth resemble thee.","I pray thee, do on them some violent death;","They have been violent to me and mine."],"id":648,"m":"Look all round the wicked streets of Rome, and when you find a man who looks like you, good Murder, stab him: he's a murderer. (To Chiron) You go with him, and when you happen to find another who looks like you, good Rape, stab him: he is a rapist. (To Tamora) You go with them. In the emperor's court there is a queen, attended by a Moor. You'll recognise her easily by your own shape, for she looks exactly like you in every way. Please give them some violent death. They have been violent to me and my family."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Well hast thou lessoned us; this shall we do.","But would it please thee, good Andronicus,","To send for Lucius, thy thrice-valiant son,","Who leads towards Rome a band of warlike Goths,","And bid him come and banquet at thy house?","When he is here, even at thy solemn feast,","I will bring in the empress and her sons,","The emperor himself, and all thy foes,","And at thy mercy shall they stoop and kneel,","And on them shalt thou ease thy angry heart.","What says Andronicus to this device?"],"id":649,"m":"You have taught us well. We shall do this. But would you be so good, Andronicus, as to send for Lucius, your thrice-brave son, who is leading an army of warlike Goths towards Rome, and invite him to come and feast at your house? When he is here, at your solemn feast, I will bring in the empress and her sons, the emperor himself, and all your enemies, and they shall bow and kneel at your mercy, and you can take out your angry heart on them. What does Andronicus say to this plan?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Marcus, my brother, 'tis sad Titus calls."],"id":650,"m":"Marcus, my brother, it is sad Titus calling."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Marcus.","id":651},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["Go, gentle Marcus, to thy nephew Lucius;","Thou shalt inquire him out among the Goths.","Bid him repair to me and bring with him","Some of the chiefest princes of the Goths;","Bid him encamp his soldiers where they are.","Tell him the emperor and the empress too","Feast at my house, and he shall feast with them.","This do thou for my love; and so let him,","As he regards his aged father's life."],"id":652,"m":"Go, gentle Marcus, to your nephew Lucius. You must seek him out among the Goths. Tell him to come to me and bring some of the leading princes of the Goths with him. Tell him to keep his soldiers camped where they are. Tell him the emperor and the empress are feasting at my house, and he shall feast with them. Do this for love of me, and let him do it, as he values his old father's life."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["This will I do, and soon return again."],"id":653,"m":"I'll do this, and come back soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":654},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Now will I hence about thy business,","And take my ministers along with me."],"id":655,"m":"Now I'll go and see to your business, and take my servants with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Nay, nay, let Rape and Murder stay with me,","Or else I'll call my brother back again","And cleave to no revenge but Lucius."],"id":656,"m":"No, no, let Rape and Murder stay with me, or else I'll call my brother back and rely on no revenge but Lucius."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["[Aside to them] What say you, boys? Will you abide with him,","Whiles I go tell my lord the emperor","How I have governed our determined jest?","Yield to his humour, smooth and speak him fair,","And tarry with him till I come again."],"id":657,"m":"(Aside to her sons) What do you say, boys? Will you stay with him while I go and tell my lord the emperor how I have managed the joke we planned? Go along with his whims, flatter him and speak kindly to him, and stay with him until I come back."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["[Aside] I knew them all, though they suppose me mad,","And will o'erreach them in their own devices,","A pair of cursed hell-hounds and their dam."],"id":658,"m":"(Aside) I recognised them all, though they think I'm mad, and I'll outwit them in their own schemes: a pair of cursed hellhounds and their mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Madam, depart at pleasure; leave us here."],"id":659,"m":"Madam, go when you like. Leave us here."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Farewell, Andronicus. Revenge now goes","To lay a complot to betray thy foes."],"id":660,"m":"Farewell, Andronicus. Revenge is going now to lay a plot to trap your enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["I know thou dost; and, sweet Revenge, farewell."],"id":661,"m":"I know you are. And, sweet Revenge, farewell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Tamora.","id":662},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Tell us, old man, how shall we be employed?"],"id":663,"m":"Tell us, old man, what shall we do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Tut, I have work enough for you to do.","Publius, come hither, Caius, and Valentine."],"id":664,"m":"Tut, I have plenty of work for you to do. Publius, come here, and Caius, and Valentine."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Publius and others.","id":665},{"t":"sp","who":"PUBLIUS","lines":["What is your will?"],"id":666,"m":"What is your wish?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Know you these two?"],"id":667,"m":"Do you know these two?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUBLIUS","lines":["The empress' sons, I take them, Chiron, Demetrius."],"id":668,"m":"The empress's sons, I think: Chiron and Demetrius."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Fie, Publius, fie, thou art too much deceived.","The one is Murder, and Rape is the other's name;","And therefore bind them, gentle Publius.","Caius and Valentine, lay hands on them.","Oft have you heard me wish for such an hour,","And now I find it. Therefore bind them sure,","And stop their mouths if they begin to cry."],"id":669,"m":"Shame on you, Publius, you are badly mistaken. One is Murder, and the other's name is Rape. So tie them up, gentle Publius. Caius and Valentine, seize them. You have often heard me wish for an hour like this, and now I have found it. So tie them up tightly, and gag them if they start to shout."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Titus.","id":670},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIRON","lines":["Villains, forbear! We are the empress' sons."],"id":671,"m":"Villains, stop! We are the empress's sons."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUBLIUS","lines":["And therefore do we what we are commanded.","Stop close their mouths, let them not speak a word.","Is he sure bound? Look that you bind them fast."],"id":672,"m":"And that is why we are doing what we are told. Gag them tightly; don't let them say a word. Is he tied securely? Make sure you bind them fast."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Titus Andronicus with a knife, and Lavinia with a basin.","id":673},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Come, come, Lavinia; look, thy foes are bound.","Sirs, stop their mouths, let them not speak to me,","But let them hear what fearful words I utter.","O villains, Chiron and Demetrius!","Here stands the spring whom you have stained with mud,","This goodly summer with your winter mixed.","You killed her husband, and for that vile fault","Two of her brothers were condemned to death,","My hand cut off and made a merry jest,","Both her sweet hands, her tongue, and that more dear","Than hands or tongue, her spotless chastity,","Inhuman traitors, you constrained and forced.","What would you say if I should let you speak?","Villains, for shame you could not beg for grace.","Hark, wretches, how I mean to martyr you.","This one hand yet is left to cut your throats,","Whiles that Lavinia 'tween her stumps doth hold","The basin that receives your guilty blood.","You know your mother means to feast with me,","And calls herself Revenge, and thinks me mad.","Hark, villains! I will grind your bones to dust,","And with your blood and it I'll make a paste,","And of the paste a coffin I will rear,","And make two pasties of your shameful heads,","And bid that strumpet, your unhallowed dam,","Like to the earth swallow her own increase.","This is the feast that I have bid her to,","And this the banquet she shall surfeit on;","For worse than Philomel you used my daughter,","And worse than Procne I will be revenged.","And now prepare your throats.—Lavinia, come","Receive the blood."],"id":674,"m":"Come, come, Lavinia. Look, your enemies are tied up. Sirs, gag them. Don't let them speak to me, but let them hear the fearful words I say. O villains, Chiron and Demetrius! Here stands the fountain you have fouled with mud, this fine summer you have mixed with your winter. You killed her husband, and for that vile crime two of her brothers were condemned to death, my hand was cut off and made into a merry joke. Both her sweet hands, her tongue, and, dearer than hands or tongue, her spotless chastity, you inhuman traitors, you took by force. What would you say if I let you speak? Villains, for shame you could not beg for mercy. Listen, wretches, to how I mean to torture you. This one hand is still left to cut your throats, while Lavinia holds between her stumps the basin that catches your guilty blood. You know your mother means to feast with me. She calls herself Revenge, and thinks I'm mad. Listen, villains! I will grind your bones to dust, and make a paste of it with your blood, and with the paste I'll build a pie crust, and make two pies of your shameful heads. And I'll invite that whore, your unholy mother, to swallow her own children, as the earth does. This is the feast I have invited her to, and this is the banquet she will gorge on. For you treated my daughter worse than Philomela was treated, and I will take worse revenge than Procne. And now get your throats ready. Lavinia, come, catch the blood."},{"t":"sd","text":"He cuts their throats.","id":675},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","cont":1,"lines":["And when that they are dead,","Let me go grind their bones to powder small,","And with this hateful liquor temper it,","And in that paste let their vile heads be baked.","Come, come, be everyone officious","To make this banquet, which I wish may prove","More stern and bloody than the Centaurs' feast.","So, now bring them in, for I'll play the cook,","And see them ready against their mother comes."],"id":676,"m":"And when they are dead, let me go and grind their bones to fine powder, and mix it with this hateful liquid, and let their vile heads be baked in that paste. Come, come, everyone, be busy making this banquet, which I hope will prove grimmer and bloodier than the Centaurs' feast. So, now bring them in, for I'll play the cook and have them ready for when their mother comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt, carrying the dead bodies.","id":677},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Rome. A Pavilion in Titus's Gardens, with tables, &c","id":678},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius, Marcus and the Goths, with Aaron, prisoner.","id":679},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Uncle Marcus, since 'tis my father's mind","That I repair to Rome, I am content."],"id":680,"m":"Uncle Marcus, since it is my father's wish that I go to Rome, I am content."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GOTH","lines":["And ours with thine, befall what fortune will."],"id":681,"m":"And we go with you, whatever fortune brings."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Good uncle, take you in this barbarous Moor,","This ravenous tiger, this accursed devil;","Let him receive no sust'nance, fetter him,","Till he be brought unto the empress' face","For testimony of her foul proceedings.","And see the ambush of our friends be strong;","I fear the emperor means no good to us."],"id":682,"m":"Good uncle, take this barbarous Moor inside, this greedy tiger, this cursed devil. Give him no food, chain him up, until he is brought face to face with the empress as evidence of her foul doings. And see that our friends lying in ambush are strong. I am afraid the emperor means us no good."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Some devil whisper curses in my ear,","And prompt me that my tongue may utter forth","The venomous malice of my swelling heart!"],"id":683,"m":"May some devil whisper curses in my ear and prompt me, so that my tongue can pour out the poisonous malice of my swelling heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Away, inhuman dog, unhallowed slave!","Sirs, help our uncle to convey him in."],"id":684,"m":"Away, inhuman dog, unholy slave! Sirs, help our uncle to take him in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound trumpets.","id":685},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","cont":1,"lines":["The trumpets show the emperor is at hand."],"id":686,"m":"The trumpets show the emperor is close by."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Goths with Aaron.","id":687},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Emperor Saturninus and Empress Tamora with Aemilius, Tribunes and others.","id":688},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["What, hath the firmament more suns than one?"],"id":689,"m":"What, does the sky have more than one sun?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["What boots it thee to call thyself a sun?"],"id":690,"m":"What good does it do you to call yourself a sun?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Rome's emperor, and nephew, break the parle;","These quarrels must be quietly debated.","The feast is ready which the careful Titus","Hath ordained to an honourable end,","For peace, for love, for league, and good to Rome.","Please you, therefore, draw nigh and take your places."],"id":691,"m":"Emperor of Rome, and nephew, stop this argument. These quarrels must be settled calmly. The feast is ready, which careful Titus has arranged for an honourable purpose: for peace, for love, for alliance and for the good of Rome. So please come forward and take your places."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Marcus, we will."],"id":692,"m":"Marcus, we will."},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets sounding, enter Titus like a cook, placing the dishes, with Young Lucius and others, and Lavinia with a veil over her face.","id":693},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Welcome, my lord; welcome, dread queen;","Welcome, ye warlike Goths; welcome, Lucius;","And welcome all. Although the cheer be poor,","'Twill fill your stomachs; please you eat of it."],"id":694,"m":"Welcome, my lord. Welcome, dread queen. Welcome, you warlike Goths. Welcome, Lucius. And welcome all. Though the food is poor, it will fill your stomachs. Please eat."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Why art thou thus attired, Andronicus?"],"id":695,"m":"Why are you dressed like this, Andronicus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Because I would be sure to have all well","To entertain your highness and your empress."],"id":696,"m":"Because I wanted to be sure everything was right for entertaining your highness and your empress."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["We are beholden to you, good Andronicus."],"id":697,"m":"We are grateful to you, good Andronicus."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["An if your highness knew my heart, you were.","My lord the emperor, resolve me this:","Was it well done of rash Virginius","To slay his daughter with his own right hand,","Because she was enforced, stained, and deflowered?"],"id":698,"m":"If your highness knew my heart, you would be. My lord the emperor, answer me this. Did hot-headed Virginius do right to kill his daughter with his own right hand because she had been raped, stained and robbed of her virginity?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["It was, Andronicus."],"id":699,"m":"He did, Andronicus."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Your reason, mighty lord?"],"id":700,"m":"Your reason, mighty lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Because the girl should not survive her shame,","And by her presence still renew his sorrows."],"id":701,"m":"Because the girl should not outlive her shame, and keep renewing his sorrows by being there."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["A reason mighty, strong, and effectual;","A pattern, precedent, and lively warrant","For me, most wretched, to perform the like.","Die, die, Lavinia, and thy shame with thee;","And with thy shame thy father's sorrow die!"],"id":702,"m":"A mighty, strong and convincing reason: a model, a precedent and a living warrant for me, most wretched man, to do the same. Die, die, Lavinia, and your shame with you, and with your shame let your father's sorrow die!"},{"t":"sd","text":"He kills Lavinia.","id":703},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["What hast thou done, unnatural and unkind?"],"id":704,"m":"What have you done, you unnatural, cruel man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Killed her for whom my tears have made me blind.","I am as woeful as Virginius was,","And have a thousand times more cause than he","To do this outrage, and it now is done."],"id":705,"m":"Killed her for whom my tears have made me blind. I am as miserable as Virginius was, and I have a thousand times more reason than he had to do this violent thing, and now it is done."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["What, was she ravished? Tell who did the deed."],"id":706,"m":"What, was she raped? Tell us who did it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Will't please you eat? Will't please your highness feed?"],"id":707,"m":"Won't you eat? Won't your highness have something?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TAMORA","lines":["Why hast thou slain thine only daughter thus?"],"id":708,"m":"Why have you killed your only daughter like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Not I; 'twas Chiron and Demetrius.","They ravished her, and cut away her tongue;","And they, 'twas they, that did her all this wrong."],"id":709,"m":"Not I: it was Chiron and Demetrius. They raped her and cut out her tongue, and it was they, they, who did her all this wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Go fetch them hither to us presently."],"id":710,"m":"Go and fetch them here to us at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Why, there they are, both baked in that pie,","Whereof their mother daintily hath fed,","Eating the flesh that she herself hath bred.","'Tis true, 'tis true; witness my knife's sharp point."],"id":711,"m":"Why, there they are, both baked in that pie, which their mother has been daintily eating, feeding on the flesh she gave birth to herself. It's true, it's true: my knife's sharp point bears witness."},{"t":"sd","text":"He stabs the Empress.","id":712},{"t":"sp","who":"SATURNINUS","lines":["Die, frantic wretch, for this accursed deed."],"id":713,"m":"Die, raving wretch, for this cursed deed."},{"t":"sd","text":"He kills Titus.","id":714},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Can the son's eye behold his father bleed?"],"id":715,"m":"Can a son's eye see his father bleed?"},{"t":"sd","text":"He kills Saturninus.","id":716},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","cont":1,"lines":["There's meed for meed, death for a deadly deed."],"id":717,"m":"There's payment for payment, death for a deadly deed."},{"t":"sd","text":"A great tumult. Lucius, Marcus, and others go aloft to the upper stage.","id":718},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["You sad-faced men, people and sons of Rome,","By uproar severed, as a flight of fowl","Scattered by winds and high tempestuous gusts,","O, let me teach you how to knit again","This scattered corn into one mutual sheaf,","These broken limbs again into one body;","Lest Rome herself be bane unto herself,","And she whom mighty kingdoms curtsy to,","Like a forlorn and desperate castaway,","Do shameful execution on herself.","But if my frosty signs and chaps of age,","Grave witnesses of true experience,","Cannot induce you to attend my words,","Speak, Rome's dear friend, [to Lucius] as erst our ancestor,","When with his solemn tongue he did discourse","To love-sick Dido's sad attending ear","The story of that baleful burning night","When subtle Greeks surprised King Priam's Troy.","Tell us what Sinon hath bewitched our ears,","Or who hath brought the fatal engine in","That gives our Troy, our Rome, the civil wound.","My heart is not compact of flint nor steel,","Nor can I utter all our bitter grief,","But floods of tears will drown my oratory","And break my utterance, even in the time","When it should move you to attend me most,","And force you to commiseration.","Here's Rome's young captain, let him tell the tale,","While I stand by and weep to hear him speak."],"id":719,"m":"You sad-faced men, people and sons of Rome, split apart by uproar like a flock of birds scattered by winds and fierce, stormy gusts: O, let me teach you how to bind this scattered corn together again into one sheaf, and these broken limbs into one body again, in case Rome becomes her own poison, and she to whom mighty kingdoms bow, like a lost and desperate outcast, shamefully destroys herself. But if my white hair and wrinkles of age, solemn witnesses of real experience, cannot persuade you to listen to me, (To Lucius) speak, Rome's dear friend, as our ancestor Aeneas once did when he solemnly told love-sick Dido, who listened sadly, the story of that deadly burning night when the cunning Greeks took King Priam's Troy by surprise. Tell us what Sinon has bewitched our ears, or who has brought in the deadly wooden horse that gives our Troy, our Rome, this wound of civil war. My heart is not made of flint or steel, and I cannot tell all our bitter grief without floods of tears drowning my speech and breaking off my words just when they should most move you to listen and force you to pity. Here is Rome's young captain. Let him tell the story, while I stand by and weep to hear him speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Then, noble auditory, be it known to you","That Chiron and the damned Demetrius","Were they that murdered our emperor's brother;","And they it were that ravished our sister.","For their fell faults our brothers were beheaded,","Our father's tears despised, and basely cozened","Of that true hand that fought Rome's quarrel out","And sent her enemies unto the grave.","Lastly, myself unkindly banished,","The gates shut on me, and turned weeping out,","To beg relief among Rome's enemies;","Who drowned their enmity in my true tears,","And oped their arms to embrace me as a friend.","I am the turned-forth, be it known to you,","That have preserved her welfare in my blood","And from her bosom took the enemy's point,","Sheathing the steel in my advent'rous body.","Alas, you know I am no vaunter, I;","My scars can witness, dumb although they are,","That my report is just and full of truth.","But soft, methinks I do digress too much,","Citing my worthless praise. O, pardon me;","For when no friends are by, men praise themselves."],"id":720,"m":"Then, noble listeners, know that Chiron and the damned Demetrius were the ones who murdered our emperor's brother, and they were the ones who raped our sister. For their cruel crimes our brothers were beheaded, our father's tears were scorned, and he was basely tricked out of that loyal hand which fought Rome's wars to the end and sent her enemies to the grave. Lastly, I myself was cruelly banished, the gates shut on me, and sent out weeping to beg for help among Rome's enemies, who drowned their hatred in my true tears and opened their arms to embrace me as a friend. Know that I, the outcast, am the one who has protected Rome's welfare with my blood, taking the enemy's sword point from her breast and sheathing the steel in my own daring body. Alas, you know I am no boaster. My scars, though they are dumb, can bear witness that what I say is just and true. But wait, I think I am straying too far, listing my own worthless praises. O, forgive me: when no friends are near, men praise themselves."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Now is my turn to speak. Behold the child.","Of this was Tamora delivered,","The issue of an irreligious Moor,","Chief architect and plotter of these woes.","The villain is alive in Titus' house,","And as he is to witness, this is true.","Now judge what cause had Titus to revenge","These wrongs unspeakable, past patience,","Or more than any living man could bear.","Now have you heard the truth. What say you, Romans?","Have we done aught amiss? Show us wherein,","And, from the place where you behold us pleading,","The poor remainder of Andronici","Will, hand in hand, all headlong hurl ourselves,","And on the ragged stones beat forth our souls,","And make a mutual closure of our house.","Speak, Romans, speak, and if you say we shall,","Lo, hand in hand, Lucius and I will fall."],"id":721,"m":"Now it is my turn to speak. Look at this child. Tamora gave birth to this, the offspring of a godless Moor, the chief designer and plotter of these sorrows. The villain is alive in Titus's house, and, as wretched as he is, he will confirm that this is true. Now judge what cause Titus had to avenge these unspeakable wrongs, beyond patience, more than any living man could bear. Now you have heard the truth. What do you say, Romans? Have we done anything wrong? Show us where, and from this place where you see us pleading, the poor remains of the Andronici will hurl ourselves headlong, hand in hand, and dash out our souls on the jagged stones, and bring our family to an end together. Speak, Romans, speak, and if you say we must, look, Lucius and I will fall hand in hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"AEMILIUS","lines":["Come, come, thou reverend man of Rome,","And bring our emperor gently in thy hand,","Lucius our emperor; for well I know","The common voice do cry it shall be so."],"id":722,"m":"Come, come, you respected man of Rome, and lead our emperor gently by the hand, Lucius our emperor. For I know very well that the voice of the people cries that it shall be so."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMANS","lines":["Lucius, all hail, Rome's royal emperor!"],"id":723,"m":"All hail Lucius, Rome's royal emperor!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Go, go into old Titus' sorrowful house,","And hither hale that misbelieving Moor","To be adjudged some direful slaught'ring death,","As punishment for his most wicked life."],"id":724,"m":"Go, go into old Titus's house of sorrow, and drag that unbelieving Moor out here, to be sentenced to some dreadful, killing death as punishment for his most wicked life."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Attendants. Lucius and Marcus come down from the upper stage.","id":725},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMANS","lines":["Lucius, all hail, Rome's gracious governor!"],"id":726,"m":"All hail Lucius, Rome's gracious ruler!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Thanks, gentle Romans. May I govern so","To heal Rome's harms and wipe away her woe!","But, gentle people, give me aim awhile,","For nature puts me to a heavy task.","Stand all aloof; but, uncle, draw you near","To shed obsequious tears upon this trunk."],"id":727,"m":"Thank you, kind Romans. May I govern so as to heal Rome's injuries and wipe away her sorrow! But, kind people, give me a little time, for nature sets me a heavy task. All stand back. But, uncle, come close and shed mourning tears on this body."},{"t":"sd","text":"He kisses Titus.","id":728},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","cont":1,"lines":["O, take this warm kiss on thy pale cold lips.","These sorrowful drops upon thy blood-stained face,","The last true duties of thy noble son."],"id":729,"m":"O, take this warm kiss on your pale cold lips, and these sorrowful tears on your bloodstained face: the last true duties of your noble son."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["Tear for tear and loving kiss for kiss","Thy brother Marcus tenders on thy lips.","O, were the sum of these that I should pay","Countless and infinite, yet would I pay them."],"id":730,"m":"Your brother Marcus offers tear for tear and loving kiss for kiss on your lips. O, even if the number I had to pay were countless and endless, still I would pay them."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Come hither, boy; come, come, and learn of us","To melt in showers. Thy grandsire loved thee well.","Many a time he danced thee on his knee,","Sung thee asleep, his loving breast thy pillow;","Many a story hath he told to thee,","And bid thee bear his pretty tales in mind","And talk of them when he was dead and gone."],"id":731,"m":"Come here, boy. Come, come, and learn from us how to melt into tears. Your grandfather loved you dearly. Many times he bounced you on his knee and sang you to sleep with his loving breast as your pillow. He told you many stories, and asked you to remember his pretty tales and talk about them when he was dead and gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCUS","lines":["How many thousand times hath these poor lips,","When they were living, warmed themselves on thine!","O, now, sweet boy, give them their latest kiss.","Bid him farewell; commit him to the grave.","Do them that kindness, and take leave of them."],"id":732,"m":"How many thousand times these poor lips, when they were alive, warmed themselves on yours! O, now, sweet boy, give them their last kiss. Say goodbye to him and lay him in the grave. Do them that kindness, and take your leave of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","lines":["O grandsire, grandsire, e'en with all my heart","Would I were dead, so you did live again!","O Lord, I cannot speak to him for weeping;","My tears will choke me if I ope my mouth."],"id":733,"m":"O grandfather, grandfather, with all my heart I wish I were dead, if only you could live again! O Lord, I can't speak to him for crying. My tears will choke me if I open my mouth."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Attendants with Aaron.","id":734},{"t":"sp","who":"AEMILIUS","lines":["You sad Andronici, have done with woes.","Give sentence on the execrable wretch","That hath been breeder of these dire events."],"id":735,"m":"You grieving Andronici, put an end to your sorrows. Pass sentence on the hateful wretch who has been the cause of these dreadful events."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Set him breast-deep in earth and famish him;","There let him stand and rave and cry for food.","If anyone relieves or pities him,","For the offence he dies. This is our doom.","Some stay to see him fastened in the earth."],"id":736,"m":"Bury him chest-deep in the earth and starve him. Let him stand there and rave and cry for food. If anyone helps or pities him, he dies for the offence. This is our sentence. Some of you stay and see him fixed in the earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"AARON","lines":["Ah, why should wrath be mute and fury dumb?","I am no baby, I, that with base prayers","I should repent the evils I have done.","Ten thousand worse than ever yet I did","Would I perform, if I might have my will.","If one good deed in all my life I did,","I do repent it from my very soul."],"id":737,"m":"Ah, why should anger be silent and fury dumb? I am no baby, to repent the evil I have done with snivelling prayers. I would do ten thousand worse things than I ever did, if I could have my way. If I ever did one good deed in all my life, I repent it from the bottom of my soul."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Some loving friends convey the emperor hence,","And give him burial in his father's grave.","My father and Lavinia shall forthwith","Be closed in our household's monument.","As for that ravenous tiger, Tamora,","No funeral rite, nor man in mournful weed,","No mournful bell shall ring her burial;","But throw her forth to beasts and birds of prey.","Her life was beastly and devoid of pity;","And being dead, let birds on her take pity."],"id":738,"m":"Let some loving friends carry the emperor away and bury him in his father's grave. My father and Lavinia shall be laid at once in our family's tomb. As for that greedy tiger, Tamora, no funeral rite, no man in mourning clothes, no mournful bell shall ring at her burial, but throw her out to the beasts and birds of prey. Her life was beastly and without pity, and now she is dead, let the birds take pity on her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":739}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Titus Andronicus","group":"The Andronici","desc":"Rome's great general, surnamed Pius for his devotion to the gods and the state. He returns after ten years of war against the Goths with four living sons, having buried twenty-one. He sacrifices Tamora's eldest son, refuses the empire and gives it to Saturninus, and kills his own son Mutius for defying him. Then he loses almost everything: two sons beheaded on a false charge, his daughter mutilated, his last son banished, his own hand tricked away. Grief drives him to the edge of madness, or into a performance of it, and he takes a revenge as cruel as anything done to him, feeding Tamora her own sons before he kills her."},{"name":"Lavinia","group":"The Andronici","desc":"Titus's only daughter, betrothed to Bassianus. Saturninus first chooses her as his empress, but Bassianus carries her off. In the forest Chiron and Demetrius murder her husband in front of her, rape her, and cut off her hands and tongue so that she cannot name them. Silent for the rest of the play, she finds a way to reveal the truth through Ovid's story of Philomela and by writing in the sand with a staff. She holds the basin for her rapists' blood, and her father kills her at the final banquet."},{"name":"Marcus Andronicus","group":"The Andronici","desc":"Titus's brother, a tribune of the people. He offers Titus the empire at the start and is the steady, reasoning voice of the family: he pleads for Mutius's burial, finds Lavinia in the forest, works out how she can write, and kills a fly. At the end he speaks to the people of Rome, presents Aaron's child as evidence, and helps make Lucius emperor."},{"name":"Lucius","group":"The Andronici","desc":"Titus's eldest surviving son. He demands the sacrifice of Alarbus, defends Lavinia's elopement, and is banished for trying to rescue his brothers. He goes to the Goths, raises an army, and captures Aaron. At the banquet he kills Saturninus to avenge his father, and he is proclaimed emperor. His first acts are to bury his family, sentence Aaron to starve and throw Tamora's body to the birds."},{"name":"Young Lucius","group":"The Andronici","desc":"Lucius's son, a boy. He runs from Lavinia when she chases after his books, then helps uncover the truth. He carries Titus's weapons and Horace verse to Chiron and Demetrius, and weeps over his grandfather's body at the end."},{"name":"Mutius","group":"The Andronici","desc":"Titus's youngest son. He blocks his father's way so that Bassianus can escape with Lavinia, and Titus kills him on the spot. His brothers and uncle have to beg for him to be buried in the family tomb."},{"name":"Quintus and Martius","group":"The Andronici","desc":"Two of Titus's sons. Aaron leads them to the pit where Bassianus's body lies; Martius falls in and Quintus is pulled in after him. Framed by Aaron's forged letter and buried gold, they are condemned for the murder. Their heads are sent back to Titus with his own severed hand."},{"name":"Publius","group":"The Andronici","desc":"Marcus's son. He humours Titus when he shoots arrows to the gods, and later binds Chiron and Demetrius for Titus to kill. Titus's kinsmen Sempronius, Caius and Valentine help him."},{"name":"Tamora","group":"The Goths","desc":"Queen of the Goths, brought to Rome as Titus's captive. When Titus ignores her plea and sacrifices her eldest son, she vows revenge. Saturninus makes her his empress, and she uses her new power to destroy the Andronici: she lets her sons rape Lavinia, helps frame Quintus and Martius, and plays the peacemaker in public. Her secret lover is Aaron, and their baby exposes them. She disguises herself as Revenge to trap Titus, and is tricked into eating her own sons."},{"name":"Aaron","group":"The Goths","desc":"A Moor, Tamora's lover. He is the play's master plotter: he sets Chiron and Demetrius on Lavinia, writes the letter and buries the gold that frame Titus's sons, and tricks Titus into cutting off his hand. He takes delight in evil and boasts of it. Yet he fiercely protects his baby son against everyone who wants it dead, and he trades his confession for the child's life. Buried up to his chest and left to starve, he regrets only that he cannot do ten thousand more evil deeds."},{"name":"Chiron and Demetrius","group":"The Goths","desc":"Tamora's two surviving sons. They quarrel over Lavinia until Aaron shows them how to share her. They stab Bassianus, rape Lavinia and mock her mutilation. They fail to understand Titus's warning verse from Horace. Disguised as Rape and Murder to help their mother trick Titus, they are bound, killed and baked into a pie."},{"name":"Alarbus","group":"The Goths","desc":"Tamora's eldest son. He does not speak. Lucius takes him away and sacrifices him to the spirits of Titus's dead sons, which starts the cycle of revenge."},{"name":"Saturninus","group":"Rome","desc":"The late emperor's elder son, who claims the throne by birth. Titus's support makes him emperor, and he chooses Lavinia as his bride. When Bassianus takes her, he turns on the Andronici at once and marries Tamora instead. He is quick to anger, easily led by his wife, and afraid of the people's love for Lucius. He kills Titus at the banquet and is killed by Lucius."},{"name":"Bassianus","group":"Rome","desc":"Saturninus's younger brother, who asks to be elected for his virtue. He is betrothed to Lavinia and claims her when his brother wants her. In the forest he insults Tamora for her affair with Aaron, and her sons stab him to death and throw his body into a pit."},{"name":"Aemilius","group":"Rome","desc":"A Roman noble. He warns Saturninus that Lucius is marching on Rome with the Goths, carries the emperor's offer of a meeting, and at the end is the first to call for Lucius to be emperor."},{"name":"The Clown","group":"Others","desc":"A countryman taking two pigeons to court to settle a quarrel. Titus pays him to deliver a petition with a knife wrapped in it to the emperor, and Saturninus has him hanged."},{"name":"The Nurse","group":"Others","desc":"Tamora's nurse, who brings Aaron his newborn black son with the empress's order to kill it. Aaron kills her to keep the birth secret."},{"name":"The Goths","group":"Others","desc":"Lucius's army. The Goth soldiers who once feared Titus now follow his son; one of them captures Aaron and the baby at a ruined monastery."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"In Rome, the late emperor's sons Saturninus and Bassianus compete for the throne until Marcus announces that the people have chosen his brother Titus, returning in triumph from war with the Goths. Titus buries his dead sons and, ignoring Tamora's plea for mercy, sacrifices her eldest son Alarbus. He refuses the crown and gives his vote to Saturninus, who chooses Lavinia as empress. Bassianus carries Lavinia off, and Titus kills his son Mutius for blocking his way. Saturninus marries Tamora instead. She persuades him to pardon the Andronici in public while she privately vows to destroy them, and Titus invites the court to a hunt.","key":["Titus's 'cruel, irreligious piety' in sacrificing Alarbus gives Tamora a reason for revenge before any Andronicus is harmed.","Titus kills his own son for the sake of a promise to the emperor, which shows how rigid his idea of honour is.","Tamora's aside ('I'll find a day to massacre them all') shows the gap between her public mercy and her private hatred."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do the Andronici sacrifice Alarbus?","o":["As a punishment for treason to Rome","To satisfy the spirits of Titus's dead sons","To please the new emperor","Because Tamora asked them to"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Titus support for emperor?","o":["Bassianus, the younger brother","Lucius","Saturninus","Himself"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Titus kill Mutius?","o":["Mutius blocks his way as he chases Lavinia","Mutius insults the new emperor to his face","Mutius attacks Lucius in the street with a sword","Mutius sides with the Goths"],"a":0},{"q":"How many sons has Titus lost in the wars?","o":["Twenty-one of his twenty-five","Two of his four sons, both young","All of his sons but young Lucius","Five of his sons in a single battle"],"a":0},{"q":"Why does Tamora tell Saturninus to pardon the Andronici?","o":["Because Titus has given him the crown of Rome","Because the people of Rome would rise up","Because Lavinia has begged him for mercy","So she can take her revenge on them later"],"a":3}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Alone, Aaron celebrates Tamora's rise and plans to rise with her as her lover. Chiron and Demetrius draw swords over which of them will have Lavinia. Aaron stops them and points out that she is married to the emperor's brother. Since both want her, he suggests they take her by force during the hunt, in the lonely parts of the forest, and promises to tell Tamora the plan.","key":["Aaron's first speech shows his ambition and his power over Tamora ('fettered in amorous chains').","The brothers' 'love' is only lust, and Aaron turns their quarrel into a plot to share a rape.","Aaron contrasts the court, 'full of tongues, of eyes and ears', with the deaf, pitiless woods."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do Chiron and Demetrius quarrel?","o":["Over who will be emperor","Over who loves Lavinia more","Over their mother's marriage","Over Aaron's gold"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Aaron suggest?","o":["That they woo Lavinia with gifts and fine words at court","That they fight a duel for her in the forest at dawn","That they take Lavinia by force at the hunt","That they kill Bassianus at the palace gate tonight"],"a":2},{"q":"Which famous chaste Roman wife does Aaron compare Lavinia to?","o":["Cornelia","Lucrece","Dido","Hecuba"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Aaron say Tamora's rise means for him?","o":["He will be sent away from Rome at last","He will become the next emperor himself","He will marry Lavinia when she is free","He will climb with her as her lover"],"a":3},{"q":"Where does Aaron say Chiron and Demetrius can act unseen?","o":["In the lonely, shady parts of the forest","In the emperor's palace while he sleeps","In Titus's house while he is away hunting","On the road to the Goths' camp at night"],"a":0}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Early in the morning Titus, his sons and Marcus wake the court with hounds and horns for the hunt. Saturninus teases that it is too early for new brides, and Lavinia replies that she has been awake for hours. Titus mentions that his sleep was troubled. Demetrius tells Chiron privately that they are hunting a different prey.","key":["The bright, cheerful opening of the hunt contrasts sharply with what follows.","Titus's troubled sleep is an ominous hint.","Demetrius's 'dainty doe' makes Lavinia the hunted animal."],"quiz":[{"q":"What has troubled Titus?","o":["A bad dream","His sleep in the night","A letter from Lucius","Tamora's frown"],"a":1},{"q":"What do Chiron and Demetrius say they hope to catch?","o":["A spotted panther","A hart","A dainty doe","A wild boar"],"a":2},{"q":"Who boasts about his hunting dogs?","o":["Marcus","Bassianus","Saturninus","Lucius"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Titus wake the court?","o":["With a hunting peal of hounds and horns","With music from the emperor's players","With a cannon shot from the city walls","With a speech in the market place"],"a":0},{"q":"Why does Titus say he is glad of the morning?","o":["It is his birthday","It ends the hunt","It gives him new comfort","It brings his sons home"],"a":2}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Aaron buries a bag of gold under an elder tree. Tamora wants to make love, but Aaron is set on revenge: he tells her Bassianus will die and Lavinia be raped that day, and gives her a letter for the king. Bassianus and Lavinia find Tamora alone with Aaron and mock her. Chiron and Demetrius arrive; Tamora claims the couple lured her there to kill her, and her sons stab Bassianus. Lavinia begs Tamora for pity, then for death, but Tamora hands her over to her sons. Aaron leads Quintus and Martius to the pit, where both fall in on Bassianus's body. He brings Saturninus, and Tamora produces the forged letter. The gold is found, Titus's sons are arrested, and Titus's offer to stand bail is refused.","key":["Tamora's two speeches about the same place (a pleasant shade, then a barren 'detested vale') show her twisting words to suit her purpose.","Lavinia's pleas are refused with the memory of Alarbus: 'I poured forth tears in vain'.","The pit, 'this swallowing womb', is both a grave and a trap, and the Andronici fall into it."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Aaron bury under the tree?","o":["A letter","A bag of gold","A dagger","Lavinia's ring"],"a":1},{"q":"Who stabs Bassianus?","o":["Aaron and Tamora together","Chiron and Demetrius","Quintus and Martius in the pit","Saturninus"],"a":1},{"q":"How are Quintus and Martius framed?","o":["By a forged letter and the buried gold","By a false witness paid by Tamora at court","By Lavinia's signs","By a bloody dagger found in their hands at the pit"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Tamora describe the forest when she is with Aaron?","o":["A dark and fearful place of death","A wild home of tigers and wolves","A cold and empty plain of stones","A cheerful place of birdsong and shade"],"a":3},{"q":"How does Tamora later describe the same place to her sons?","o":["A happy garden full of birdsong","A quiet place for lovers to meet","A green field for a royal hunt","A barren, detested vale of death"],"a":3}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"Chiron and Demetrius bring in Lavinia, whose hands and tongue they have cut off after raping her, and leave her with cruel jokes about how she can never tell. Marcus finds her. In a long speech he slowly realises what has happened, compares her to Philomela, whose tongue was cut out by her rapist Tereus, and takes her to her father.","key":["The brothers' mockery shows that they mutilated her to keep their crime hidden.","Marcus's elaborate speech, full of classical comparisons, is one of the play's most debated passages: tender and beautiful, or strangely distant from the horror?","The Philomela story is introduced here and becomes the key to the plot in Act 4."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Chiron and Demetrius cut off Lavinia's hands and tongue?","o":["To punish Titus","So she could not reveal who raped her","Because Aaron ordered it as a sacrifice","To make her unrecognisable"],"a":1},{"q":"Who finds Lavinia in the forest?","o":["Titus","Lucius","Marcus","Young Lucius"],"a":2},{"q":"Which myth does Marcus compare her to?","o":["Philomela and Tereus","Dido and Aeneas","Actaeon and Diana","Hecuba"],"a":0},{"q":"What do Chiron and Demetrius tell Lavinia to do with no hands?","o":["Write the story of what was done","Go home, call for water and wash","Pray to the gods for her revenge","Wait in the woods for her husband"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Marcus compare Lavinia's bleeding mouth to?","o":["A red rose in a field of white snow","A river running down to the sea","A fire burning in the cold night","A bubbling fountain stirred with wind"],"a":3}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Titus lies in the street pleading with the judges for his condemned sons, but they pass him by. Lucius, banished for trying to rescue them, tells him he is talking to stones, and Titus calls Rome 'a wilderness of tigers'. Marcus brings Lavinia, and the family share their grief. Aaron arrives with a false offer: the emperor will spare the sons if one of the Andronici sends his hand. While Marcus and Lucius fetch an axe, Titus has Aaron cut off his own hand. A messenger returns it with his sons' heads. Titus laughs, swears revenge, and sends Lucius to raise an army among the Goths.","key":["Titus's grief grows in images of rain, sea and flood until he says 'I am the sea'.","The 'hand' bargain is the cruellest trick in the play, and Aaron enjoys it openly in an aside.","Titus's laugh, 'I have not another tear to shed', marks the turn from suffering to revenge."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Lucius banished?","o":["For killing Mutius in the Pantheon","For trying to rescue his brothers","For insulting Tamora before the whole court","For marrying a Goth"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Aaron say the emperor wants in exchange for the sons?","o":["A great ransom in gold coin","An Andronicus's hand","Lavinia, as a slave in the palace","Titus's sword"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the messenger bring back?","o":["A pardon","Titus's hand and the two sons' heads","A letter from Tamora","Lucius's banishment order"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Titus say he tells his sorrows to the stones?","o":["Stones can carry his words to Rome","Stones are kinder than the tribunes","He has nobody left in the family","The judges have forbidden his speech"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Titus laugh when the heads come back?","o":["He has no more tears to shed","He thinks it is a cruel joke","He has gone mad with sorrow","He knows they are not his sons"],"a":0}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"At a meal in Titus's house, Titus tries to interpret Lavinia's signs and promises to learn her silent language. Young Lucius is upset by the grief around him. When Marcus kills a fly, Titus scolds him for murdering an innocent creature; when Marcus says it looked like Aaron, Titus stabs at it in delight. Marcus decides grief has unhinged him. Titus takes Lavinia and the boy off to read old stories.","key":["Titus's promise to 'wrest an alphabet' from Lavinia's gestures prepares for the way she reveals the truth.","The fly scene swings from tenderness to violence in moments and is often read as a sign of Titus's madness.","This scene appears only in the Folio of 1623, not in the earlier printed editions."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Marcus kill at the table?","o":["A spider","A fly","A wasp","A mouse"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Titus change his mind about the fly?","o":["It was poisonous","Marcus says it looked like Aaron","Lavinia signals that it was evil","Young Lucius asks him to"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Titus decide to do after the meal?","o":["Write a long letter of protest to the emperor","Read sad stories with Lavinia and the boy","Shoot arrows with messages at the court of the gods","Join Lucius"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Titus tell Marcus not to say?","o":["The name of Lavinia","The word 'hands'","The emperor's name","The word 'Rome'"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Titus say about the fly's family?","o":["It was a pest that deserved to die a death","It belonged to the empress and her sons","It had a father and mother who will mourn it","It came from the Goths' camp with Aaron"],"a":2}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Lavinia chases Young Lucius until he drops his books. She turns the pages of Ovid's Metamorphoses to the story of Philomela, showing that she was raped in the woods. Marcus writes his name in the sand with a staff held in his mouth, and Lavinia copies him, writing 'Stuprum' (rape) and the names Chiron and Demetrius. Marcus leads the family in a vow of revenge. Titus warns that Tamora is powerful and plans to send her sons a message.","key":["Lavinia, silenced, makes a book speak for her: Ovid's myth tells her story.","Writing in the sand with a staff is a powerful stage image of the truth forcing its way out.","Titus is cautious, not mad here: he knows the 'dam' (Tamora) will wake if they hunt her cubs."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which book does Lavinia use?","o":["Virgil's Aeneid, in Latin","Ovid's Metamorphoses","Cicero's Orator, in Latin","Horace's Odes"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Lavinia write the names?","o":["With a pen held in her teeth","With a staff guided by her mouth and stumps","By pointing at letters","With her blood"],"a":1},{"q":"What does 'Stuprum' mean?","o":["Murder","Revenge","Rape","Treason"],"a":2},{"q":"Why is Young Lucius frightened of Lavinia?","o":["She follows him everywhere","She has struck him with a staff","She is shouting at him angrily","She has torn up all his books"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Titus decide to do with the boy?","o":["Send him off to his father among the Goths","Arm him and send him with gifts to the brothers","Keep him locked up in the house for safety","Teach him to read Lavinia's signs for him"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Young Lucius delivers weapons from Titus to Chiron and Demetrius, wrapped in a Latin verse from Horace. They miss its meaning, but Aaron realises Titus knows their guilt. A nurse brings Tamora's newborn baby, which is black: Aaron is the father, and Tamora has ordered him to kill it. Aaron defends his son fiercely, holding off Chiron and Demetrius with his sword. He kills the nurse to keep the secret, plans to swap the baby for a white one, and sets off to the Goths with his son.","key":["The Horace verse ('the upright man needs no Moorish javelins') is a coded accusation; only Aaron understands it.","Aaron's defence of his child and of black skin ('Coal-black is better than another hue') gives him a new, surprising depth.","He kills the nurse with a joke ('Wheak, wheak!'), which shows that his love for his son and his cruelty to others exist together."],"quiz":[{"q":"What message comes with Titus's weapons?","o":["A verse from Horace","A letter of thanks","A challenge to a duel","A list of their crimes"],"a":0},{"q":"What has Tamora ordered Aaron to do with the baby?","o":["Hide it among the Goths","Kill it","Pass it off as the emperor's","Bring it to court"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Aaron kill the nurse?","o":["She insulted him","She tried to take the baby","To keep the birth secret","Tamora ordered it"],"a":2},{"q":"What does the Horace verse mean?","o":["The upright man needs no weapons","Death comes to all men at last","Love conquers all things alike","Rome is the mistress of the world"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Aaron plan to hide the birth?","o":["Swap the baby for Muliteus's white newborn","Send the baby away to the Goths by night","Tell the emperor the child was born dead","Hide the baby in Titus's house in secret"],"a":0}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Titus, saying that Justice has left the earth, has his kinsmen shoot arrows with petitions to the gods. Marcus makes sure they land in the emperor's court. A clown passes with two pigeons, and Titus pays him to deliver a petition to the emperor, with a knife wrapped inside it.","key":["'Terras Astraea reliquit' (Justice has left the earth) sums up Titus's view of Rome.","Marcus and Publius humour Titus, but Marcus also turns the arrows into a public attack on the emperor.","The Clown's innocent jokes bring comedy to a dark scene, and send him to his death."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Titus write on the arrows?","o":["Insults to the emperor","Petitions to the gods","Lavinia's story","Messages to Lucius"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Marcus aim the arrows?","o":["At the moon and the stars above","Into the emperor's court","At Tamora's own window in the palace","Over the city wall"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Titus wrap in the Clown's petition?","o":["A knife","Gold","A lock of hair","A ring"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Titus say about Justice?","o":["She lives with the emperor","She has left the earth","She is asleep in the woods","She is waiting for his sons"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Pluto send word that Justice is?","o":["In the emperor's court with Saturninus","In the Senate house among the tribunes","In the Goths' camp with the army of Lucius","With Jove in heaven, or somewhere else"],"a":3}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Saturninus rages at the arrows and has the Clown hanged for bringing Titus's petition. Aemilius brings news that Lucius is marching on Rome at the head of a Goth army, and Saturninus admits that the people love Lucius. Tamora calms him and promises to charm Titus into persuading Lucius to stop. Aemilius is sent to ask Lucius for a meeting at his father's house.","key":["Saturninus is weak and frightened under pressure; Tamora is the real ruler.","Tamora's image of the eagle that can silence the little birds shows her confidence in her own power.","Her plan to 'enchant' Titus with sweet words sets up the trap that will catch her."],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to the Clown?","o":["He is rewarded","He is hanged","He escapes","He joins Lucius"],"a":1},{"q":"What news does Aemilius bring?","o":["Aaron has fled the city with his child in the night","Lucius leads a Goth army towards Rome","Titus is dead of grief at his own house","The people have rebelled against the emperor's rule"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Tamora arrange the meeting with Lucius?","o":["At the palace","At Titus's house","In the forest","At the Goth camp"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is Saturninus afraid of Lucius?","o":["Lucius has more soldiers than all of Rome","Lucius has a letter that could ruin him","Lucius has taken Tamora's sons as hostages","The people love Lucius and want him as emperor"],"a":3},{"q":"How does Tamora say she will deal with Titus?","o":["Send her sons to kill him in his sleep","Ask Aaron to poison the old man's wine","Bribe him with a great sum of gold","Flatter him with words sweeter than honey"],"a":3}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Lucius addresses his Goth army. A Goth brings in Aaron and his baby, captured at a ruined monastery. Lucius orders them both hanged, the child first, but Aaron offers to reveal everything if Lucius swears to save the baby. Lucius swears, and Aaron confesses the murder of Bassianus, the rape, the forged letter and the trick with Titus's hand, boasting of a lifetime of evil and regretting only that he cannot do more. Aemilius arrives with the emperor's request for a meeting, and Lucius agrees.","key":["Aaron, who believes in no god, makes Lucius swear by his own: he uses Lucius's conscience against him.","His confession ties together every crime of the play and proudly claims them.","Lucius keeps his oath: the child's survival is one of the open questions of the ending."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is Aaron captured?","o":["In Tamora's chamber","At a ruined monastery","At Titus's house","On the road to Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Aaron want in return for his confession?","o":["His freedom from the Goths","His child's life","A quick and painless death","Tamora's safety"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Aaron say he regrets?","o":["Killing the nurse who saw his child at the palace","That he cannot do ten thousand more evil deeds","Loving Tamora more than his own life and honour","Betraying Chiron and Demetrius to the Goths"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Aaron make Lucius swear?","o":["He threatens to kill the baby himself first","He says Lucius believes in a god and keeps oaths","He offers to lead the Goths to the gold","He promises to fight on the side of Lucius"],"a":1},{"q":"Why did Aaron have Titus's hand cut off?","o":["To prove his own loyalty","To save the sons' lives","On Saturninus's order","For sport, as he boasts"],"a":3}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Tamora and her sons come to Titus's door disguised as Revenge, Rape and Murder, believing him mad. Titus plays along and sends Marcus to invite Lucius to a feast with the emperor and empress. He insists that Rape and Murder stay with him. When Tamora leaves, his kinsmen bind and gag Chiron and Demetrius. Titus tells them what they did and how he will repay it, then cuts their throats while Lavinia holds a basin for their blood. He plans to bake their heads into pies and serve them to their mother.","key":["Titus's aside ('I knew them all, though they suppose me mad') reveals that he sees through the disguise, whatever his state of mind.","Tamora acts out a revenge play and walks into a real one.","Titus models his revenge on Ovid: as Procne fed Tereus his son, he will feed Tamora hers, and 'worse than Procne'."],"quiz":[{"q":"How are Tamora and her sons disguised?","o":["As priests","As Revenge, Rape and Murder","As Goth soldiers","As cooks"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Titus ask of 'Revenge'?","o":["That Rape and Murder stay with him","That she kill the emperor for him at once","That she bring Lucius back to Rome from the Goths","That she free his sons"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Lavinia hold while Titus kills the brothers?","o":["A knife","A torch","A basin","Ovid's book"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Titus play along with the disguised Tamora?","o":["He truly believes they are the gods","He knows who they are and plans revenge","He hopes they will help him find Lucius","He is too frightened to send them away"],"a":1},{"q":"What will Titus make from the brothers' bones?","o":["Ashes for their mother's urn","Meal for the hounds in the yard","Dust, to make a paste","A gift to send back to the Goths"],"a":2}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"Lucius hands Aaron over to Marcus. The emperor and empress arrive and Titus, dressed as a cook, serves the feast. He asks whether Virginius was right to kill his raped daughter; when Saturninus says yes, Titus kills Lavinia. He reveals that Chiron and Demetrius are baked in the pie Tamora has eaten and stabs her. Saturninus kills Titus, and Lucius kills Saturninus. Marcus and Lucius explain the family's wrongs to the people and show Aaron's child. Lucius is proclaimed emperor. He mourns his father with Marcus and Young Lucius, sentences Aaron to be buried chest-deep and starved, and orders Tamora's body thrown to the beasts.","key":["Four deaths in a few lines turn the feast into a bloodbath, mirroring the sacrifice of the first scene.","Titus kills Lavinia to end her 'shame' and his sorrow, which modern audiences often find more shocking than any other killing.","The play ends with healing language ('knit again This scattered corn') and with fresh cruelty, so it is hard to tell whether the cycle of revenge is really over."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Titus ask about Virginius?","o":["To test Saturninus's mercy","To justify killing Lavinia","To praise Roman history","To distract Tamora"],"a":1},{"q":"Who kills Titus?","o":["Tamora, with a hidden knife","Lucius, by mistake","Saturninus","Aaron's child's nurse"],"a":2},{"q":"What is Aaron's sentence?","o":["Hanging","Burial chest-deep and starvation","Banishment","Burning"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Titus kill Lavinia?","o":["Her shame and his sorrow should die with her","She asks him for death in front of the court","She has been poisoned by the empress Tamora","She tried to stab the emperor at the feast"],"a":0},{"q":"Who explains the family's wrongs to the people?","o":["Lucius and Marcus","Young Lucius alone","Aemilius and a Goth","Titus's ghost"],"a":0}]}},"notes":{"2":"Saturninus claims the throne by birth ('successive title'): he is the eldest son. Bassianus asks for an election based on merit ('desert'). The play opens with a Rome divided over how a ruler should be chosen.","5":"'Pius' was the surname of Aeneas in Virgil, meaning dutiful to the gods, the state and one's family. Titus's whole tragedy tests that kind of duty.","17":"Priam, King of Troy, had fifty sons, so Titus's twenty-five are 'half the number'. The Styx was the river of the underworld; the unburied dead were believed to wander on its bank.","20":"'Ad manes fratrum' is Latin for 'to the spirits of our brothers'. Lucius treats the human sacrifice as a religious duty, though there is no evidence the real Romans did this.","22":"Tamora's plea is the play's first great appeal for mercy, and it echoes Portia's later 'quality of mercy' speech in The Merchant of Venice. Its refusal starts the chain of revenge.","26":"'Irreligious piety' is an oxymoron: Tamora says the Romans' religious duty is itself godless.","28":"Hecuba, Queen of Troy, took revenge on Polymnestor, the Thracian king who had murdered her son, by blinding him and killing his children. Demetrius hopes the gods will give Tamora the same chance.","40":"Solon, the Athenian lawgiver, said no one should be called happy until they are dead. Marcus means Titus's dead sons are safe from all further bad luck. A 'palliament' is a robe; the Latin 'candidatus' meant 'dressed in white', which is where 'candidate' comes from.","41":"Titus has buried twenty-one sons. He refuses power with a soldier's modesty, but by giving his vote to Saturninus he puts Rome in the hands of the man who will destroy his family.","54":"The Pantheon was the Roman temple to all the gods. Saturninus's choice of Lavinia is a reward to Titus, not a love match, and she has no say in it.","65":"'Suum cuique' means 'to each his own', a principle of Roman law. Marcus uses it to argue that Bassianus is only taking what was already his.","78":"Titus kills his son in a single line, for blocking his way. His loyalty to the emperor matters more to him at this moment than his own child.","88":"A 'stale' is a laughing stock or a decoy. Saturninus instantly turns on the family that made him emperor.","92":"Phoebe (Diana) was the moon goddess. Hymenaeus, or Hymen, was the god of marriage. Saturninus has just met Tamora as his prisoner and now makes her his empress.","117":"Ajax killed himself after losing Achilles' armour to Ulysses ('wise Laertes' son'), who nevertheless persuaded the Greeks to bury him. Marcus uses the story to shame Titus into burying Mutius.","134":"Tamora's aside is the play's first open statement of revenge. In public she plays the merciful peacemaker; in private she plans a massacre.","148":"Prometheus was chained to a rock in the Caucasus mountains for stealing fire from the gods. Semiramis was a legendary Assyrian queen famous for lust and cruelty. Aaron's speech is full of classical learning, which makes him the cleverest character in the play.","166":"'Vulcan's badge' means the cuckold's horns: Vulcan's wife Venus was unfaithful with Mars. Demetrius suggests Lavinia can be seduced as easily as a slice can be stolen from a cut loaf.","175":"Lucrece, a chaste Roman wife, was raped by Tarquin, the king's son, and killed herself; the scandal led to the end of the monarchy in Rome. The 'house of Fame' (rumour) comes from Ovid and Chaucer.","177":"Demetrius's Latin is adapted from Seneca's tragedies: 'right or wrong' and 'I am carried through the Styx and among the ghosts'. Seneca's bloody revenge plays were a model for Titus.","197":"Tamora recalls Dido and Aeneas, who sheltered from a storm in a cave and became lovers (Virgil's Aeneid). That love story ended in desertion and suicide.","198":"Venus is the planet of love; Saturn was believed to make people gloomy and violent. Philomel (Philomela) was raped by Tereus, who cut out her tongue. Aaron gives away the whole plot in advance.","204":"Actaeon saw the goddess Diana bathing; she turned him into a stag and his own hounds tore him apart. Horns were also the sign of a cuckold, so Tamora's threat is also a sexual insult.","206":"The Cimmerians were a legendary people who lived in perpetual darkness. Bassianus insults Aaron's colour, a theme that runs through the play.","213":"Compare Tamora's description of the same place earlier in the scene, full of birdsong and sunshine. Now it is a 'barren detested vale'. The story is a lie to set her sons on Bassianus.","228":"Lavinia argues that the cubs have learned cruelty from their mother, and turns Tamora's own image of the 'tiger' back on her. Rome itself will later be called a 'wilderness of tigers'.","233":"Tamora refuses Lavinia's plea with the memory of her own plea for Alarbus. The two scenes mirror each other: a mother's tears in Act 1, a daughter's in Act 2.","259":"Pyramus and Thisbe were lovers who met at night; finding her bloody cloak, Pyramus thought her dead and killed himself. Cocytus is a river of the underworld. The pit is described like the mouth of hell.","276":"The letter is deliberately vague: it never names the writer. Aaron has made sure the elder tree, the pit and the gold all match its words.","300":"Marcus's speech has divided critics: some find its beautiful images (a 'crimson river', 'lily hands') an attempt to understand an unbearable sight, others find them grotesquely out of place. Cerberus, the three-headed dog of hell, was lulled to sleep by the music of Orpheus, 'the Thracian poet'.","304":"Titus now says he has lost twenty-two sons (Act 1 says twenty-one were buried; Mutius makes twenty-two). The judges ignore him, and the stage direction has him lie down in the street.","315":"'Rome is but a wilderness of tigers' is one of the play's most famous lines: the civilised city has become more savage than the forest.","322":"Throwing a faggot (a bundle of sticks) on burning Troy and adding water to the sea are images of pointless excess. The Nile was famous for flooding.","337":"Ravens were birds of ill omen; larks sang at dawn. Titus mistakes the bringer of disaster for a herald of joy.","350":"Titus's line 'Lend me thy hand, and I will give thee mine' plays grimly on the polite phrase 'give me your hand'. Hands are mentioned more than in any other Shakespeare play.","355":"Aaron says his soul will be as black as his face. The play links his colour to evil, a link that modern productions often question.","361":"Titus's long image of sky, sea and earth turns his grief into a cosmic flood. 'Welkin' means the sky.","371":"Titus's laugh is one of the turning points of the play. Grief is used up, and from now on he plans revenge.","373":"Titus makes the family swear a vow of revenge, then gives each a burden: a head for Marcus, a head and his hand for himself, the hand for Lavinia to carry in her teeth.","375":"Tarquin and his queen were driven out of Rome after the rape of Lucrece. Lucius expects to drive out Saturninus and Tamora the same way.","381":"Aeneas told Dido the story of the fall of Troy. Titus means that talking about hands makes them relive their loss.","390":"The fly scene was added in the Folio. Titus's pity for a fly's 'father and mother' sounds mad, but it comes from a man who has lost his own children.","403":"Cornelia, mother of the Gracchi brothers, was famous in Rome as a devoted teacher of her sons. 'Tully' is Cicero, whose writings on speechmaking were standard schoolbooks.","413":"In Ovid's Metamorphoses, Tereus rapes his sister-in-law Philomela and cuts out her tongue; she weaves the story into a tapestry so that her sister Procne learns the truth. Lavinia uses the same book to tell hers.","423":"'Stuprum' is Latin for rape or sexual dishonour.","425":"Latin, adapted from Seneca: 'Ruler of the great heavens, are you so slow to hear crimes, so slow to see them?'","426":"After Lucrece's rape, Junius Brutus swore with her husband and father to drive out the Tarquins. The vow led to the founding of the Roman Republic.","427":"The Sibyl of Cumae wrote her prophecies on leaves, which the wind scattered. Titus wants the truth carved in brass so that it cannot be lost.","445":"Latin from Horace's Odes: 'The man of upright life, free from crime, needs no Moorish javelins or bow.' The verse is a coded accusation, and the 'Moorish' javelins point at Aaron.","472":"'Zounds' is an oath meaning 'by God's wounds'. Aaron's first words to his child are tender, and his first words to the nurse are furious.","485":"Enceladus was one of the giants (sons of Typhon) who fought the gods; Alcides is Hercules. Aaron claims black is the best colour because, unlike white skin, it cannot blush or be stained.","492":"Aaron mocks Chiron's blush: fair skin betrays guilt, black skin hides it.","514":"'Terras Astraea reliquit': Astraea, goddess of justice, left the earth (from Ovid). Pluto was the god of the underworld.","522":"Each arrow is addressed to a god. Titus pointedly writes to Saturn, 'not to Saturnine', the emperor's name.","524":"Virgo, Taurus and Aries are signs of the zodiac. The arrows land in the emperor's court, so the joke about the bull's horns is also a cuckold joke at Saturninus.","533":"The Clown mishears Jupiter as 'Jubiter' and 'tribunal plebs' (tribune of the people) as a garbled phrase. His comedy makes his hanging more shocking.","568":"Coriolanus was a banished Roman general who led an enemy army against Rome. Shakespeare later wrote a play about him.","588":"The Goth's story lets the audience hear Aaron talking to his baby when he thinks he is alone, and his words are loving as well as rough.","599":"Aaron calls Lucius's religious practices 'popish', a word from Shakespeare's own time. He uses Lucius's faith to bind him, knowing an oath means everything to him.","605":"Aaron puns cruelly on 'trim' (to cut, to dress) and 'trim sport' (fine fun).","611":"Aaron's catalogue of crimes is borrowed in style from Barabas in Marlowe's The Jew of Malta, a similar stage villain who boasts of his evil.","635":"Hyperion was the sun god. Titus imagines himself as Revenge's servant, trotting beside her chariot all day.","674":"In Ovid, Procne avenged her sister Philomela by killing her son and serving him to his father Tereus. Titus will be 'worse than Procne': he serves Tamora both her sons. A 'coffin' was the word for a pie crust.","676":"At the Centaurs' feast (the wedding of Pirithous) the drunken Centaurs tried to carry off the bride and a bloody battle broke out.","698":"In Roman legend the centurion Virginius killed his daughter to save her from being raped by a corrupt official. Titus changes the story: in his version she has already been raped.","717":"'Meed for meed' means payment for payment. Lucius's rhyming couplet makes the killing sound like justice.","719":"Sinon was the Greek who persuaded the Trojans to take the wooden horse ('the fatal engine') into Troy. Rome's founding legend made the Romans descendants of the Trojans, so Marcus compares Rome's destruction to Troy's.","736":"Lucius's first act as emperor is a slow, cruel execution. The play ends with justice and revenge still hard to tell apart.","738":"Tamora is denied burial, just as Titus once denied it to his own son Mutius. The play ends as it began, with an argument about who deserves to be buried."},"gloss":{"patricians":"noblemen of Rome","successive":"by right of succession","diadem":"crown","continence":"self-control","empery":"empire, rule","accited":"summoned","affy":"trust","circumscribed":"confined, hemmed in","bark":"ship","fraught":"cargo","lading":"load, cargo","Styx":"river of the underworld","receptacle":"container, store","shadows":"ghosts","prodigies":"evil omens, unnatural events","Patient":"calm (as a verb)","Scythia":"region thought of as savage and barbarous","withal":"as well","quit":"repay","'larums":"trumpet calls","obsequies":"funeral rites","cordial":"comfort, restoring medicine","palliament":"robe","candidatus":"candidate, dressed in white","suffrages":"votes","gratulate":"greet with joy","commonweal":"the state","espouse":"marry","imperious":"imperial, commanding","ensigns":"banners, emblems","fealty":"loyalty owed to a lord","Ransomless":"without ransom","avaunt":"get away","stale":"laughing stock","bandy":"brawl, quarrel","ruffle":"swagger, make trouble","Hymenæus":"god of marriage","re-edified":"rebuilt","servitors":"servants","crest":"top of a helmet; honour","dismall'st":"most dreadful","dumps":"low spirits","remunerate":"reward","meed":"reward, payment","forfend":"forbid","incorporate":"made one with, absorbed into","tractable":"obedient, manageable","gramercy":"many thanks","bonjour":"good day (French)","Olympus":"mountain home of the gods","pitch":"height (in falconry)","weeds":"clothes","wanton":"play amorously","Semiramis":"lustful legendary Assyrian queen","shipwrack":"shipwreck","overween":"presume too far","lath":"wooden sword","wot":"know","brabble":"squabble","jet":"encroach, trespass","controlment":"restraint","youngling":"youngster","brook":"tolerate","shive":"slice","square":"quarrel; also shape, frame","policy":"cunning","languishment":"lovesickness","strike":"shoot (a deer)","engines":"plots, schemes","ruthless":"pitiless","Uncouple":"let the hounds loose","bay":"barking of hounds; the corner where a hunted animal turns","peal":"loud ringing","chase":"hunting ground","inherit":"take possession of","chequered":"patterned like a chessboard","Saturn":"planet thought to cause gloom and violence","venereal":"to do with love (Venus)","Philomel":"Philomela, raped by Tereus, who cut out her tongue","parcel":"part, portion","Unfurnished":"without","Dian":"Diana, goddess of the hunt and of chastity","Actaeon":"hunter turned into a stag by Diana","horning":"making a cuckold","swarthy":"dark-skinned","Cimmerian":"one of a people living in darkness","rated":"scolded","ticed":"enticed","baleful":"deadly","mistletoe":"plant thought to be poisonous","urchins":"hedgehogs","yew":"tree of churchyards and death","poniard":"dagger","minion":"hussy","nice-preserved":"carefully guarded","honesty":"chastity","dam":"mother (of animals)","obdurate":"hard-hearted","Fond":"foolish","trull":"whore","berayed":"smeared","taper":"candle","Cocytus":"river of the underworld","complot":"plot, conspiracy","timeless":"untimely","writ":"written document","fell":"cruel, deadly","whelps":"puppies, cubs","scrowl":"scrawl","bewray":"reveal","Tereus":"king who raped Philomela and cut out her tongue","sampler":"piece of needlework","Cerberus":"three-headed dog guarding hell","meads":"meadows","urns":"vessels; here, eyes","bootless":"useless","Nilus":"the Nile","engine":"instrument","unrecuring":"incurable","brinish":"salty","spurn":"kick, blow","welkin":"sky","coil":"turmoil, fuss","tributary":"paid as tribute","tofore":"before","requite":"repay","passionate":"express with passion","wreathen":"twisted, folded","dote":"be foolish, go senile","martyred":"tortured, mutilated","meshed":"mashed, brewed","wrest":"wring out","sapling":"young tree","cloyed":"sickened by excess","insult":"triumph, gloat","Tully":"Cicero","quotes":"notes, marks","Stuprum":"rape (Latin)","fere":"husband","gad":"spike, point","Sibyl":"prophetess who wrote on leaves","bondmen":"slaves","ecstasy":"madness","deciphered":"found out","appointed":"equipped","grammar":"Latin schoolbook","blowse":"chubby, red-faced girl","broach":"spit, skewer","Enceladus":"giant who fought the gods","Alcides":"Hercules","sanguine":"red-faced","lave":"wash","maugre":"in spite of","escape":"misdeed, slip","ignomy":"ignominy, disgrace","leer":"complexion","enfranchised":"freed","physic":"medicine","Astraea":"goddess of justice","mattock":"pickaxe","Pluto":"god of the underworld","Acheron":"river of the underworld","Cyclops":"one-eyed giants","Jubiter":"the Clown's mistake for Jupiter","gibbet-maker":"gallows-maker","emperal":"the Clown's mistake for imperial","supplication":"petition","wreaks":"acts of revenge","libelling":"slandering","blazoning":"proclaiming","gloze":"flatter, smooth over","e'en":"evening","gathered head":"raised an army","amain":"at full speed","Coriolanus":"banished Roman who led an enemy army against Rome","successantly":"in succession; with success","scath":"harm","slip":"offshoot","wall-eyed":"glaring","popish":"Catholic (a hostile word)","bauble":"jester's stick","insatiate":"insatiable","luxurious":"lustful","codding":"lustful","sounded":"fainted (swooned)","haystalks":"haystacks","habiliment":"clothing","Rapine":"rape, plunder","palfreys":"saddle horses","Hyperion":"the sun god","surance":"assurance","waggoner":"charioteer","proportion":"shape","abide":"stay","forbear":"stop","coffin":"pie crust","pasties":"pies","Procne":"Philomela's sister, who fed Tereus his son","officious":"busy, eager","Centaurs":"half-horse creatures who brawled at a wedding feast","parle":"talk, dispute","Virginius":"Roman who killed his daughter to save her honour","Sinon":"Greek who tricked the Trojans into taking the wooden horse","chaps":"cracks, wrinkles","auditory":"listeners","cozened":"cheated","vaunter":"boaster","closure":"end","hale":"drag","obsequious":"dutiful in mourning","famish":"starve","weed":"clothes"}};
const PARTS={"5":[[0,6,"Princes, you who are competing for power through parties and supporters: know that the people of Rome, whom we tribunes represent, have chosen by a common vote, in this election for emperor, Andronicus, surnamed Pius (the dutiful), for his many great services to Rome."],[7,17,"There is no nobler man or braver warrior alive within the city walls today. The senate has called him home from tiring wars against the barbarous Goths, a strong and warlike nation that he and his sons, a terror to our enemies, have conquered. Ten years have passed since he took up Rome's cause and began punishing our enemies' pride. Five times he has come back to Rome bleeding, carrying his brave sons home from the battlefield in coffins."],[18,27,"And now at last good Andronicus, famous Titus, returns to Rome loaded with the spoils of honour, at the height of his success in war. In the name of the man you would have succeed your father, and out of respect for the Capitol and the senate, which you claim to honour, we ask you to withdraw, reduce your forces, dismiss your followers, and argue your claims peacefully and humbly, as candidates should."]],"17":[[0,8,"Hail, Rome, victorious even in your mourning clothes! Like a ship that has unloaded her cargo and returns with a precious load to the harbour where she first raised her anchor, Andronicus comes home, crowned with laurel, to greet his country again with tears, tears of real joy at his return to Rome. Jupiter, great protector of this Capitol, look kindly on the rites we are about to perform."],[9,19,"Romans, of my twenty-five brave sons, half as many as King Priam had, look at what is left, the living and the dead. Let Rome reward those who survive with love. These, whom I bring to their final home, let Rome reward with burial among their ancestors. Here the Goths have let me put away my sword. Titus, you unkind father, careless of your own, why do you leave your sons unburied, hovering on the dreadful bank of the river Styx? Make way to lay them beside their brothers."]],"22":[[0,9,"Wait, Roman brothers! Gracious conqueror, victorious Titus, pity the tears I shed, a mother's tears of grief for her son. If your sons were ever dear to you, then think that my son is just as dear to me. Is it not enough that we are brought to Rome to decorate your triumph, captives under your Roman yoke? Must my sons be slaughtered in the streets as well, for fighting bravely for their country?"],[10,16,"If fighting for king and country was a sacred duty for your sons, it was the same for mine. Andronicus, do not stain your tomb with blood. Do you want to come close to the nature of the gods? Then come close to them by being merciful. Sweet mercy is the true badge of nobility. Thrice-noble Titus, spare my first-born son."]],"28":[[0,9,"Don't compare Scythia with ambitious Rome. Alarbus goes to his rest, and we live on to tremble under Titus's threatening look. So, madam, stand firm, but hope as well that the same gods who gave Hecuba, Queen of Troy, the chance of sharp revenge on the Thracian tyrant in his tent may favour Tamora, Queen of the Goths (when the Goths were still Goths and Tamora was queen), to repay her enemies for these bloody wrongs."]],"40":[[0,6,"And welcome, nephews, home from successful wars: those of you who survive, and those who sleep in glory. Noble lords, your fortunes are equal in this, that you all drew your swords in your country's service. But this funeral procession is a safer triumph. It has reached the happiness Solon spoke of, which no one can call happy until death, and it triumphs over chance in the bed of honour."],[7,14,"Titus Andronicus, the people of Rome, whose friend in justice you have always been, send you this white and spotless robe by me, their tribune and the one they trust. They name you as a candidate for emperor alongside these sons of our late emperor. Be a candidate, then, and put it on, and help to give headless Rome a head."]],"41":[[0,5,"A better head than mine suits her glorious body, a head that does not shake with age and weakness. What, should I put on this robe and trouble you? Be chosen with proclamations today, give up power and die tomorrow, and leave you all with fresh business?"],[6,13,"Rome, I have been your soldier for forty years. I have led my country's armies successfully, and I have buried twenty-one brave sons, knighted on the battlefield and killed bravely in arms, fighting for their noble country. Give me a staff of honour for my old age, but not a sceptre to rule the world. The man who held it last, lords, held it with honour."]],"54":[[0,9,"Titus Andronicus, for the favours you have done me in my election today, I thank you as part of what you deserve, and I will repay your kindness with deeds. And to begin with, Titus, to raise up your name and your honourable family, I will make Lavinia my empress, Rome's royal mistress and mistress of my heart, and marry her in the sacred Pantheon. Tell me, Andronicus, does this proposal please you?"]],"58":[[0,9,"A fine lady, believe me, with the very looks I would choose if I were choosing again. Brighten up, fair queen, and clear that cloudy face. Though the fortunes of war have brought about this change in your mood, you have not come to be mocked in Rome. You will be treated like a princess in every way. Trust my word, and do not let unhappiness crush all your hopes. Madam, the man who comforts you can make you greater than the Queen of the Goths. Lavinia, you are not offended by this?"]],"92":[[0,6,"And so, lovely Tamora, Queen of the Goths, who outshine the finest ladies of Rome like the stately moon goddess among her nymphs: if you are pleased with this sudden choice of mine, look, I choose you, Tamora, to be my bride, and will make you Empress of Rome. Speak, Queen of the Goths, do you approve my choice?"],[7,13,"And here I swear by all the Roman gods, since the priest and holy water are so near, and the candles are burning so brightly, and everything stands ready for the god of marriage, that I will not greet the streets of Rome again or climb up to my palace until I lead my bride away from this place as my wife."]],"130":[[0,5,"My lord, I must answer for what I have done as best I can, and I will, with my life. I only want your grace to know this much: by all the duty I owe Rome, this noble gentleman, Lord Titus here, has been wronged in his reputation and his honour."],[6,12,"To rescue Lavinia for you, he killed his youngest son with his own hand, out of loyalty to you, and furious at being thwarted in the gift he freely gave. So take him back into favour, Saturnine. In all his deeds he has shown himself a father and a friend to you and to Rome."]],"134":[[0,7,"Not so, my lord. The gods of Rome forbid that I should be the cause of your dishonour! But I dare to vouch on my honour that good Lord Titus is innocent in all this. His fury, which he does not hide, shows how much he is grieving. So at my request look kindly on him. Do not lose so noble a friend over an empty suspicion, or hurt his gentle heart with sour looks."],[8,15,"(Aside, to Saturninus) My lord, take my advice. Let yourself be won over at last. Hide all your grievances and resentment. You have only just been planted on your throne. In case the people, and the lords too, look carefully at the matter, take Titus's side, and overthrow you for ingratitude, which Rome thinks a terrible sin, give way to our pleading, and leave the rest to me."],[16,21,"I'll find a day to massacre them all, and wipe out their party and their family: the cruel father and his treacherous sons, whom I begged for my dear son's life. I'll make them learn what it means to let a queen kneel in the streets and beg for mercy in vain."],[22,24,"(Aloud) Come, come, sweet emperor. Come, Andronicus. Raise up this good old man, and cheer the heart that is dying in the storm of your angry frown."]],"137":[[0,5,"Titus, I am now part of Rome, a Roman happily adopted, and I must advise the emperor for his own good. All quarrels die today, Andronicus. And let it be to my credit, my good lord, that I have reconciled your friends and you."],[6,11,"As for you, Prince Bassianus, I have given my word and promise to the emperor that you will be gentler and more obedient. And do not be afraid, lords, nor you, Lavinia. On my advice, you shall all kneel humbly and ask his majesty's pardon."]],"142":[[0,3,"Marcus, for your sake, and your brother's here, and at the request of my lovely Tamora, I forgive these young men's terrible offences. Stand up."],[4,9,"Lavinia, though you left me like a peasant, I found a friend, and I swore as surely as death that I would not leave the priest a bachelor. Come, if the emperor's court can feast two brides, you are my guest, Lavinia, and your friends too. This shall be a day of reconciliation, Tamora."]],"148":[[0,8,"Now Tamora climbs to the top of Olympus, safe from Fortune's arrows, and sits up high, out of reach of the crack of thunder or the flash of lightning, raised above the threatening reach of pale envy. Just as the golden sun greets the morning, gilds the ocean with its rays, gallops through the zodiac in its glittering chariot and looks down on the highest hills: so does Tamora."],[9,16,"Earthly honour waits on her intelligence, and virtue bows and trembles when she frowns. Then, Aaron, steel your heart and shape your thoughts to rise high with your imperial mistress. Rise to her height, she whom you have long held prisoner in triumph, chained in the chains of love, and bound faster to Aaron's charming eyes than Prometheus is chained to the Caucasus."],[17,24,"Away with the clothes and thoughts of a slave! I will be splendid, and shine in pearl and gold, to serve this new-made empress. Serve, did I say? To play with this queen, this goddess, this Semiramis, this nymph, this siren, who will bewitch Rome's Saturnine and watch him and his state wrecked. Hello! What storm is this?"]],"160":[[0,4,"Get apart, I say! Now, by the gods the warlike Goths worship, this silly squabble will ruin us all. Why, lords, don't you see how dangerous it is to trespass on a prince's rights?"],[5,10,"What, has Lavinia become so loose, or Bassianus so feeble, that quarrels can be started over her love without restraint, justice or revenge? Young lords, be careful! If the empress knew the cause of this discord, the tune would not please her."]],"175":[[0,8,"For shame, be friends, and join together for what you are quarrelling over. Cunning and strategy must get you what you want. You must make up your minds that what you cannot achieve the way you would like, you must achieve however you can. Take it from me: Lucrece was not more chaste than this Lavinia, Bassianus's love. We must take a quicker route than lingering lovesickness, and I have found the way."],[9,16,"My lords, a grand hunt is being planned. The lovely Roman ladies will flock there. The forest paths are wide and roomy, and there are many lonely places that nature has made fit for rape and villainy. Pick out this dainty doe there, then, and bring her down by force if not by words. This way, or not at all, is your hope."],[17,22,"Come, come, we will tell our empress everything we intend. Her mind is devoted to villainy and revenge, and she will sharpen our plans with advice that will stop you fighting each other and lift you both to the height of your wishes."],[23,28,"The emperor's court is like the House of Rumour: the palace is full of tongues, eyes and ears. The woods are pitiless, dreadful, deaf and silent. There, brave boys, speak and strike and take your turns. There satisfy your lust, hidden from heaven's eye, and feast on Lavinia's treasure."]],"181":[[0,9,"The hunt is on, the morning is bright and grey, the fields smell sweet and the woods are green. Let the hounds loose here, and let us make them bark, and wake the emperor and his lovely bride, and rouse the prince, and ring out a hunting peal so the whole court echoes with the noise. Sons, make it your duty, as it is ours, to look after the emperor's person carefully. My sleep was troubled last night, but the dawning day has given me new comfort."]],"197":[[0,10,"My lovely Aaron, why do you look sad when everything is showing off its happiness? The birds sing on every bush, the snakes lie coiled in the cheerful sun, the green leaves quiver in the cool breeze and make a chequered shadow on the ground. Let us sit in their sweet shade, Aaron, and while the chattering echo mocks the hounds, answering the tuneful horns so shrilly that it sounds like two hunts at once, let us sit and listen to their yelping."],[11,19,"And after a struggle like the one the wandering prince Aeneas and Dido are said to have enjoyed, when a lucky storm caught them and they were hidden in a cave that kept their secret, we may lie wrapped in each other's arms, our games over, and fall into a golden sleep, while hounds and horns and sweet singing birds are to us like a nurse's lullaby that sends her baby to sleep."]],"198":[[0,7,"Madam, though Venus rules your desires, Saturn rules mine. What do you think my deadly fixed stare means, my silence and my gloomy melancholy, my fleece of woolly hair uncurling like an adder unrolling to make a fatal strike? No, madam, these are not signs of love."],[8,15,"Vengeance is in my heart and death in my hand. Blood and revenge are hammering in my head. Listen, Tamora, empress of my soul, which hopes for no more heaven than it finds in you: this is the day of doom for Bassianus. His Philomel must lose her tongue today. Your sons will plunder her chastity and wash their hands in Bassianus's blood."],[16,20,"Do you see this letter? Please pick it up and give the king this deadly scroll. Now ask me nothing more. We are being watched. Here comes some of the loot we are hoping for, and they have no idea their lives are about to be destroyed."]],"213":[[0,6,"Don't you think I have reason to look pale? These two lured me here to this place. You can see it is a barren, hateful valley. The trees, though it is summer, are bare and thin, smothered in moss and deadly mistletoe. The sun never shines here, nothing breeds here, except the owl of the night and the raven of death."],[7,17,"And when they showed me this horrible pit, they told me that here, in the dead of night, a thousand devils, a thousand hissing snakes, ten thousand swelling toads and as many hedgehogs make such terrifying, confused cries that any living person who hears them would go mad at once or die suddenly. As soon as they had told this hellish story, they told me they would tie me here to the trunk of a gloomy yew tree and leave me to this miserable death."],[18,24,"And then they called me a foul adulteress, a lustful Goth, and all the bitterest names ever heard of that kind. If you had not come here by wonderful luck, they would have carried out this vengeance on me. Revenge it, if you love your mother's life, or never call yourselves my children again."]],"259":[[0,6,"On his bloody finger he wears a precious ring that lights up the whole hole. Like a candle in a tomb, it shines on the dead man's earthy cheeks and shows the jagged insides of the pit. The moon shone just as pale on Pyramus when he lay at night bathed in a maiden's blood."],[7,10,"O brother, if fear has made you faint, as it has me, help me with your fainting hand out of this cruel, devouring hole, as hateful as the misty mouth of the river Cocytus in hell."]],"276":[[0,7,"(Reads) 'If we fail to catch him neatly, sweet huntsman (it's Bassianus we mean), you just dig the grave for him. You know what we mean. Look for your reward among the nettles at the elder tree which overhangs the mouth of the same pit where we decided to bury Bassianus. Do this, and make us your friends for ever.'"],[8,11,"O Tamora, did anyone ever hear the like? This is the pit, and this is the elder tree. Look, sirs, and see if you can find the huntsman who was meant to murder Bassianus here."]],"300":[[0,10,"Who is this? My niece, running away so fast? Niece, a word. Where is your husband? If I am dreaming, I would give all my wealth to wake up! If I am awake, may some planet strike me down so that I may sleep for ever! Speak, gentle niece: what harsh, cruel hands have lopped and hacked your body and stripped it of its two branches, those sweet ornaments whose embracing shade kings have longed to sleep in, and could not win so great a happiness as half your love? Why don't you speak to me?"],[11,21,"Alas, a crimson river of warm blood, like a bubbling fountain stirred by the wind, rises and falls between your rosy lips, coming and going with your sweet breath. Surely some Tereus has raped you, and cut out your tongue so that you could not expose him. Ah, now you turn your face away in shame. And in spite of all this loss of blood, pouring as if from a fountain with three spouts, your cheeks still look as red as the sun's face blushing when it meets a cloud."],[22,32,"Shall I speak for you? Shall I say that is what happened? O, if only I knew your heart, and knew the beast, so that I could rage at him to ease my mind. Hidden sorrow, like a closed oven, burns the heart where it is to cinders. Fair Philomela only lost her tongue, and she stitched her story into a laborious piece of needlework. But, lovely niece, that way has been cut off from you. You have met a craftier Tereus, niece, and he has cut off those pretty fingers that could have sewn better than Philomela."],[33,40,"O, if the monster had seen those lily-white hands tremble like aspen leaves on a lute and make the silk strings delight to kiss them, he would not have touched them to save his life. Or if he had heard the heavenly music that sweet tongue made, he would have dropped his knife and fallen asleep, as Cerberus did at the feet of Orpheus, the Thracian poet."],[41,46,"Come, let us go and make your father blind, for such a sight will blind a father's eyes. One hour's storm will flood the sweet meadows: what will months of tears do to your father's eyes? Don't draw back, for we will mourn with you. O, if only our mourning could ease your misery!"]],"304":[[0,10,"Hear me, wise fathers! Noble tribunes, stop! Out of pity for my age, whose youth was spent in dangerous wars while you slept safely; for all the blood I have shed in Rome's great cause; for all the frosty nights I have kept watch; and for these bitter tears that you now see filling the old wrinkles in my cheeks: have mercy on my condemned sons, whose souls are not as corrupt as people think. I never wept for twenty-two sons, because they died in honour's noble bed."]],"307":[[0,10,"For these sons, tribunes, I write my heart's deep grief and my soul's sad tears in the dust. Let my tears satisfy the earth's dry hunger; my sons' sweet blood would make it blush with shame. O earth, I will water you more with rain dripping from these two old eyes than young April with all his showers. In summer's drought I'll keep dripping on you. In winter I'll melt the snow with warm tears and keep your face in eternal spring, if only you refuse to drink my dear sons' blood."]],"313":[[0,7,"Why, it doesn't matter, man. If they did hear, they would not take notice of me. If they took notice, they would not pity me. Yet I must plead, uselessly, to them. So I tell my sorrows to the stones. Though they cannot answer my distress, they are in some ways better than the tribunes, because they will not interrupt my story."],[8,15,"When I weep, they humbly take my tears at my feet and seem to weep with me. If only they were dressed in solemn robes, Rome could not provide tribunes as good as these. A stone is soft as wax; tribunes are harder than stones. A stone is silent and does no harm, and tribunes condemn men to death with their tongues. But why are you standing there with your sword drawn?"]],"322":[[0,6,"Faint-hearted boy, get up and look at her. Speak, Lavinia: what cursed hand has made you handless in your father's sight? What fool has added water to the sea, or brought a bundle of sticks to Troy as it burned? My grief was at its height before you came, and now, like the Nile, it overflows its banks."],[7,15,"Give me a sword. I'll chop off my hands too, for they have fought for Rome, all for nothing. They kept me alive to nurse this misery. They have been raised in useless prayer, and they have served me to no purpose. Now the only service I want from them is for one to help cut off the other. It is just as well, Lavinia, that you have no hands, for hands that serve Rome are useless."]],"327":[[0,9,"She was my dear, and whoever wounded her has hurt me more than if he had killed me. For now I am like a man standing on a rock, surrounded by a wilderness of sea, who watches the rising tide grow wave by wave, always waiting for some spiteful wave to swallow him in its salty belly. This way my wretched sons have gone to their deaths. Here stands my other son, a banished man, and here my brother, weeping at my sorrows."],[10,18,"But what wounds my soul most is dear Lavinia, dearer than my soul. If I had only seen a picture of you in this state, it would have driven me mad. What shall I do, now that I see your living body like this? You have no hands to wipe away your tears, no tongue to tell me who has tortured you. Your husband is dead, and for his death your brothers are condemned, and dead by now."],[19,22,"Look, Marcus! Ah, son Lucius, look at her! When I mentioned her brothers, fresh tears stood on her cheeks, like honeydew on a picked lily that is almost withered."]],"329":[[0,10,"If they did kill your husband, then be glad, because the law has taken revenge on them. No, no, they would never do such a foul deed: look at how their sister grieves. Gentle Lavinia, let me kiss your lips, or make some sign of how I can comfort you. Shall your good uncle and your brother Lucius and you and I sit round some fountain, all looking down to see how our cheeks are stained, like meadows not yet dry, with muddy slime left by a flood?"],[11,19,"And shall we stare into the fountain so long that its fresh taste is spoiled and our bitter tears turn it into a pool of brine? Or shall we cut off our hands like yours? Or bite off our tongues, and spend the rest of our hateful days in dumb show? What shall we do? Let those of us who still have tongues plan some further misery, to make people marvel at us in times to come."]],"361":[[0,7,"If there were any reason for these miseries, then I could keep my sorrows within limits. When heaven weeps, doesn't the earth flood? If the winds rage, doesn't the sea go mad, threatening the sky with its great swollen face? And do you want a reason for this turmoil? I am the sea. Listen to how her sighs blow! She is the weeping sky, and I am the earth."],[8,14,"So my sea must be stirred by her sighs. So my earth must be flooded and drowned by her endless tears. For my insides cannot hold her sorrows, but must vomit them up like a drunkard. So give me licence, for losers are allowed to ease their stomachs with bitter words."]],"370":[[0,5,"Now goodbye to comforting lies. Die, Andronicus: you are not dreaming. Look at your two sons' heads, your warrior's hand, your mutilated daughter here, your other son, banished, struck pale and bloodless by this dreadful sight, and your brother, me, cold and numb like a statue."],[6,10,"Ah, I won't try to restrain your grief any more. Tear out your silver hair, gnaw your other hand with your teeth, and let this dreadful sight be the last thing our wretched eyes see before they close. Now is the time to rage. Why are you so quiet?"]],"373":[[0,8,"Why, I haven't another tear to shed. Besides, this sorrow is an enemy that wants to take over my watering eyes and blind them with the tears they pay it. Then how shall I find the cave of Revenge? For these two heads seem to speak to me, and threaten that I shall never reach heaven until all these crimes are thrust back down the throats of those who committed them."],[9,16,"Come, let me see what work I have to do. You sorrowing people, stand in a circle round me, so that I can turn to each of you and swear on my soul to right your wrongs. The vow is made. Come, brother, take a head, and I will carry the other in this hand. And, Lavinia, you shall have a job too. Carry my hand between your teeth, sweet girl."],[17,21,"As for you, boy, go, get out of my sight. You are an exile, and you must not stay. Hurry to the Goths and raise an army there. And if you love me, as I think you do, let's kiss and part, for we have much to do."]],"375":[[0,7,"Farewell, Andronicus, my noble father, the most miserable man who ever lived in Rome. Farewell, proud Rome, until Lucius comes again: he leaves behind those he loves more than his life. Farewell, Lavinia, my noble sister. O, if only you were as you used to be! But now neither Lucius nor Lavinia lives, except in being forgotten and in hateful grief."],[8,12,"If Lucius lives, he will repay your wrongs, and make proud Saturnine and his empress beg at the gates, like Tarquin and his queen. Now I'll go to the Goths and raise an army to take revenge on Rome and Saturnine."]],"379":[[0,10,"There, there. Now sit down, and make sure you eat no more than will keep just enough strength in us to avenge these bitter sorrows of ours. Marcus, undo that sad knot of folded arms. Your niece and I, poor creatures, have no hands, and cannot show our tenfold grief by folding our arms. This poor right hand of mine is left to beat cruelly on my breast, and when my heart, mad with misery, pounds in this hollow prison of my flesh, I thump it down like this."],[11,19,"(To Lavinia) You picture of grief, who talk in signs: when your poor heart beats wildly, you cannot strike it like this to quieten it. Wound it with sighs, girl, kill it with groans. Or take some little knife between your teeth and make a hole just over your heart, so that all the tears your poor eyes shed may run into that drain and soak in, and drown the grieving fool in salt tears."]],"381":[[0,10,"What's this! Has sorrow made you senile already? Why, Marcus, no one should be mad but me. What violent hands can she lay on her life? Ah, why do you keep on about hands? It's like asking Aeneas to tell the story twice of how Troy was burnt and he was made miserable. O, don't touch the subject of hands, in case we keep remembering that we have none. Ugh, how madly I am shaping my words, as if we would forget we had no hands if Marcus did not say the word!"],[11,22,"Come, let's start eating. And, gentle girl, eat this. There's nothing to drink! Listen, Marcus, to what she says. I can interpret all her tortured signs. She says she drinks no drink but tears, brewed from her sorrow and mashed on her cheeks. Speechless complainer, I will learn your thoughts. I will be as perfect in your silent gestures as begging hermits are in their holy prayers. You shall not sigh, or hold your stumps up to heaven, or wink, nod, kneel or make a sign, but I will wring an alphabet out of it and by constant practice learn to know what you mean."]],"392":[[0,7,"O, O, O! Then forgive me for scolding you, for you have done a good deed. Give me your knife. I will gloat over him, pretending to myself that it is the Moor, come here on purpose to poison me. There's one for you, and that's for Tamora. Ah, you rascal!"],[8,10,"And yet I think we are not brought so low that between us we can't kill a fly that comes looking like a coal-black Moor."]],"405":[[0,5,"My lord, I don't know, and I can't guess, unless some fit or frenzy has taken hold of her. For I have often heard my grandfather say that extreme grief can drive people mad, and I have read that Hecuba of Troy went mad with sorrow. That made me afraid."],[6,12,"Though, my lord, I know my noble aunt loves me as dearly as my mother ever did, and would not frighten me, being young, unless she were mad. That made me throw down my books and run, without cause, perhaps. Forgive me, sweet aunt. And, madam, if my uncle Marcus will come, I will gladly go with your ladyship."]],"426":[[0,5,"O, calm yourself, gentle lord, though I know there is enough written on this ground to start a rebellion in the mildest mind and arm the minds of babies with outcries. My lord, kneel down with me. Lavinia, kneel. And kneel, sweet boy, the hope of Rome's Hector."],[6,11,"And swear with me, as Lord Junius Brutus swore with the grieving husband and the father of that chaste, dishonoured lady after the rape of Lucrece, that with careful planning we will pursue deadly revenge on these treacherous Goths, and see their blood, or die with this disgrace."]],"427":[[0,5,"That's sure enough, if you knew how. But if you hunt these bear cubs, be careful: the mother will wake if she once gets your scent. She is still in close league with the lion, and lulls him while she lies on her back, and when he sleeps she does whatever she likes."],[6,11,"You are a young huntsman, Marcus. Leave it alone. And come, I will get a sheet of brass and carve these words into it with a steel point, and keep it safe. The angry north wind will scatter these sands like the Sibyl's leaves, and where's our lesson then? Boy, what do you say?"]],"443":[[0,1,"(Aside) That you have both been found out as villains branded with rape: that's the news. (Aloud) If you please,"],[2,9,"my grandfather, after careful thought, has sent by me the finest weapons in his armoury as a present for your honourable youth, the hope of Rome. That's what he told me to say, and so I do, and I present your lordships with his gifts, so that whenever you need to, you may be well armed and equipped. And so I leave you both, (Aside) like bloody villains."]],"447":[[0,7,"Yes, exactly, a verse from Horace. Right, you've got it. (Aside) Now, what a thing it is to be an ass! This is no joke! The old man has found out their guilt, and sends them weapons wrapped in verses that cut them to the quick without their feeling it. If our clever empress were up and about, she would applaud Andronicus's idea. But let her rest in her restlessness for a while."],[8,12,"(To them) And now, young lords, wasn't it a lucky star that led us to Rome as strangers, and worse, as captives, to be raised to this height? It did me good to defy the tribune in front of the palace gate, where his brother could hear."]],"485":[[0,8,"Stop, murderous villains, would you kill your brother? Now, by the burning candles of the sky that shone so brightly when this boy was conceived, whoever touches my first-born son and heir dies on the sharp point of my scimitar. I tell you, youngsters, not Enceladus with all his threatening gang of giants, not great Hercules, not the god of war himself, shall snatch this prey out of his father's hands."],[9,17,"What, what, you red-faced, shallow-hearted boys! You whitewashed walls, you painted alehouse signs! Coal-black is better than any other colour, because it refuses to take on any other colour. For all the water in the ocean can never turn the swan's black legs white, though she washes them in the waves every hour. Tell the empress from me that I am old enough to keep what is mine. Let her explain it however she can."]],"492":[[0,5,"Why, that's the privilege your fair skin gives you. Shame on that treacherous colour, which betrays the secret deeds and thoughts of your heart by blushing! Here's a young lad of a different complexion. Look how the black rascal smiles at his father, as if to say, 'Old lad, I am your own.'"],[6,11,"He is your brother, lords, plainly fed by the same blood that first gave you life, and he has been set free from the same womb where you were imprisoned and has come into the light. Indeed, he is your brother on the surer side, his mother's, though my seal is stamped on his face."]],"504":[[0,6,"O Lord, sir, it was a deed of good sense. Should she live to betray our guilt, a long-tongued, babbling gossip? No, lords, no. And now let me tell you my whole plan. Not far from here lives a countryman of mine called Muliteus. His wife gave birth only last night. Their child looks like her, as fair as you are."],[7,14,"Go and do a deal with him. Give the mother gold, tell them both the whole story, and how their child will be raised up by this and accepted as the emperor's heir, put in the place of mine, to calm this storm whirling round the court. And let the emperor dandle him as his own. Listen, lords. You see I have given her medicine,"]],"514":[[0,8,"Come, Marcus, come. Kinsmen, this is the way. Young sir, let me see your archery. Make sure you draw the bow back far enough, and it will go straight there. 'Astraea has left the earth.' Remember, Marcus, she's gone, she's fled. Sirs, take up your tools. You, kinsmen, go and sound the depths of the ocean and cast your nets. Perhaps you may catch her in the sea, though there is as little justice there as on land."],[9,16,"No, Publius and Sempronius, you must do it. You must dig with pick and spade and bore into the very centre of the earth. Then, when you reach Pluto's kingdom, please give him this petition. Tell him it is for justice and help, and that it comes from old Andronicus, shaken with sorrows in ungrateful Rome."],[17,23,"Ah, Rome! Well, well, I made you miserable when I threw the people's votes to the man who now tyrannises over me like this. Go, off you go, and please, all of you, be careful, and don't leave a warship unsearched. This wicked emperor may have shipped her away, and then, kinsmen, we can whistle for justice."]],"520":[[0,6,"He wrongs me by fobbing me off with delays. I'll dive into the burning lake down there and pull her out of the river Acheron by the heels. Marcus, we are only shrubs, not cedars, not big-boned men built to the size of the Cyclops. But we are metal, Marcus, steel right through to the back, and yet weighed down with more wrongs than our backs can bear."],[7,10,"And since there is no justice on earth or in hell, we will petition heaven and move the gods to send Justice down to avenge our wrongs. Come, to work. You are a good archer, Marcus."]],"552":[[0,7,"Why, lords, what insults are these! Was an emperor in Rome ever so overpowered, troubled and defied, and treated with such contempt for applying lawful justice? My lords, you know, as the mighty gods know, that whatever these disturbers of our peace whisper in the people's ears, nothing has been done except by law against the wilful sons of old Andronicus."],[8,19,"And what if his sorrows have overwhelmed his wits? Must we be tormented by his acts of revenge, his fits, his frenzy and his bitterness? And now he writes to heaven for justice! Look, here's one 'To Jove', and this 'To Mercury', this 'To Apollo', this to the god of war. Sweet scrolls to fly about the streets of Rome! What is this but slandering the senate and proclaiming our injustice everywhere? A fine joke, isn't it, my lords? As if to say there is no justice in Rome."],[20,25,"But if I live, his pretended madness will not protect these outrages. He and his family shall learn that justice lives in Saturninus's health, and if she sleeps, he will wake her so that in her fury she will cut down the proudest conspirator alive."]],"553":[[0,7,"My gracious lord, my lovely Saturnine, lord of my life, ruler of my thoughts, calm yourself, and put up with the faults of Titus's age, the effects of grief for his brave sons, whose loss has pierced him deeply and scarred his heart. Comfort him in his distress, rather than prosecute the lowest or the highest of them for these insults. (Aside) Why, it suits clever Tamora to smooth everyone over like this."],[8,11,"But, Titus, I have wounded you to the quick. Once your life-blood is drained, if Aaron is wise now, then all is safe, the anchor is in the harbour."]],"565":[[0,5,"Spiteful and unbearable insults! Must I put up with this monstrous villainy? I know where this trick comes from. Is this to be borne, as if his treacherous sons, who died lawfully for murdering our brother, had been wrongly butchered on my orders?"],[6,10,"Go, drag the villain here by the hair. Neither age nor honour shall give him special treatment. For this proud mockery I'll be your executioner, you sly, raving wretch, who helped to make me great in the hope that you would rule Rome and me."]],"572":[[0,6,"King, let your thoughts be as commanding as your title. Is the sun dimmed because gnats fly in it? The eagle lets little birds sing and does not care what they mean by it, knowing that he can stop their song whenever he likes with the shadow of his wings. You can do the same with the fickle men of Rome."],[7,12,"So cheer up. For know, my emperor, I will bewitch old Andronicus with words sweeter, and yet more dangerous, than bait to fish or clover to sheep: the fish is hooked by the bait, and the sheep is made sick by its delicious food."]],"588":[[0,6,"Famous Lucius, I wandered away from our troops to look at a ruined monastery. And as I was staring hard at the wrecked building, I suddenly heard a child crying underneath a wall. I went towards the noise, and soon I heard the crying baby being hushed with these words:"],[7,16,"'Quiet, brown rascal, half me and half your mother! If your colour didn't give away whose brat you are, if nature had only given you your mother's looks, you villain, you might have been an emperor. But when the bull and cow are both milk-white, they never produce a coal-black calf. Quiet, villain, quiet!' That's how he scolded the baby. 'For I must carry you to a trusty Goth, who, when he knows you are the empress's baby, will treasure you for your mother's sake.'"],[17,19,"At that, I drew my weapon and rushed at him, took him by surprise, and brought him here for you to deal with as you think necessary."]],"599":[[0,7,"What if I don't? And indeed I don't. Yet because I know you are religious, and have a thing inside you called conscience, with twenty popish tricks and ceremonies that I have seen you careful to observe, I insist on your oath. For I know that an idiot takes his jester's stick for a god and keeps the oath he swears by that god, and I'll hold him to it."],[8,12,"So you shall swear by that same god, whatever god it is that you worship and revere, to save my boy and feed him and bring him up, or else I will reveal nothing to you."]],"607":[[0,5,"Indeed, I was their tutor and taught them. They got that lustful spirit from their mother, as sure a card as ever won the game. That bloody mind I think they learned from me, as true a dog as ever went for the bull's head. Well, let my deeds prove what I am worth."],[6,12,"I lured your brothers to that treacherous hole where the dead body of Bassianus lay. I wrote the letter your father found and hid the gold that the letter mentioned, in league with the queen and her two sons. And what has been done that you have cause to grieve over, in which I did not have a hand in the mischief?"],[13,22,"I tricked your father out of his hand, and when I had it, I went off on my own and almost broke my heart with laughing. I peeped through a crack in a wall when he got his two sons' heads in return for his hand. I watched his tears and laughed so heartily that both my eyes were as wet as his. And when I told the empress about this sport, she almost fainted at my pleasing story, and gave me twenty kisses for my news."]],"611":[[0,10,"Yes: sorry that I didn't do a thousand more. Even now I curse the day (and yet I think few days come within reach of my curse) in which I did not do some notorious evil: killed a man or plotted his death, raped a girl or planned how to do it, accused some innocent person and swore falsely, set deadly hatred between two friends, made poor men's cattle break their necks, set fire to barns and haystacks at night and told the owners to put them out with their tears."],[11,20,"I have often dug dead men up from their graves and stood them upright at their dear friends' doors, just when their grief was almost forgotten, and carved on their skins with my knife, as on the bark of trees, in Roman letters: 'Let not your sorrow die, though I am dead.' Oh, I have done a thousand dreadful things as willingly as one would kill a fly, and nothing really grieves me except that I cannot do ten thousand more."]],"632":[[0,6,"Know, sad man, that I am not Tamora. She is your enemy, and I am your friend. I am Revenge, sent from the kingdom of hell to ease the vulture gnawing at your mind by taking furious vengeance on your enemies. Come down and welcome me to the light of this world. Talk with me of murder and death."],[7,12,"There is no hollow cave or hiding place, no vast darkness or misty valley, where bloody murder or hateful rape can crouch in fear, that I will not find them out and tell them my dreadful name in their ears: Revenge, which makes the foul offender tremble."]],"635":[[0,5,"Do me some service before I come to you. Look, beside you stand Rape and Murder. Now give me some proof that you are Revenge. Stab them, or tear them apart on your chariot wheels, and then I'll come and be your driver and whirl round the world with you."],[6,15,"Get yourself two fine horses, black as jet, to pull your chariot of vengeance swiftly away and find murderers in their guilty caves. And when your chariot is loaded with their heads, I will get down and trot beside the wheel like a humble servant all day long, from the moment the sun rises in the east until it sinks into the sea. And I'll do this heavy task day after day, if you destroy Rape and Murder there."]],"641":[[0,5,"Going along with him like this suits his madness. Whatever I invent to feed his sick fancies, you back it up and keep it going in what you say. For now he firmly takes me for Revenge, and since he believes this mad idea, I'll make him send for his son Lucius."],[6,10,"And while I keep him safely occupied at a banquet, I'll quickly find some clever trick to scatter and break up the fickle Goths, or at least turn them against him. Look, here he comes, and I must keep up my part."]],"643":[[0,5,"I have been lonely a long time, and all for you. Welcome, dreadful Fury, to my house of sorrow. Rape and Murder, you are welcome too. How like the empress and her sons you are! Your costumes would be complete if only you had a Moor. Couldn't all hell provide you with such a devil?"],[6,10,"For I know very well the empress never stirs without a Moor in her company, and if you want to play our queen properly, you ought to have such a devil. But welcome as you are. What shall we do?"]],"648":[[0,5,"Look all round the wicked streets of Rome, and when you find a man who looks like you, good Murder, stab him: he's a murderer. (To Chiron) You go with him, and when you happen to find another who looks like you, good Rape, stab him: he is a rapist."],[6,11,"(To Tamora) You go with them. In the emperor's court there is a queen, attended by a Moor. You'll recognise her easily by your own shape, for she looks exactly like you in every way. Please give them some violent death. They have been violent to me and my family."]],"649":[[0,4,"You have taught us well. We shall do this. But would you be so good, Andronicus, as to send for Lucius, your thrice-brave son, who is leading an army of warlike Goths towards Rome, and invite him to come and feast at your house?"],[5,10,"When he is here, at your solemn feast, I will bring in the empress and her sons, the emperor himself, and all your enemies, and they shall bow and kneel at your mercy, and you can take out your angry heart on them. What does Andronicus say to this plan?"]],"674":[[0,11,"Come, come, Lavinia. Look, your enemies are tied up. Sirs, gag them. Don't let them speak to me, but let them hear the fearful words I say. O villains, Chiron and Demetrius! Here stands the fountain you have fouled with mud, this fine summer you have mixed with your winter. You killed her husband, and for that vile crime two of her brothers were condemned to death, my hand was cut off and made into a merry joke. Both her sweet hands, her tongue, and, dearer than hands or tongue, her spotless chastity, you inhuman traitors, you took by force."],[12,19,"What would you say if I let you speak? Villains, for shame you could not beg for mercy. Listen, wretches, to how I mean to torture you. This one hand is still left to cut your throats, while Lavinia holds between her stumps the basin that catches your guilty blood. You know your mother means to feast with me. She calls herself Revenge, and thinks I'm mad."],[20,31,"Listen, villains! I will grind your bones to dust, and make a paste of it with your blood, and with the paste I'll build a pie crust, and make two pies of your shameful heads. And I'll invite that whore, your unholy mother, to swallow her own children, as the earth does. This is the feast I have invited her to, and this is the banquet she will gorge on. For you treated my daughter worse than Philomela was treated, and I will take worse revenge than Procne. And now get your throats ready. Lavinia, come, catch the blood."]],"719":[[0,9,"You sad-faced men, people and sons of Rome, split apart by uproar like a flock of birds scattered by winds and fierce, stormy gusts: O, let me teach you how to bind this scattered corn together again into one sheaf, and these broken limbs into one body again, in case Rome becomes her own poison, and she to whom mighty kingdoms bow, like a lost and desperate outcast, shamefully destroys herself."],[10,20,"But if my white hair and wrinkles of age, solemn witnesses of real experience, cannot persuade you to listen to me, (To Lucius) speak, Rome's dear friend, as our ancestor Aeneas once did when he solemnly told love-sick Dido, who listened sadly, the story of that deadly burning night when the cunning Greeks took King Priam's Troy by surprise. Tell us what Sinon has bewitched our ears, or who has brought in the deadly wooden horse that gives our Troy, our Rome, this wound of civil war."],[21,28,"My heart is not made of flint or steel, and I cannot tell all our bitter grief without floods of tears drowning my speech and breaking off my words just when they should most move you to listen and force you to pity. Here is Rome's young captain. Let him tell the story, while I stand by and weep to hear him speak."]],"720":[[0,7,"Then, noble listeners, know that Chiron and the damned Demetrius were the ones who murdered our emperor's brother, and they were the ones who raped our sister. For their cruel crimes our brothers were beheaded, our father's tears were scorned, and he was basely tricked out of that loyal hand which fought Rome's wars to the end and sent her enemies to the grave."],[8,16,"Lastly, I myself was cruelly banished, the gates shut on me, and sent out weeping to beg for help among Rome's enemies, who drowned their hatred in my true tears and opened their arms to embrace me as a friend. Know that I, the outcast, am the one who has protected Rome's welfare with my blood, taking the enemy's sword point from her breast and sheathing the steel in my own daring body."],[17,22,"Alas, you know I am no boaster. My scars, though they are dumb, can bear witness that what I say is just and true. But wait, I think I am straying too far, listing my own worthless praises. O, forgive me: when no friends are near, men praise themselves."]],"721":[[0,8,"Now it is my turn to speak. Look at this child. Tamora gave birth to this, the offspring of a godless Moor, the chief designer and plotter of these sorrows. The villain is alive in Titus's house, and, as wretched as he is, he will confirm that this is true. Now judge what cause Titus had to avenge these unspeakable wrongs, beyond patience, more than any living man could bear."],[9,17,"Now you have heard the truth. What do you say, Romans? Have we done anything wrong? Show us where, and from this place where you see us pleading, the poor remains of the Andronici will hurl ourselves headlong, hand in hand, and dash out our souls on the jagged stones, and bring our family to an end together. Speak, Romans, speak, and if you say we must, look, Lucius and I will fall hand in hand."]],"738":[[0,9,"Let some loving friends carry the emperor away and bury him in his father's grave. My father and Lavinia shall be laid at once in our family's tomb. As for that greedy tiger, Tamora, no funeral rite, no man in mourning clothes, no mournful bell shall ring at her burial, but throw her out to the beasts and birds of prey. Her life was beastly and without pity, and now she is dead, let the birds take pity on her."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"revenge":["Revenge","Wrongs repaid with worse wrongs, from Alarbus's sacrifice to the final feast."],"justice":["Justice and power","Law, election, mercy and who has the right to judge in Rome."],"grief":["Grief and madness","Titus's sorrow, his tears and floods, and the question of whether he is mad."],"rome":["Rome and barbarism","Civilised Romans and 'barbarous' Goths, and how the two change places."],"family":["Fathers, mothers and children","Parents who kill or save their children, and children who avenge their parents."],"bodies":["Hands, tongues and bodies","Mutilation, severed hands and heads, and the body as a sign."],"race":["Aaron, colour and otherness","How the play speaks about blackness, and how Aaron answers back."],"words":["Words, books and silence","Speech, writing, Ovid, Latin tags, and Lavinia's silence."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"SATURNINUS","text":"I am his firstborn son that was the last That wore the imperial diadem of Rome;","themes":["justice","rome"],"note":"Saturninus claims power by birth alone."},{"id":"3","who":"BASSIANUS","text":"But let desert in pure election shine,","themes":["justice","rome"],"note":"Bassianus asks for rule by merit, which Rome rejects."},{"id":"5","who":"MARCUS","text":"A nobler man, a braver warrior, Lives not this day within the city walls.","themes":["rome"],"note":"Titus is introduced as Rome's ideal hero."},{"id":"17","who":"TITUS","text":"Hail, Rome, victorious in thy mourning weeds!","themes":["rome","grief"],"note":"Triumph and mourning together from Titus's first line."},{"id":"17","who":"TITUS","text":"Romans, of five-and-twenty valiant sons, Half of the number that King Priam had, Behold the poor remains, alive and dead.","themes":["family","grief"],"note":"The cost of Titus's wars, counted in sons."},{"id":"20","who":"LUCIUS","text":"That we may hew his limbs, and on a pile Ad manes fratrum sacrifice his flesh","themes":["revenge","rome","bodies"],"note":"The first act of violence is a Roman religious rite."},{"id":"22","who":"TAMORA","text":"A mother's tears in passion for her son.","themes":["family"],"note":"Tamora's grief mirrors Titus's own."},{"id":"22","who":"TAMORA","text":"Sweet mercy is nobility's true badge.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The play's great plea for mercy, and it fails."},{"id":"23","who":"TITUS","text":"To this your son is marked, and die he must,","themes":["revenge","justice"],"note":"Titus's rigid piety refuses mercy."},{"id":"26","who":"TAMORA","text":"O cruel, irreligious piety!","themes":["rome","justice"],"note":"An oxymoron that exposes Roman religion."},{"id":"27","who":"CHIRON","text":"Was never Scythia half so barbarous!","themes":["rome"],"note":"The 'barbarian' accuses the Romans of barbarism."},{"id":"28","who":"DEMETRIUS","text":"To quit the bloody wrongs upon her foes.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"The Goths' revenge is announced in the first scene."},{"id":"33","who":"TITUS","text":"Here lurks no treason, here no envy swells,","themes":["rome","grief"],"note":"Only the tomb is safe in Rome."},{"id":"41","who":"TITUS","text":"Give me a staff of honour for mine age, But not a sceptre to control the world.","themes":["justice","rome"],"note":"Titus's modest refusal of power proves fatal."},{"id":"45","who":"SATURNINUS","text":"Andronicus, would thou were shipped to hell Rather than rob me of the people's hearts!","themes":["justice"],"note":"Saturninus's jealousy of Titus begins at once."},{"id":"65","who":"MARCUS","text":"Suum cuique is our Roman justice.","themes":["justice","words"],"note":"A Latin legal tag used to defend an elopement."},{"id":"78","who":"TITUS","text":"What, villain boy, Barr'st me my way in Rome?","themes":["family","rome"],"note":"Titus kills his son in a single line."},{"id":"84","who":"TITUS","text":"Nor thou nor he are any sons of mine;","themes":["family"],"note":"Honour matters more to Titus than his children."},{"id":"91","who":"TITUS","text":"These words are razors to my wounded heart.","themes":["words","grief"],"note":"Words wound in this play as weapons do."},{"id":"101","who":"TITUS","text":"Here none but soldiers and Rome's servitors Repose in fame; none basely slain in brawls.","themes":["rome","family"],"note":"Titus denies his own son burial."},{"id":"117","who":"MARCUS","text":"Thou art a Roman; be not barbarous.","themes":["rome"],"note":"Marcus shames Titus with the ideal of Rome."},{"id":"121","who":"ALL","text":"He lives in fame that died in virtue's cause.","themes":["family","grief"],"note":"The family's epitaph for Mutius."},{"id":"134","who":"TAMORA","text":"Dissemble all your griefs and discontents.","themes":["justice","revenge"],"note":"Tamora teaches Saturninus to hide his hatred."},{"id":"134","who":"TAMORA","text":"I'll find a day to massacre them all,","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Tamora's private vow of revenge."},{"id":"134","who":"TAMORA","text":"And make them know what 'tis to let a queen Kneel in the streets and beg for grace in vain.","themes":["revenge","justice"],"note":"The sacrifice of Alarbus is the root of her revenge."},{"id":"142","who":"SATURNINUS","text":"This day shall be a love-day, Tamora.","themes":["justice"],"note":"A false reconciliation."},{"id":"148","who":"AARON","text":"Now climbeth Tamora Olympus' top,","themes":["race","justice"],"note":"Aaron celebrates Tamora's rise and plans his own."},{"id":"148","who":"AARON","text":"This siren, that will charm Rome's Saturnine, And see his shipwrack and his commonweal's.","themes":["rome","race"],"note":"Aaron foresees Rome's ruin."},{"id":"166","who":"DEMETRIUS","text":"She is a woman, therefore may be wooed; She is a woman, therefore may be won;","themes":["words","bodies"],"note":"Women are treated as prizes to be taken."},{"id":"175","who":"AARON","text":"Lucrece was not more chaste Than this Lavinia, Bassianus' love.","themes":["bodies","words"],"note":"Aaron names the Roman story that Lavinia's will repeat."},{"id":"175","who":"AARON","text":"The woods are ruthless, dreadful, deaf, and dull.","themes":["rome"],"note":"The forest as a place outside law."},{"id":"175","who":"AARON","text":"There serve your lust, shadowed from heaven's eye,","themes":["bodies"],"note":"Aaron's plan in one line."},{"id":"181","who":"TITUS","text":"The hunt is up, the morn is bright and grey,","themes":["grief"],"note":"A bright morning before the darkest day."},{"id":"190","who":"DEMETRIUS","text":"But hope to pluck a dainty doe to ground.","themes":["bodies"],"note":"Lavinia as hunted prey."},{"id":"194","who":"AARON","text":"Know that this gold must coin a stratagem,","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Aaron's gold buys a trap, not goods."},{"id":"197","who":"TAMORA","text":"The birds chant melody on every bush,","themes":["words"],"note":"Tamora's pastoral picture of the place."},{"id":"198","who":"AARON","text":"Vengeance is in my heart, death in my hand, Blood and revenge are hammering in my head.","themes":["revenge","race"],"note":"Aaron's driving passion is vengeance, not love."},{"id":"198","who":"AARON","text":"His Philomel must lose her tongue today,","themes":["words","bodies"],"note":"Aaron predicts the crime using Ovid's story."},{"id":"206","who":"BASSIANUS","text":"Believe me, queen, your swarthy Cimmerian Doth make your honour of his body's hue,","themes":["race"],"note":"Bassianus insults Tamora through Aaron's colour."},{"id":"213","who":"TAMORA","text":"A barren detested vale you see it is;","themes":["words"],"note":"The same place, described as hell to fit her lie."},{"id":"213","who":"TAMORA","text":"Revenge it, as you love your mother's life,","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Tamora sets her sons on Bassianus."},{"id":"220","who":"DEMETRIUS","text":"First thrash the corn, then after burn the straw.","themes":["bodies"],"note":"A farming image for rape and murder."},{"id":"228","who":"LAVINIA","text":"When did the tiger's young ones teach the dam?","themes":["family","rome"],"note":"Cruelty passed from mother to sons."},{"id":"231","who":"TAMORA","text":"I know not what it means; away with her!","themes":["justice","family"],"note":"Tamora claims not to know what pity is."},{"id":"233","who":"TAMORA","text":"Remember, boys, I poured forth tears in vain To save your brother from the sacrifice,","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"The memory of Alarbus hardens her heart."},{"id":"236","who":"LAVINIA","text":"O, keep me from their worse than killing lust,","themes":["bodies"],"note":"Lavinia begs for death rather than rape."},{"id":"239","who":"LAVINIA","text":"No grace, no womanhood? Ah, beastly creature,","themes":["rome","family"],"note":"Lavinia's last spoken words."},{"id":"242","who":"TAMORA","text":"Ne'er let my heart know merry cheer indeed Till all the Andronici be made away.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Tamora's aim: the whole family."},{"id":"257","who":"MARTIUS","text":"In this detested, dark, blood-drinking pit.","themes":["bodies"],"note":"The pit as a hungry mouth."},{"id":"274","who":"TAMORA","text":"And wonder greatly that man's face can fold In pleasing smiles such murderous tyranny.","themes":["words","justice"],"note":"Tamora accuses others of the falseness she practises."},{"id":"280","who":"TAMORA","text":"How easily murder is discovered!","themes":["justice","words"],"note":"Dramatic irony: the 'discovery' is a frame-up."},{"id":"285","who":"SATURNINUS","text":"Let them not speak a word; the guilt is plain;","themes":["justice"],"note":"Saturninus judges without a hearing."},{"id":"291","who":"DEMETRIUS","text":"So, now go tell, an if thy tongue can speak,","themes":["words","bodies"],"note":"The rapists mock the silence they have forced on her."},{"id":"300","who":"MARCUS","text":"Alas, a crimson river of warm blood,","themes":["bodies","grief"],"note":"Marcus's much-debated, lyrical description."},{"id":"300","who":"MARCUS","text":"Sorrow concealed, like an oven stopped, Doth burn the heart to cinders where it is.","themes":["grief","words"],"note":"Grief must be spoken or it destroys."},{"id":"300","who":"MARCUS","text":"A craftier Tereus, cousin, hast thou met,","themes":["words","bodies"],"note":"The rapists have outdone the myth."},{"id":"304","who":"TITUS","text":"For two and twenty sons I never wept, Because they died in honour's lofty bed.","themes":["grief","family"],"note":"Titus weeps now because these sons die in dishonour."},{"id":"310","who":"LUCIUS","text":"And you recount your sorrows to a stone.","themes":["grief","justice"],"note":"Rome will not hear Titus."},{"id":"313","who":"TITUS","text":"A stone is soft as wax, tribunes more hard than stones;","themes":["justice","rome"],"note":"Rome's judges are harder than stones."},{"id":"315","who":"TITUS","text":"That Rome is but a wilderness of tigers?","themes":["rome"],"note":"The civilised city has become a jungle."},{"id":"319","who":"MARCUS","text":"This was thy daughter.","themes":["family","bodies"],"note":"Four words that mark Lavinia as no longer herself."},{"id":"320","who":"TITUS","text":"Why, Marcus, so she is.","themes":["family"],"note":"Titus insists she is still his daughter."},{"id":"322","who":"TITUS","text":"What fool hath added water to the sea,","themes":["grief"],"note":"Grief added to grief."},{"id":"322","who":"TITUS","text":"'Tis well, Lavinia, that thou hast no hands, For hands to do Rome service is but vain.","themes":["bodies","rome"],"note":"Titus's bitter view of service to Rome."},{"id":"324","who":"MARCUS","text":"O, that delightful engine of her thoughts,","themes":["words","bodies"],"note":"The tongue as an instrument of thought."},{"id":"327","who":"TITUS","text":"For now I stand as one upon a rock, Environed with a wilderness of sea,","themes":["grief"],"note":"Titus pictures himself drowning in sorrow."},{"id":"334","who":"TITUS","text":"Mark, Marcus, mark! I understand her signs.","themes":["words"],"note":"Titus begins to read Lavinia's silence."},{"id":"337","who":"TITUS","text":"Did ever raven sing so like a lark","themes":["race","words"],"note":"Titus mistakes Aaron's message for good news."},{"id":"350","who":"TITUS","text":"Lend me thy hand, and I will give thee mine.","themes":["bodies","words"],"note":"A polite phrase turned grotesque."},{"id":"355","who":"AARON","text":"Let fools do good, and fair men call for grace, Aaron will have his soul black like his face.","themes":["race","revenge"],"note":"Aaron embraces the villainy others see in him."},{"id":"361","who":"TITUS","text":"If there were reason for these miseries, Then into limits could I bind my woes.","themes":["grief"],"note":"Titus's answer to calls for reason."},{"id":"361","who":"TITUS","text":"I am the sea. Hark how her sighs doth flow!","themes":["grief","family"],"note":"Titus and Lavinia's grief as sea and sky."},{"id":"363","who":"MESSENGER","text":"Thy grief their sports, thy resolution mocked;","themes":["grief","justice"],"note":"Even the messenger pities Titus."},{"id":"365","who":"MARCUS","text":"But sorrow flouted at is double death.","themes":["grief"],"note":"Mockery doubles the pain."},{"id":"369","who":"TITUS","text":"When will this fearful slumber have an end?","themes":["grief"],"note":"Titus hopes it is a nightmare."},{"id":"371","who":"TITUS","text":"Ha, ha, ha!","themes":["grief","revenge"],"note":"The laugh that turns grief into revenge."},{"id":"373","who":"TITUS","text":"Why, I have not another tear to shed.","themes":["grief"],"note":"Sorrow is exhausted."},{"id":"373","who":"TITUS","text":"Then which way shall I find Revenge's cave?","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Titus begins his revenge."},{"id":"373","who":"TITUS","text":"Bear thou my hand, sweet wench, between thy teeth.","themes":["bodies"],"note":"A grotesque image often cut or changed in performance."},{"id":"375","who":"LUCIUS","text":"If Lucius live, he will requite your wrongs,","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Lucius's vow."},{"id":"379","who":"TITUS","text":"Thou map of woe, that thus dost talk in signs,","themes":["words","bodies"],"note":"Lavinia's body as a map of sorrow."},{"id":"381","who":"TITUS","text":"Why, Marcus, no man should be mad but I.","themes":["grief"],"note":"Titus claims madness as his own."},{"id":"381","who":"TITUS","text":"But I of these will wrest an alphabet,","themes":["words"],"note":"Titus will learn Lavinia's silent language."},{"id":"384","who":"TITUS","text":"Peace, tender sapling; thou art made of tears,","themes":["family","grief"],"note":"Grief reaches the next generation."},{"id":"390","who":"TITUS","text":"\"But\"? How if that fly had a father and mother?","themes":["family","grief"],"note":"Pity for a fly from a father who has lost his children."},{"id":"391","who":"MARCUS","text":"Pardon me, sir; 'twas a black ill-favoured fly, Like to the empress' Moor;","themes":["race"],"note":"Even gentle Marcus links blackness with evil."},{"id":"393","who":"MARCUS","text":"He takes false shadows for true substances.","themes":["grief"],"note":"Marcus thinks Titus is mad."},{"id":"413","who":"TITUS","text":"This is the tragic tale of Philomel,","themes":["words","bodies"],"note":"Ovid's book tells Lavinia's story."},{"id":"415","who":"TITUS","text":"By nature made for murders and for rapes.","themes":["rome"],"note":"The forest seems designed for crime."},{"id":"423","who":"TITUS","text":"\"Stuprum. Chiron. Demetrius.\"","themes":["words","bodies"],"note":"Lavinia names the truth."},{"id":"425","who":"TITUS","text":"Tam lentus audis scelera, tam lentus vides?","themes":["justice","words"],"note":"Titus asks why the gods are so slow to act."},{"id":"427","who":"TITUS","text":"The dam will wake, and if she wind you once.","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Titus is cautious, not mad."},{"id":"428","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","text":"I say, my lord, that if I were a man, Their mother's bedchamber should not be safe","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Revenge is passed to the next generation."},{"id":"435","who":"MARCUS","text":"Revenge ye heavens for old Andronicus!","themes":["revenge","justice"],"note":"Marcus calls on heaven to act."},{"id":"443","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","text":"That you are both deciphered, that's the news,","themes":["words"],"note":"The boy delivers a coded message."},{"id":"445","who":"DEMETRIUS","text":"Integer vitae, scelerisque purus, Non eget Mauri iaculis, nec arcu.","themes":["words","race"],"note":"Horace's verse as a coded accusation."},{"id":"447","who":"AARON","text":"Now, what a thing it is to be an ass!","themes":["words"],"note":"Only Aaron understands the Latin."},{"id":"469","who":"NURSE","text":"A devil.","themes":["race"],"note":"The baby's colour is seen as damning."},{"id":"471","who":"NURSE","text":"And bids thee christen it with thy dagger's point.","themes":["race","family"],"note":"Tamora orders her own child killed."},{"id":"472","who":"AARON","text":"Zounds, ye whore, is black so base a hue?","themes":["race"],"note":"Aaron defends his son's colour."},{"id":"476","who":"AARON","text":"Villain, I have done thy mother.","themes":["race","words"],"note":"Aaron's crude pun on 'undone'."},{"id":"479","who":"AARON","text":"It shall not die.","themes":["family","race"],"note":"Aaron stands alone for his child."},{"id":"485","who":"AARON","text":"Coal-black is better than another hue In that it scorns to bear another hue;","themes":["race"],"note":"Aaron's praise of blackness."},{"id":"485","who":"AARON","text":"For all the water in the ocean Can never turn the swan's black legs to white,","themes":["race"],"note":"Blackness cannot be washed away, and Aaron is proud of it."},{"id":"487","who":"AARON","text":"My mistress is my mistress; this my self;","themes":["family","race"],"note":"Aaron chooses his son over Tamora."},{"id":"492","who":"AARON","text":"Fie, treacherous hue, that will betray with blushing The close enacts and counsels of thy heart!","themes":["race"],"note":"White skin betrays guilt; black skin does not."},{"id":"500","who":"AARON","text":"Two may keep counsel when the third's away.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"A proverb before a murder."},{"id":"502","who":"AARON","text":"\"Wheak, wheak!\" So cries a pig prepared to the spit.","themes":["race","bodies"],"note":"Aaron jokes as he kills."},{"id":"514","who":"TITUS","text":"Terras Astraea reliquit.","themes":["justice","words"],"note":"Justice has left the earth."},{"id":"519","who":"PUBLIUS","text":"If you will have Revenge from hell, you shall.","themes":["revenge","justice"],"note":"Only revenge, not justice, is available."},{"id":"520","who":"TITUS","text":"And sith there's no justice in earth nor hell, We will solicit heaven and move the gods","themes":["justice"],"note":"Titus appeals to heaven."},{"id":"522","who":"TITUS","text":"\"To Saturn,\" Caius, not to Saturnine;","themes":["words","justice"],"note":"A pun aimed at the emperor."},{"id":"552","who":"SATURNINUS","text":"Sweet scrolls to fly about the streets of Rome!","themes":["words","justice"],"note":"Titus's words hurt the emperor."},{"id":"552","who":"SATURNINUS","text":"As who would say, in Rome no justice were.","themes":["justice","rome"],"note":"Saturninus fears public opinion."},{"id":"553","who":"TAMORA","text":"But, Titus, I have touched thee to the quick;","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Tamora gloats in an aside."},{"id":"569","who":"SATURNINUS","text":"'Tis he the common people love so much;","themes":["justice","rome"],"note":"The people prefer Lucius."},{"id":"572","who":"TAMORA","text":"Is the sun dimmed, that gnats do fly in it?","themes":["justice"],"note":"Tamora's contempt for the common people."},{"id":"572","who":"TAMORA","text":"I will enchant the old Andronicus With words more sweet, and yet more dangerous,","themes":["words","revenge"],"note":"Tamora trusts in her power over words."},{"id":"588","who":"SECOND GOTH","text":"Villain, thou mightst have been an emperor.","themes":["race","family"],"note":"Aaron's words to his son, overheard."},{"id":"591","who":"LUCIUS","text":"First hang the child, that he may see it sprawl,","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Lucius threatens a child."},{"id":"595","who":"AARON","text":"Ruthful to hear, yet piteously performed.","themes":["revenge","words"],"note":"Aaron promises a tale of horrors."},{"id":"599","who":"AARON","text":"An idiot holds his bauble for a god,","themes":["words","justice"],"note":"Aaron's contempt for religion."},{"id":"603","who":"AARON","text":"Tut, Lucius, this was but a deed of charity","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Aaron's boastful confession begins."},{"id":"607","who":"AARON","text":"I pried me through the crevice of a wall When, for his hand, he had his two sons' heads;","themes":["revenge","bodies"],"note":"Aaron enjoys Titus's suffering."},{"id":"609","who":"AARON","text":"Ay, like a black dog, as the saying is.","themes":["race","words"],"note":"Aaron turns a proverb against himself."},{"id":"611","who":"AARON","text":"Ay, that I had not done a thousand more.","themes":["revenge","race"],"note":"Aaron's only regret."},{"id":"611","who":"AARON","text":"\"Let not your sorrow die, though I am dead.\"","themes":["grief","words"],"note":"Aaron carves words on dead bodies."},{"id":"611","who":"AARON","text":"As willingly as one would kill a fly,","themes":["race","grief"],"note":"An echo of Titus's fly."},{"id":"613","who":"AARON","text":"If there be devils, would I were a devil,","themes":["race"],"note":"Aaron chooses to be what others call him."},{"id":"631","who":"TITUS","text":"I am not mad; I know thee well enough.","themes":["grief"],"note":"Titus sees through Tamora's disguise."},{"id":"632","who":"TAMORA","text":"I am Revenge, sent from th' infernal kingdom","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Tamora plays the part she has lived."},{"id":"639","who":"TITUS","text":"But we worldly men Have miserable, mad, mistaking eyes.","themes":["grief","words"],"note":"Titus pretends to be fooled."},{"id":"658","who":"TITUS","text":"I knew them all, though they suppose me mad,","themes":["grief","revenge"],"note":"Titus's aside reveals his plan."},{"id":"674","who":"TITUS","text":"Here stands the spring whom you have stained with mud,","themes":["family","bodies"],"note":"Lavinia as a fouled fountain."},{"id":"674","who":"TITUS","text":"And make two pasties of your shameful heads,","themes":["revenge","bodies"],"note":"The cannibal feast planned."},{"id":"674","who":"TITUS","text":"For worse than Philomel you used my daughter, And worse than Procne I will be revenged.","themes":["revenge","words"],"note":"Titus outdoes the myth."},{"id":"676","who":"TITUS","text":"More stern and bloody than the Centaurs' feast.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"The banquet will end in slaughter."},{"id":"689","who":"SATURNINUS","text":"What, hath the firmament more suns than one?","themes":["justice"],"note":"Saturninus mocks Lucius's claim."},{"id":"702","who":"TITUS","text":"Die, die, Lavinia, and thy shame with thee;","themes":["family","bodies"],"note":"Titus kills his daughter."},{"id":"705","who":"TITUS","text":"Killed her for whom my tears have made me blind.","themes":["grief","family"],"note":"Titus's grief ends in killing."},{"id":"711","who":"TITUS","text":"Why, there they are, both baked in that pie,","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"The revelation of the feast."},{"id":"711","who":"TITUS","text":"Eating the flesh that she herself hath bred.","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Tamora eats her own children."},{"id":"717","who":"LUCIUS","text":"There's meed for meed, death for a deadly deed.","themes":["revenge","justice"],"note":"Revenge spoken as justice."},{"id":"719","who":"MARCUS","text":"O, let me teach you how to knit again This scattered corn into one mutual sheaf, These broken limbs again into one body;","themes":["rome","bodies"],"note":"Rome as a broken body to be healed."},{"id":"721","who":"MARCUS","text":"Chief architect and plotter of these woes.","themes":["race","justice"],"note":"Aaron is blamed for everything."},{"id":"727","who":"LUCIUS","text":"May I govern so To heal Rome's harms and wipe away her woe!","themes":["justice","rome"],"note":"Lucius's hope as emperor."},{"id":"733","who":"YOUNG LUCIUS","text":"My tears will choke me if I ope my mouth.","themes":["grief","family"],"note":"The boy's grief closes the family's story."},{"id":"736","who":"LUCIUS","text":"Set him breast-deep in earth and famish him;","themes":["revenge","justice"],"note":"The new emperor's first sentence."},{"id":"737","who":"AARON","text":"If one good deed in all my life I did, I do repent it from my very soul.","themes":["race","revenge"],"note":"Aaron unrepentant to the end."},{"id":"738","who":"LUCIUS","text":"Her life was beastly and devoid of pity; And being dead, let birds on her take pity.","themes":["revenge","rome"],"note":"The last lines deny Tamora burial."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Titus Andronicus, Rome's greatest general, comes home from ten years of war against the Goths with his prisoners: Tamora, Queen of the Goths, her three sons and her secret lover Aaron the Moor. To honour his own dead sons, Titus sacrifices Tamora's eldest, ignoring her plea for mercy. He refuses the empire and hands it to Saturninus, who then marries Tamora. Now empress, she vows to destroy Titus's family.","In the forest, during a royal hunt, Tamora's sons Chiron and Demetrius murder Saturninus's brother Bassianus and rape Titus's daughter Lavinia, cutting off her hands and tongue. Aaron frames two of Titus's sons for the murder; they are executed, and he tricks Titus into cutting off his own hand to save them. Titus's last son, Lucius, is banished and raises an army among the Goths.","Lavinia names her attackers by writing in the sand. Titus, apparently driven mad by grief, plots his revenge. When Tamora and her sons visit him disguised as Revenge, Rape and Murder, he kills the sons and bakes them into a pie that he serves to their mother. At the banquet he kills Lavinia and Tamora; Saturninus kills Titus, Lucius kills Saturninus and becomes emperor, and Aaron is sentenced to a slow death."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Saturninus and Bassianus compete for the throne","id":2},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Titus returns from war with his dead sons and Gothic prisoners","id":17},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Tamora begs for her son's life","id":22},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Alarbus is sacrificed","id":30},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Titus refuses the crown and names Saturninus","id":51},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Saturninus chooses Lavinia; Bassianus carries her off","id":71},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Titus kills his son Mutius","id":78},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Saturninus marries Tamora","id":92},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Tamora secretly vows to massacre the Andronici","id":134},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Aaron plans the rape of Lavinia with Chiron and Demetrius","id":175},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Aaron buries the gold and gives Tamora a forged letter","id":198},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Chiron and Demetrius kill Bassianus","id":214},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Tamora refuses Lavinia's pleas","id":237},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Quintus and Martius fall into the pit and are arrested","id":279},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Marcus finds Lavinia mutilated","id":300},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Titus pleads for his sons in the street","id":304},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Lucius is banished","id":314},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Aaron cuts off Titus's hand","id":351},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The sons' heads are returned; Titus laughs","id":371},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Lucius goes to raise an army among the Goths","id":375},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Titus and the fly","id":390},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Lavinia writes the names of her rapists","id":423},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Tamora's black baby is born; Aaron kills the nurse","id":502},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Titus shoots arrows to the gods","id":522},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Saturninus hears Lucius is marching on Rome","id":568},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Aaron is captured and confesses","id":607},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Tamora visits Titus disguised as Revenge","id":632},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Titus kills Chiron and Demetrius","id":674},{"s":"a5s3","t":"The banquet: Titus kills Lavinia and Tamora, and is killed","id":711},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Lucius becomes emperor and sentences Aaron","id":736}],"irony":{"5":"Marcus praises Titus as the noblest man in Rome; within the scene he will kill his own son.","41":"Titus refuses the crown as too heavy for his age; giving it away dooms his family.","51":"Titus hopes Saturninus's virtues will 'ripen justice'. Saturninus will condemn his sons without a hearing.","56":"Saturninus swears never to forget Titus's services. He forgets them within minutes.","93":"Tamora promises to be a loving nurse to Saturninus; she is already plotting with Aaron.","134":"Tamora pleads for Titus in public while vowing to massacre his family in an aside.","136":"Titus thanks Tamora for giving him new life; she means to take it.","143":"Titus invites the court to a hunt. The hunt is where his family is destroyed.","181":"Titus's troubled night is the only warning of what is to come.","267":"Saturninus says his brother is alive at the lodge. He is lying dead in the pit.","280":"Tamora marvels at how easily murder is discovered, when she has arranged the frame-up.","286":"Tamora promises to plead for Titus's sons. She has just helped condemn them.","337":"Titus thanks 'gentle Aaron' for the chance to save his sons.","354":"Titus thinks he has bought back his sons cheaply; he has bought their heads.","553":"Tamora thinks Titus's madness is safely contained.","572":"Tamora boasts she will enchant Titus; he will trap her.","639":"Titus pretends to mistake Tamora for Revenge. She mistakes him for a madman.","697":"Tamora thanks Titus for the feast. She is eating her sons."},"devices":{"hands":"Hands, tongues and mutilation","beasts":"Hunting and beasts","water":"Tears, sea and flood","myth":"Myths and classical stories","eating":"Eating, mouths and devouring","colour":"Black and white"},"dev":{"17":[["water","Titus as a ship returning to port with its cargo.","Lo, as the bark that hath discharged her fraught"]],"22":[["hands","Tamora asks Titus not to stain his tomb with blood.","stain not thy tomb with blood"]],"28":[["myth","Hecuba's revenge on the Thracian tyrant.","Queen of Troy"]],"92":[["myth","Tamora as Phoebe, the moon goddess.","Phœbe 'mongst her nymphs"]],"148":[["myth","Tamora as a siren, Aaron as Prometheus's chain.","Prometheus tied to Caucasus"]],"166":[["eating","Lavinia as a cut loaf.","Of a cut loaf to steal a shive"]],"175":[["beasts","Lavinia as a dainty doe.","this dainty doe"]],"198":[["myth","Lavinia as Philomel.","His Philomel must lose her tongue today"],["colour","Aaron's fleece of woolly hair as an adder.","My fleece of woolly hair"]],"204":[["beasts","Bassianus as Actaeon, hunted by his hounds.","With horns, as was Actaeon's"]],"206":[["colour","Aaron's colour stains Tamora's honour.","Spotted, detested, and abominable"]],"228":[["beasts","Tamora as a tigress teaching her cubs.","When did the tiger's young ones teach the dam?"]],"257":[["eating","The pit drinks blood.","blood-drinking pit"]],"260":[["eating","The pit as a swallowing womb.","the swallowing womb"]],"300":[["hands","Lavinia's arms as lopped branches.","Of her two branches"],["water","Her blood as a bubbling fountain.","Like to a bubbling fountain stirred with wind"],["myth","Philomela and Tereus.","Fair Philomela"]],"315":[["beasts","Rome as a wilderness of tigers.","Rome is but a wilderness of tigers"]],"322":[["water","Grief like the flooding Nile.","like Nilus it disdaineth bounds"]],"327":[["water","Titus on a rock as the tide rises.","Environed with a wilderness of sea"]],"350":[["hands","The polite phrase made literal.","Lend me thy hand"]],"355":[["colour","A soul black like his face.","Aaron will have his soul black like his face"]],"361":[["water","Titus as the sea, Lavinia as the sky.","I am the sea"]],"381":[["hands","Talk of hands reminds them of loss.","talk of hands"]],"391":[["colour","The fly as black as Aaron.","a black ill-favoured fly"]],"413":[["myth","Ovid's tale of Philomel.","tragic tale of Philomel"]],"485":[["colour","Black that scorns another hue.","Coal-black is better than another hue"],["colour","The swan's black legs.","the swan's black legs to white"]],"492":[["colour","White skin betrays by blushing.","Fie, treacherous hue"]],"502":[["eating","The nurse as a pig on the spit.","So cries a pig prepared to the spit"]],"572":[["beasts","The emperor as an eagle among small birds.","The eagle suffers little birds to sing"]],"674":[["eating","The heads baked into pies.","make two pasties of your shameful heads"],["myth","Worse than Procne.","worse than Procne"]],"711":[["eating","Tamora eats her sons.","Eating the flesh that she herself hath bred"]],"719":[["myth","Rome as Troy, Sinon and the horse.","the fatal engine"],["hands","Rome's broken limbs.","These broken limbs again into one body"]],"738":[["beasts","Tamora as a ravenous tiger.","that ravenous tiger, Tamora"]]},"stage":{"capitol":"Before the Capitol and the Andronici tomb","palace":"Saturninus's palace","forest":"The forest outside Rome","pit":"A lonely glade with a hidden pit","street":"A street in Rome","room":"A room in Titus's house","house":"Before Titus's house","camp":"The Goths' camp on the plains near Rome","banquet":"The banquet in Titus's garden pavilion"},"stagerules":[["capitol","capitol"],["lonely","pit"],["forest","forest"],["before titus","house"],["titus's house","room"],["palace","palace"],["plains","camp"],["pavilion","banquet"],["street","street"],["public","street"]],"place":{"a1s1":"Before the Capitol","a2s1":"Before the palace","a2s2":"The forest","a2s3":"A lonely part of the forest","a2s4":"The forest","a3s1":"A street in Rome","a3s2":"Titus's house","a4s1":"Before Titus's house","a4s2":"The palace","a4s3":"A public place in Rome","a4s4":"Before the palace","a5s1":"The Goths' camp","a5s2":"Before Titus's house","a5s3":"Titus's banquet"},"arc":{"Titus's suffering":[2,1,2,6,9,10,8,6,5,6,4,3,2,9],"Tamora's power":[4,6,7,9,9,9,8,6,6,5,8,6,7,1]},"arcnote":"Titus starts as a triumphant general, and his suffering is only the grief of a father who has buried sons. It climbs steeply in Act 2 as his sons are framed and Lavinia is mutilated, and peaks in 3.1 when his hand and his sons' heads come back. From then on it eases as he turns to revenge. Tamora's power rises from captive to empress in the first scene and stays high through Acts 2 to 4, dipping when her black baby threatens to expose her and when Lucius's army approaches. It collapses at the banquet, where the two lines cross for the last time.","context":[["Date","Probably written around 1591 to 1593, making it one of Shakespeare's earliest plays and his first tragedy. It was a great success on stage and was printed in 1594. The fly scene (3.2) first appeared in the First Folio of 1623."],["Authorship","Francis Meres listed the play among Shakespeare's tragedies in 1598, and it was printed in the First Folio. Even so, later editors such as Theobald and Malone doubted he wrote it, and in 1687 Edward Ravenscroft claimed Shakespeare had only touched up another man's play. Many scholars now think George Peele, a playwright known for his Latin learning, wrote the first act and a few later scenes, and Shakespeare the rest."],["Seneca","The Roman writer Seneca's tragedies, full of revenge, ghosts and cannibal feasts, were a model for Elizabethan revenge plays. His Thyestes, in which a king feeds his brother his own children, lies behind the final banquet."],["Ovid","Ovid's Metamorphoses, a schoolbook in Shakespeare's time, tells the story of Philomela, raped by Tereus, who cut out her tongue, and of Procne's revenge. Lavinia uses the book to tell her story."],["Revenge tragedy","Thomas Kyd's The Spanish Tragedy (around 1587) made revenge plays hugely popular: a wronged hero, feigned madness, a play or feast where the revenge is carried out, and a stage full of bodies."],["Rome","Elizabethans saw Rome as the model of civilisation. The play invents a late Roman Empire where Romans behave like barbarians and Goths become allies, turning that model upside down."],["Race","Aaron is one of the first major black characters on the English stage. The play uses the racist assumptions of its time (black as evil, devilish), but also gives Aaron a powerful defence of his colour and his child."],["The Goths","The Goths were Germanic peoples who sacked Rome in 410. The play is not set in any real period; it imagines Goths as both enemies and allies of Rome."],["Lucrece","Shakespeare's poem The Rape of Lucrece (1594) was written around the same time. Its story of rape, a woman's honour, and the founding of the Republic is echoed in Lavinia's."],["Reputation","Popular in its own day, the play was later despised as crude and bloody: Ravenscroft rewrote it in 1687 as a more 'refined' version. Since the 1950s, especially after Peter Brook's 1955 production with Laurence Olivier and Julie Taymor's film Titus (1999), it has been reassessed."],["Staging violence","Elizabethan theatres used animal blood and props for severed hands and heads. Modern productions choose between realism and stylisation, such as Brook's red ribbons for Lavinia's wounds."],["Virginius","Livy tells how the centurion Virginius killed his daughter to save her from rape by a corrupt official. Titus changes the story to justify killing Lavinia after the rape."]],"critics":{"revenge":[["Eugene M. Waith, 'The Metamorphosis of Violence' (1957)","Paraphrased: Waith argued the play follows Ovid, where extreme suffering transforms people, and Titus becomes something other than human through his grief."],["Jonathan Bate, introduction to the Arden edition (1995)","Paraphrased: Bate argued the play is a serious, controlled work that questions the values of revenge and Roman honour, not a crude shocker."]],"grief":[["Albert H. Tricomi, 'The Aesthetics of Mutilation' (1974)","Paraphrased: Tricomi showed how the play's metaphors (hands, heads, tongues) are made literal on stage, so language and violence are bound together."],["Harold Bloom, Shakespeare: The Invention of the Human (1998)","Paraphrased: Bloom read the play as an early parody of revenge tragedy, with Titus's grief tipping into grotesque comedy."]],"race":[["Kim F. Hall, Things of Darkness (1995)","Paraphrased: Hall examined how early modern writing linked blackness to evil and how Aaron both repeats and challenges those links."],["Emily C. Bartels, 'Making More of the Moor' (1990)","Paraphrased: Bartels argued that Aaron is more than a stock villain: he knowingly plays on the stereotypes others hold about him."]],"bodies":[["Marion Wynne-Davies, 'The Swallowing Womb' (1991)","Paraphrased: Wynne-Davies linked the pit, the womb and the feast, showing how women's bodies are seen as both consumed and consuming."],["Katherine A. Rowe, 'Dismembering and Forgetting' (1994)","Paraphrased: Rowe traced the play's obsession with hands as signs of agency, and what it means to lose them."]],"rome":[["Heather James, 'Cultural Disintegration in Titus Andronicus' (1991)","Paraphrased: James argued the play breaks apart Virgil's and Ovid's Rome, showing the city's founding myths collapsing."],["Coppélia Kahn, Roman Shakespeare (1997)","Paraphrased: Kahn showed how Roman virtue in the play depends on controlling women's bodies."]],"words":[["Gillian Murray Kendall, 'Lend me thy hand' (1989)","Paraphrased: Kendall showed how figures of speech in the play become literal, so that words themselves seem to cause violence."],["Jonathan Bate, Shakespeare and Ovid (1993)","Paraphrased: Bate showed how the play uses Ovid as both a source and an object on stage, a book that tells the truth."]]},"symbols":{"Hand":"\\bhands?\\b","Tongue":"\\btongues?\\b","Revenge":"\\brevenge\\w*|\\bvengeance\\b","Tears":"\\btears?\\b|\\bweep\\w*","Rome":"\\brome\\b|\\broman\\w*","Black":"\\bblack\\w*|\\bmoor\\b"},"solo":{"96":1,"148":1,"194":1,"375":1,"435":1,"510":1},"iago":[["a1s1","Titus sacrifices Alarbus to the spirits of his dead sons, ignoring Tamora's plea."],["a1s1","Tamora vows to 'massacre them all' for her son."],["a2s1","Aaron plots the rape of Lavinia with Chiron and Demetrius."],["a2s3","Tamora sets her sons on Bassianus, and they kill him."],["a2s3","Lavinia is handed to the brothers: Tamora remembers Alarbus."],["a2s3","Quintus and Martius are framed for Bassianus's murder."],["a3s1","Aaron tricks Titus into cutting off his hand."],["a3s1","The sons' heads come back; Titus laughs and vows revenge."],["a3s1","Lucius goes to raise an army against Rome."],["a4s1","Lavinia names Chiron and Demetrius; the family swears revenge."],["a4s2","Titus sends the brothers a warning in Latin."],["a4s3","Titus's arrows torment the emperor."],["a5s1","Aaron is captured and confesses."],["a5s2","Titus kills Chiron and Demetrius and plans the feast."],["a5s3","Tamora eats her sons; Titus kills Lavinia and Tamora."],["a5s3","Saturninus kills Titus; Lucius kills Saturninus."],["a5s3","Lucius sentences Aaron and denies Tamora burial."]],"rel":{"1":[["TITUS","TAMORA","hate"],["TAMORA","TITUS","deceive"],["TITUS","SATURNINUS","trust"],["SATURNINUS","TAMORA","love"],["BASSIANUS","LAVINIA","love"],["TITUS","MUTIUS","hate"],["MARCUS","TITUS","love"],["TAMORA","AARON","love"]],"2":[["AARON","CHIRON","deceive"],["AARON","DEMETRIUS","deceive"],["TAMORA","LAVINIA","hate"],["CHIRON","LAVINIA","hate"],["DEMETRIUS","BASSIANUS","hate"],["AARON","SATURNINUS","deceive"],["MARCUS","LAVINIA","love"],["TAMORA","AARON","love"]],"3":[["TITUS","LAVINIA","love"],["AARON","TITUS","deceive"],["TITUS","LUCIUS","love"],["MARCUS","TITUS","love"],["TITUS","SATURNINUS","hate"],["LUCIUS","SATURNINUS","hate"]],"4":[["LAVINIA","TITUS","trust"],["TITUS","CHIRON","hate"],["AARON","TAMORA","deceive"],["TAMORA","SATURNINUS","deceive"],["MARCUS","TITUS","love"],["YOUNG LUCIUS","TITUS","love"]],"5":[["AARON","LUCIUS","deceive"],["LUCIUS","AARON","hate"],["TAMORA","TITUS","deceive"],["TITUS","TAMORA","deceive"],["TITUS","LAVINIA","love"],["LUCIUS","SATURNINUS","hate"],["MARCUS","LUCIUS","trust"],["YOUNG LUCIUS","TITUS","love"]]},"why":["Sets up the contest between birth and merit that Titus decides wrongly.","The road not taken: Rome passes over its most virtuous candidate.","Builds Titus up as the perfect Roman before his fall.","Joy and grief mixed from the first moment, the play's keynote.","Shows how much Titus has already given to Rome.","Roman 'religion' begins the bloodshed, not the barbarians.","Makes Tamora a grieving mother, like Titus, before she is a villain.","The ideal Titus fails to live up to; mercy is never shown again.","Titus's certainty that the rite is right is the root of his tragedy.","Names the paradox at the heart of Roman piety.","Turns the labels round: who are the barbarians?","The first promise of revenge, made by the victims of the first killing.","The dead are safer than the living in Rome.","His modesty hands power to the worst possible ruler.","Shows Saturninus's insecurity and his fear of Titus's popularity.","Law is quoted to justify seizing a woman: justice as a slogan.","Titus kills his son for the emperor he has just made.","Honour comes before love of his children.","Words cut like blades, an early sign of how language will wound.","Titus refuses his own son burial, as Tamora will be refused at the end.","Rome's ideal set against its practice.","The family's code: fame redeems death.","Tamora teaches the art of hiding hatred, which drives the plot.","The vow behind everything that follows.","Her motive is clear: Alarbus.","A reconciliation that is really a trap.","Aaron's ambition rises with Tamora.","Aaron sees Rome's destruction coming and welcomes it.","Women reduced to targets, the attitude behind the rape.","Aaron frames Lavinia's fate through the story of Lucrece.","The woods are a world without witnesses or law.","The plot in its plainest terms.","Bright opening that the scene's horror will darken.","The hunting image turns Lavinia into game.","Aaron's plot uses money as bait.","Within one scene the same glade becomes a hell in her next speech: a lesson in how this play's speakers bend description to purpose.","Ties Aaron to Saturn, the planet of melancholy and violence, and shows revenge driving him even in a love scene.","Foreshadowing: the audience knows the crime and its model before it happens, so Act 2 plays as dreadful suspense.","Bassianus's taunt gives Tamora the excuse for murder: his contempt helps cause his own death.","Useful for essays on setting: the forest's meaning changes with the speaker's need.","Tamora turns a family bond into a weapon, which Titus later answers with his own family.","Shows how lightly the brothers treat murder: Bassianus is 'straw' to be burned after use.","Lavinia's argument about inherited cruelty prepares for the play's question of what Young Lucius will inherit.","The exact reversal of her own plea in Act 1: the victim of no mercy now shows none.","Structural echo: Act 2 repays Act 1, so the play's violence moves in a chain.","Lavinia's wish to be killed rather than raped is the idea Titus later uses to justify killing her.","After this line Lavinia never speaks again, so her final words are an accusation.","Tamora's revenge is total, not proportionate: the play's revenges grow at every step.","The pit is the first of the play's devouring mouths, which end in Tamora's own mouth at the feast.","Tamora's line describes herself exactly, an irony the audience can enjoy.","A neat example for essays on dramatic irony and false appearances.","Saturninus's justice is the opposite of the 'pure election' Bassianus hoped for.","The brothers think silence will protect them; the plot turns on Lavinia finding another way to speak.","A test case for critics: is beauty of language a way to bear horror, or a way to look away from it?","Marcus states a principle that explains Titus's outbursts: unspoken grief destroys.","The classical model is outdone, a pattern Titus will repeat in his revenge.","Shows Titus's code: death in battle is honourable, death by false law is not.","Lucius's blunt line punctures his father's rhetoric and shows how alone Titus is.","A paradox that sums up Titus's view of Rome's institutions.","The play's most quoted line: it reverses the opposition of city and wild on which the play is built.","Marcus's past tense ('was') raises the question of what Lavinia now is, which Titus answers at once.","Titus's refusal to let go of her identity is moving here, and complicates his decision to kill her.","Hyperbole that shows grief has already reached its limit, and yet it keeps growing.","Titus's bitterness against Rome begins here: forty years of service have bought nothing.","Mechanical imagery ('engine') for the body, typical of the play's way of seeing people as instruments.","An extended image of isolation that prepares for 'I am the sea'.","Titus's role shifts from speaker to interpreter, a skill he will use to read the Horace trick and Tamora's disguise.","Irony: Titus misreads the one sign that matters, and believes Aaron.","Black comedy at the darkest moment: the play mixes horror and grim wit.","Aaron's rhyming couplet makes his villainy a performance for the audience.","Titus answers Marcus's call for reason: grief this great cannot be measured.","The peak of Titus's flood imagery, merging his body with the world.","The only sympathetic voice from the court side, and it belongs to a nameless messenger.","Explains why the returned heads hurt more than the deaths themselves.","A quiet line before the laugh, showing Titus can no longer tell waking from nightmare.","The play's turning point: from here on Titus acts instead of weeping.","Explains the laugh: sorrow is exhausted and something else takes its place.","Revenge personified for the first time, preparing for Tamora's disguise as Revenge in Act 5.","One of the play's most notorious stage images, often cut or changed in performance.","Passes the duty of revenge to the next Andronicus, who will finally carry it out.","Lavinia's body is now something to be read, like a map or a book.","Titus's claim to madness can be read as real or as the start of a disguise.","A promise kept in Act 4, when Lavinia reveals the truth through signs and writing.","Young Lucius is shaped by grief and revenge from childhood: what kind of ruler will he be?","The fly scene in miniature: absurd and heartbreaking at once.","Marcus's lie about the fly shows how easily the play links black with evil.","Marcus's verdict, which the audience can test against Titus's later cunning.","The play admits its own source and makes it part of the plot.","Titus sees a pattern: the world seems built for crime, which justifies extreme measures.","The silenced victim names her attackers: the crisis of Act 4.","A Latin line from Seneca, the model for revenge tragedy, asking why justice is delayed.","Useful against the view that Titus is simply mad: he plans strategically.","The boy's eagerness for violence is admired by Marcus, which may trouble a modern audience.","Marcus prays for heaven to act; Titus will act himself.","The boy's asides make him a small actor in the revenge plot.","Learning as a weapon: only those who understand Latin can read the threat.","Aaron's contempt for the brothers' stupidity shows his superiority.","Two words that show how the baby is judged before anyone looks at it.","Tamora sacrifices her own child to protect herself, the reverse of her Act 1 plea.","Aaron's first response is to defend the colour that others despise.","Aaron's wordplay is crude and confident: he is never lost for words.","Four words that set Aaron against everyone else on stage.","The play's strongest defence of blackness, spoken by its villain.","An image of permanence: black is constant, white can be stained.","Aaron's love for his son outweighs his love for Tamora.","Aaron turns the language of colour back on white skin.","A proverb used to justify murder, typical of Aaron's wit.","Aaron's comic sound effect shows how lightly he takes life, except his son's.","A Latin tag from Ovid that frames Titus's search for justice.","Publius's joke points to the play's real choice: revenge, since justice is absent.","Titus moves from earthly justice to divine, and then to private revenge.","A pun that makes the arrows a public insult.","Titus's words, not his weapons, rattle the emperor.","Saturninus fears the people's opinion, which Lucius will exploit.","Tamora's aside reveals her triumph, which is short-lived.","Explains why Lucius can take Rome so easily.","Tamora's eagle image shows her contempt for the Roman people.","Tamora's faith in her own persuasion leads her into Titus's trap.","The overheard words humanise Aaron and complicate his villainy.","Lucius's cruelty to a baby links him to the cycle of revenge.","A preview of Aaron's confession, which ties the plot together.","Aaron's contempt for faith exposes the oath he forces on Lucius.","Aaron reframes his affair as minor compared with what follows.","Aaron's pleasure in Titus's pain makes him the play's most hateful figure.","Aaron's self-mocking wit shows him in control even as a prisoner.","Aaron's regret is that he has not done more evil, the opposite of repentance.","Aaron's carved message shows how he uses words to torment.","The fly links Aaron and Titus: one weeps over a fly, the other kills men like flies.","Taking up the label 'devil' is Aaron's last act of defiance against the people who use it.","Key evidence for essays on Titus's madness: he claims sanity and proves it by naming her.","She plays Revenge as a role, but the audience has watched her be revenge since Act 1.","Read as irony: the 'mistaking eyes' are Tamora's, not Titus's.","The aside lets the audience in on the trap, so the rest of the scene is suspense, not surprise.","Natural images of purity (spring, summer) set against the brothers' 'mud' and 'winter'.","The language of the kitchen makes the horror almost comic, a mix typical of the play.","Shows Titus using Ovid as a script, just as Lavinia did, and pushing past it.","The Centaurs' feast was a wedding that became a massacre: a precise warning of the banquet to come.","Saturninus still competes for status even as the trap closes.","Use for essays on honour and gender: Lavinia's 'shame' is treated as a reason to kill her.","A rhyming line that turns murder into a statement of grief.","The flat, rhyming announcement makes the horror land like a punchline.","Completes the eating imagery that began with the pit and the sacrificial fire.","The couplet's neat balance hides the fact that it describes one more killing.","A body image for the state that answers the play's mutilations.","Putting the blame on the outsider lets Rome avoid its own guilt.","Hope at the end, which his first decisions will test.","The play's last private grief is a child's, who may carry the story on.","The new order begins with a cruel punishment, so the cycle may not be over.","Aaron denies the audience the comfort of repentance.","A frame: the play began with arguments over burial and ends with one."],"insults":[{"id":"26","f":"O cruel, irreligious piety!","who":"Tamora","n":"Tamora accuses the Romans of being godless in the name of religion."},{"id":"27","f":"Was never Scythia half so barbarous!","who":"Chiron","n":"The 'barbarian' throws the word back at Rome."},{"id":"78","f":"What, villain boy,","who":"Titus","n":"Titus calls his own son a villain before killing him."},{"id":"84","f":"Traitor, restore Lavinia to the Emperor.","who":"Titus","n":"Titus calls Lucius a traitor for defending his sister."},{"id":"90","f":"go, give that changing piece","who":"Saturninus","n":"Saturninus treats Lavinia as a fickle piece of goods."},{"id":"99","f":"Unworthy brother and unworthy sons!","who":"Titus","n":"Titus rejects his family for disobeying him."},{"id":"153","f":"Why, boy, although our mother, unadvised, Gave you a dancing-rapier by your side,","who":"Demetrius","n":"Brotherly mockery of Chiron's manhood."},{"id":"159","f":"Foul-spoken coward, that thund'rest with thy tongue,","who":"Chiron","n":"The brothers fight with words before swords."},{"id":"204","f":"Unmannerly intruder as thou art.","who":"Tamora","n":"Tamora's fury at being caught."},{"id":"206","f":"your swarthy Cimmerian","who":"Bassianus","n":"Bassianus insults Aaron's colour to shame Tamora."},{"id":"207","f":"let her joy her raven-coloured love;","who":"Lavinia","n":"Lavinia joins in the racial taunt, which the play does not let her forget."},{"id":"218","f":"nay, barbarous Tamora,","who":"Lavinia","n":"No name fits Tamora but her own."},{"id":"239","f":"Ah, beastly creature,","who":"Lavinia","n":"Lavinia's last words are an insult."},{"id":"279","f":"Two of thy whelps, fell curs of bloody kind,","who":"Saturninus","n":"The Andronici are called dogs, as Romans call the Goths beasts."},{"id":"355","f":"Aaron will have his soul black like his face.","who":"Aaron","n":"Aaron turns the insult into a boast."},{"id":"391","f":"'twas a black ill-favoured fly,","who":"Marcus","n":"Even gentle Marcus insults Aaron through a fly."},{"id":"472","f":"Zounds, ye whore, is black so base a hue?","who":"Aaron","n":"Aaron answers racist contempt with fury."},{"id":"477","f":"hellish dog,","who":"Demetrius","n":"Demetrius curses Aaron and his child."},{"id":"485","f":"Ye white-limed walls, ye alehouse-painted signs!","who":"Aaron","n":"Aaron insults white skin as whitewash and cheap paint."},{"id":"589","f":"Say, wall-eyed slave,","who":"Lucius","n":"Lucius heaps insults on Aaron."},{"id":"606","f":"O barbarous beastly villains, like thyself!","who":"Lucius","n":"Beasts and barbarians again."},{"id":"684","f":"Away, inhuman dog, unhallowed slave!","who":"Lucius","n":"Aaron is denied humanity."},{"id":"738","f":"As for that ravenous tiger, Tamora,","who":"Lucius","n":"The last insult of the play, spoken over a corpse."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"21","f":"I give him you, the noblest that survives,","n":"Titus uses the formal 'you' to his sons on public business."},{"id":"22","f":"And if thy sons were ever dear to thee,","n":"Tamora pleads with Titus as 'thou', an intimate appeal from a mother to a father."},{"id":"23","f":"Patient yourself, madam, and pardon me.","n":"Titus answers with the cold, formal 'you'."},{"id":"84","f":"Nor thou nor he are any sons of mine;","n":"'Thou' to his son, now in anger and contempt."},{"id":"200","f":"Ah, my sweet Moor, sweeter to me than life!","n":"Tamora's private warmth to Aaron."},{"id":"201","f":"No more, great empress. Bassianus comes.","n":"Aaron switches to her title the moment others appear."},{"id":"631","f":"I am not mad; I know thee well enough.","n":"Titus uses 'thee' to Tamora: contempt for an enemy he sees through."},{"id":"729","f":"O, take this warm kiss on thy pale cold lips.","n":"Lucius's loving 'thy' to his dead father."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Titus becomes emperor","Titus gives the crown to Saturninus"],"a2s1":["Aaron sets the brothers against each other","Aaron unites the brothers in a plan to rape Lavinia"],"a2s2":["The hunt passes in peace","The hunt sets the trap"],"a2s3":["Bassianus escapes and warns Titus","Bassianus is killed and Titus's sons are framed"],"a2s4":["Lavinia names her attackers","Lavinia is silenced and found by Marcus"],"a3s1":["The hand saves the sons","The hand is returned with the sons' heads"],"a3s2":["Titus plans revenge openly","Titus seems to lose his mind over a fly"],"a4s1":["Lavinia stays silent","Lavinia writes the names of her rapists"],"a4s2":["Aaron kills the baby","Aaron saves the baby and kills the nurse"],"a4s3":["The gods answer Titus","Titus's arrows land in the emperor's court"],"a4s4":["Saturninus marches on Lucius","Tamora plans to trick Titus"],"a5s1":["Lucius hangs Aaron and the baby","Aaron confesses to save his son"],"a5s2":["Tamora tricks Titus","Titus traps and kills her sons"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Titus and Tamora","pts":["Both are parents who lose children and want revenge.","Titus refuses Tamora's plea for mercy; Tamora refuses Lavinia's.","Each kills or harms their own child: Titus kills Mutius and Lavinia, Tamora orders her baby killed."],"ids":[23,233]},{"t":"Titus and Aaron","pts":["Both are skilled at reading and using words, Latin and stories.","Titus kills his own children; Aaron protects his son against everyone.","Titus becomes a revenger; Aaron enjoys evil for its own sake."],"ids":[702,485]},{"t":"Lavinia and Tamora","pts":["The two women at the centre of the plot, one Roman, one Goth.","Lavinia is praised for chastity; Tamora is attacked for lust.","Lavinia loses her voice; Tamora's voice controls the emperor."],"ids":[236,572]},{"t":"Saturninus and Bassianus","pts":["Brothers who compete for the throne.","Saturninus claims by birth; Bassianus by merit.","Saturninus lives to rule badly; Bassianus is murdered early."],"ids":[2,3]},{"t":"Marcus and Titus","pts":["Brothers, both devoted to Rome.","Marcus reasons and pleads; Titus refuses and rages.","Marcus survives to speak for the family at the end."],"ids":[117,361]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Most Lamentable Roman Tragedy of Titus Andronicus"],["Written","About 1591 to 1593; first printed in 1594; the fly scene (3.2) first appeared in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Tragedy (in the First Folio's Tragedies); a revenge tragedy modelled on Seneca and Ovid"],["Setting","Rome and the forest outside it, in an imagined late Roman Empire"],["Protagonist","Titus Andronicus"],["Antagonists","Tamora, Aaron, Saturninus, Chiron and Demetrius"],["Inciting incident","Titus sacrifices Tamora's son Alarbus (1.1)"],["Turning point","Titus's sons' heads are returned with his hand, and he laughs and vows revenge (3.1)"],["Climax","The banquet: Titus kills Lavinia, reveals the pie and kills Tamora (5.3)"],["Time span","A few days in Act 1 to Act 3; then an unspecified time while Lucius raises an army"],["Major conflicts","Roman against Goth; Titus against Tamora; mercy against revenge; order against barbarism"],["Themes","Revenge, justice and power, grief and madness, Rome and barbarism, parents and children, the body, race, words and silence"]],"analysis":{"TITUS":["At the start Titus is the perfect Roman: a soldier of forty years, 'surnamed Pius', who has given twenty-one sons to the state. His virtues are rigid ones. He sacrifices Alarbus because religion demands it, refuses the throne, gives it to the man with the best claim by birth, and kills Mutius for blocking his way.","Each of these choices returns to destroy him. His sons are framed, Lavinia is mutilated, Lucius banished and his hand tricked away. His language swells to match his suffering, from tears in the dust to 'I am the sea'.","His laugh in 3.1 marks the turn from grief to revenge. From then on he may be mad, or may be playing mad: he talks to flies and shoots arrows at the gods, but he also reads the Horace trick perfectly and tells Tamora 'I am not mad'.","His revenge is as cruel as the wrongs done to him. He kills Chiron and Demetrius, bakes them in a pie, kills Lavinia and Tamora. He dies as both victim and monster."],"TAMORA":["Tamora enters as a captive mother begging for her son's life, and her plea for mercy is one of the play's most moving speeches. Titus's refusal gives her motive.","As empress she controls Saturninus completely, teaching him to 'dissemble' and pretending to be a peacemaker while planning to 'massacre them all'.","She is ruthless towards Lavinia, refusing even a quick death, and towards her own black baby, which she orders killed to hide her affair.","Her disguise as Revenge shows her confidence in her own power to deceive, and is her undoing: the 'madman' she plans to use uses her."],"AARON":["Aaron is the play's master plotter and its most vivid speaker. He arranges the rape, the frame-up and the trick with Titus's hand, and boasts of each.","He is presented through the racist language of the time: other characters call him a devil, a raven, a 'black dog'. He embraces the role ('Aaron will have his soul black like his face') and turns the insults back on white skin.","His love for his son is the surprise of the play. He defends the baby against Tamora, her sons and Lucius, and trades his confession for its life.","He dies unrepentant, regretting only that he cannot do 'ten thousand more'. Critics argue over whether he is a stage devil, a racist caricature, or a man responding to the hatred around him."],"LAVINIA":["Lavinia begins as a prize: chosen by Saturninus, claimed by Bassianus, with little say of her own. She speaks briefly but with spirit, mocking Tamora and then pleading with her.","After the rape and mutilation she is silent for the rest of the play, but always on stage, a constant reminder of what has been done.","She finds a way to communicate through Ovid's book and by writing in the sand, and takes part in the revenge by holding the basin.","Her death at her father's hands, to end her 'shame', is the play's most troubling killing, and modern productions often stage it as Lavinia's own wish."],"MARCUS":["Marcus is the voice of reason in the family. He pleads for Mutius's burial, tries to calm Titus and thinks of practical ways forward.","His long speech on finding Lavinia, full of beautiful images, is one of the most argued-over passages in Shakespeare.","He is the one who works out how Lavinia can write, and who leads the family's vow of revenge.","At the end he speaks to Rome, presents the evidence and helps make Lucius emperor: he survives to rebuild."],"LUCIUS":["Lucius demands the sacrifice of Alarbus, which starts the cycle of revenge.","He is loyal to his family, defending Lavinia and trying to rescue his brothers, and is banished for it.","He leads the Goths against Rome, a reversal of everything his father fought for.","As emperor he promises to heal Rome, but his first acts are a slow execution and denial of burial: the cycle may not be broken."],"SATURNINUS":["Saturninus claims the throne by birth and is jealous of anyone more popular.","He switches from loving Lavinia to marrying Tamora in a moment, and turns on Titus at once.","He is easily led, fearful of the people and cruel to the powerless, like the Clown.","He dies at the banquet, killed by Lucius moments after he kills Titus."]},"motifs":[["Hands","Hands are cut off, offered, begged for and carried in teeth. Titus's 'lend me thy hand' turns a phrase into horror.",[63,68,78,80]],["Tongues and silence","Lavinia's tongue is cut out; Titus wants to read her signs; Marcus calls the tongue the 'engine of her thoughts'.",[52,64,66,82]],["Hunting and beasts","Lavinia is a doe, Rome is a 'wilderness of tigers', Tamora a tiger and Aaron a dog.",[33,42,59,149]],["Tears and flood","Titus's grief grows from tears in the dust to the sea itself.",[62,65,71,76]],["Eating and devouring","The pit as a mouth, the sacrificial fire, and the final feast where Tamora eats her sons.",[48,134,140,141]],["Books and myths","Ovid, Horace and Seneca guide the plot, and Lavinia uses a book to speak.",[29,37,87,95]]],"symbols2":[["The pit","A 'blood-drinking' hole in the forest that swallows Bassianus, Quintus and Martius: a grave, a trap and a mouth."],["Ovid's Metamorphoses","The book that tells Lavinia's story for her, and that gives Titus the model for his revenge."],["The tomb","The Andronici family tomb, where sons are buried with honour or refused burial: the play begins and ends with burial."],["The arrows","Titus's petitions to the gods, which land in the emperor's court and turn grief into a public attack."],["The baby","Aaron and Tamora's son, the living evidence of their affair and the one thing Aaron loves."],["The pie","The cannibal feast: revenge made literal, as Tamora eats what she produced."]],"bigq":[["Is Titus mad?",["He talks to flies and shoots arrows at the gods","He tells Tamora 'I am not mad' and sees through her disguise","His aside 'I knew them all' suggests he plays mad","Grief may have made him both mad and cunning"],[81,86,129,132]],["Who is to blame for the tragedy?",["Titus, for sacrificing Alarbus and choosing Saturninus","Tamora, for her revenge","Aaron, who plots every crime","Rome itself, which values duty over mercy"],[8,23,125,59]],["Is Aaron a racist caricature?",["Others describe him in racist terms throughout","He embraces and turns those terms back","His love for his son gives him depth","He is the play's most intelligent character"],[69,102,104,128]],["Why does Titus kill Lavinia?",["To end her shame, following Virginius","To end his own sorrow","Because she wants it, in some productions","It shows his idea of honour, not her worth"],[138,139,60,61]],["Is the ending hopeful?",["Lucius promises to heal Rome","The cycle continues with Aaron's execution and Tamora's denial of burial","Young Lucius survives, but trained in revenge","Aaron's baby is spared, but what will happen to it?"],[143,145,147,149]],["Who are the barbarians?",["The Romans sacrifice a prisoner","The Goths rape and mutilate","Rome becomes a 'wilderness of tigers'","Lucius leads Goths to save Rome"],[9,10,59,20]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present revenge in the play?",[11,23,77,135,142,147]],["How does Shakespeare present Titus?",[3,13,16,75,129,138]],["How does Shakespeare present Aaron?",[26,36,69,102,104,125]],["How does Shakespeare present Lavinia?",[45,53,60,80,89,138]],["How does Shakespeare present Rome?",[0,9,20,59,108,143]],["How does Shakespeare present grief?",[54,62,65,71,76,146]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (imagery, soliloquy, aside, classical allusion, irony, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Titus's madness and the ending","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[75,"Shakespeare presents Titus's laugh as the moment grief turns into revenge.","The laugh comes when Marcus expects him to storm: it is a shocking reversal of expectation.","'I have not another tear to shed' explains it: sorrow has been used up.","Seneca's revenge heroes also pass from grief to a terrible calm before acting.","Some productions play the laugh as madness, others as a sign of a cold new purpose."],[102,"Shakespeare gives Aaron a powerful defence of his colour and his child.","'Coal-black is better than another hue' reverses the play's language of black as evil.","The image of the swan's legs shows that black cannot be washed white, and Aaron is proud of it.","Elizabethan audiences saw black skin as a sign of sin; Aaron's speech challenges that.","Some critics see him as more human here; others say his violence undercuts his love."],[59,"Shakespeare presents Rome as a place more savage than the forest.","'Wilderness of tigers' turns the civilised city into a jungle.","Titus says Lucius is lucky to be banished from it.","Rome was the model of civilisation for Elizabethans, so the reversal is shocking.","The Goths, called barbarians, end up rescuing Rome."],[60,"Shakespeare presents Lavinia's mutilation through the reactions of her family.","'This was thy daughter' suggests she has lost her identity.","Titus's reply 'so she is' shows his love insisting she is still his.","Ovid's Philomela gives the story a classical frame.","Feminist critics point out that she is defined by men's reactions."],[135,"Shakespeare presents Titus's revenge as an outdoing of Ovid.","'Worse than Procne' shows he knows the story and means to exceed it.","The pie makes the revenge fit the crime: a mother eats her children.","Elizabethan audiences knew Ovid; the allusion makes the horror familiar.","Some critics see the revenge as justice; others as madness."],[143,"Shakespeare ends the play with an image of healing.","'Knit again This scattered corn' suggests Rome can be whole again.","'Broken limbs' recalls the mutilations of the play.","Elizabethans feared civil war; the ending speaks to that fear.","But the cruelty of Aaron's sentence makes the healing doubtful."]],"lang":{"un":[["17","Thou great defender of this Capitol, Stand gracious to the rites that we intend.","Great protector of this Capitol, look kindly on the rites we intend."],["304","For two and twenty sons I never wept, Because they died in honour's lofty bed.","I never wept for twenty-two sons, because they died honourably."],["519","Marry, for Justice, she is so employed,","As for Justice, she is so busy."],["737","I do repent it from my very soul.","I regret it with all my soul."]],"pun":[["350","Lend me thy hand, and I will give thee mine.","hand","pun","Help me, and also literally give your hand."],["476","Villain, I have done thy mother.","done","pun","'Undone' means ruined; Aaron uses 'done' for sex."],["522","\"To Saturn,\" Caius, not to Saturnine;","Saturnine","pun","Saturn the god, and the emperor's name."],["605","Trim sport for them which had the doing of it.","Trim","pun","Cut, and also fine fun."],["674","And of the paste a coffin I will rear,","coffin","pun","A pie crust, and also a burial box."],["717","There's meed for meed, death for a deadly deed.","meed","pun","Reward, and payment: revenge as payment."]],"st":[["315","That Rome is but a wilderness of tigers?",[["wilderness","The city as wild land."],["tigers","Romans as beasts of prey."]]],["361","I am the sea. Hark how her sighs doth flow!",[["sea","Titus's grief is boundless."],["sighs","Lavinia's grief as wind on the sea."]]],["485","Coal-black is better than another hue In that it scorns to bear another hue;",[["Coal-black","Aaron reclaims the colour."],["scorns","Pride, not shame."]]]]},"dir":[["371","Ha, ha, ha!","How should Titus laugh?",[["Wildly, like a madman","Grief has broken his mind."],["Quietly and coldly","He has found a new purpose: revenge."],["Through tears","He cannot cry any more, so he laughs."]]],["319","This was thy daughter.","How should Marcus present Lavinia?",[["Gently, leading her by the arm","He protects her from the shock."],["Hesitantly, unable to look","He is overwhelmed."],["Bluntly","He cannot soften what has happened."]]],["485","Coal-black is better than another hue","How should Aaron say this?",[["Proudly, to the audience","He challenges their prejudice."],["Tenderly, to the baby","His love for his son drives the speech."],["Angrily, at Chiron and Demetrius","He is defending his child against them."]]],["390","\"But\"? How if that fly had a father and mother?","How should Titus react to the fly?",[["Weeping","He sees himself in the fly's parents."],["Raging","His grief is uncontrolled."],["Calmly, as if reasonable","His madness is quiet and strange."]]],["702","Die, die, Lavinia, and thy shame with thee;","How should the killing of Lavinia be staged?",[["She kneels and offers her neck","She wants to die."],["She is taken by surprise","Titus acts against her will."],["Titus hesitates, then strikes","He is torn."]]],["737","If one good deed in all my life I did, I do repent it from my very soul.","How should Aaron deliver his last speech?",[["Defiantly, laughing","He is unrepentant to the end."],["Exhausted but proud","He is dying but will not give in."],["To the audience","He accuses them as well."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["5","22"],["485","607"],["674","711","676"],["198","300","413"],["639","658","371"],["26","117","315"],["471","479","737"],["17","28","727"],["175","413","423"],["711","371"],["373","674","363"],["705","702"]],"cr":{"revenge":[["371","413","658"],["26","117","717"]],"grief":[["363","373","319"],["371","658","674"]],"race":[["471","479","737"],["476","479","737"]],"bodies":[["236","257","674"],["319","373","379"]],"rome":[["315","117","727"],["236","702","705"]],"words":[["91","300","379"],["413","423"]]}}};
